window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("adoption", {"topics":[{"key":"adoption/change-management","sources":["Kotter, 1996 — Leading Change","Hiatt, 2006 — ADKAR: A Model for Change in Business, Government and Our Community","Rogers, 1962 — Diffusion of Innovations"],"why":"- **Shipping is not adoption.** A typical internal agent launch shows a burst of curious usage in the first\n  week, then a slow decay back to the old process, while the dashboard still shows the system \"live\".\n  Nothing in the code is wrong; the people around it never changed what they do.\n- **Probabilistic output forces users to learn *when* to trust it.** Under-trusters redo the work by hand\n  and now spend more time than before, because they read the agent's draft *and* do the task. Over-trusters\n  let errors through (automation bias), and the first public mistake poisons the agent for everyone.\n- **Agents arrive with an implied question about jobs.** An agent that completes tasks end to end raises it\n  more sharply than an autocomplete. If nobody answers it, people answer it themselves, and quiet resistance\n  is a rational response.\n- **The value needs the work redesigned around the agent.** If staff are still measured on the old metric,\n  still follow the old checklist and still route through the old approval step, the agent's step is pure\n  overhead.\n- **The naive alternatives fail predictably.** \"Announce it, record a training video, it's intuitive\"\n  creates awareness but not desire and leaves incentives untouched. \"Mandate it\" produces compliance\n  theatre: people open the tool so the login count looks good, then ignore the output. Change management\n  instead names the behavior change, finds what blocks it per group, and removes those blocks one by one.","how":"### Background: frameworks as diagnostic checklists\n| Model | Level | Stages |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ADKAR** (Prosci) | The individual | *Awareness* of why, *Desire* to take part, *Knowledge* of how, *Ability* to do it in real work, *Reinforcement* that makes it stick |\n| **Kotter's eight steps** | The organization | Create urgency, build a guiding coalition, form a vision, communicate it, remove obstacles, generate short-term wins, consolidate gains, anchor the new way in culture |\n| **Unfreeze-change-refreeze** | Compact framing | Loosen the old habit, form the new one, then deliberately lock it in |\n\n- **ADKAR is diagnostic.** Find the first stage that is blocked for a group and work there, *because*\n  training (Knowledge) cannot fix a Desire problem, and enthusiasm (Desire) cannot fix a missing\n  integration (Ability), such as an agent that cannot reach the system of record.\n- **Lewin attribution caveat.** Unfreeze-change-refreeze is traditionally attributed to Kurt Lewin;\n  historians note the tidy three-step version was largely popularized by later writers.\n- **None of these is an algorithm.** Use them to ask \"what is missing for this group?\", not as a sequence\n  of ceremonies.\n\n### 1. Define the change as behavior\n- **Unmanageable:** \"Adopt the AI agent\" cannot be managed or measured.\n- **Manageable:** \"Claims adjusters start every file over 30 pages from the agent's summary and verify its\n  cited sections, instead of reading the file front to back.\"\n- **Write one such sentence per affected role**, *because* every later step (training, metrics,\n  incentives, the events your agent logs) hangs off it.\n\n### 2. Assess impact per group\n- **List what changes for each group:** tasks, skills, metrics, tools, approvals, and any effect on\n  headcount or roles.\n- **Be explicit about job impact**, *because* silence is filled with rumor.\n- **Groups differ.** End users, their team leads, QA reviewers, IT support and compliance each experience\n  a different change; for an agent with write tools, whoever approves its actions is a group too.\n\nTreat resistance as data about which stage is blocked, not as an attitude problem. Each needs a different\nfix, which is why the diagnosis has to be per group:\n\n| What you hear | What is blocked |\n|---|---|\n| \"It's slower\" | Ability or incentive problem |\n| \"It got the prior-injury note wrong\" | Trust problem |\n| \"Why would I train my replacement?\" | Desire, rooted in job security |\n| A senior expert's \"I don't need it\" | Often loss of status as the person who knows |\n\nThe incentive question can usually be made arithmetic. A user adopts the agent's path when, on the metric\nthey are judged by, it beats the old one *including* the cost of verifying the output:\n\n- **The comparison:** `t_ai = t_review_output + t_verify + t_fix_errors` versus `t_manual`.\n- **The rational outcome:** if verification is slow or the task is short, `t_ai > t_manual` and\n  non-adoption is the rational choice, not a training gap.\n- **The builder's lever:** most of `t_verify` is UI and trace design you own, such as citations that open\n  the exact source passage.\n\n### 3. Build sponsorship and a change network\n- **Executive sponsor creates legitimacy** by visibly using the agent and explaining the why.\n- **Champions create proof.** These are usually the early adopters in Rogers' diffusion model, inside each\n  team, *because* people copy respected peers more readily than memos.\n- **Team leads matter most.** If a lead says \"don't trust that thing\", no amount of central communication\n  wins.\n\n### 4. Change the system around the agent\n- **Update SOPs, QA checklists and KPIs** so the new way is the measured way.\n- **Decide when the old path is retired** and what the fallback is when the agent is down, times out or is\n  wrong, so a failed tool call hands the case back to a person cleanly.\n- **Why:** people follow incentives, so an unchanged scorecard quietly pulls them back to the old process.\n\n### 5. Roll out in phases, and plan for the dip\n1. **Pilot cohort first**, fix what they hit, then expand.\n2. **Expect performance to *fall* before it rises** (often called the J-curve): people are learning a new\n   habit, running old and new paths side by side, and hitting first-version bugs.\n3. **Tell the sponsor in advance** and temporarily protect the pilot cohort's productivity targets.\n4. **Judge the trend after the learning period**, not week two, *because* a dip that nobody predicted\n   reads as failure and gets the project cancelled at its lowest point.\n5. **Close the feedback loop.** Publish a \"you said, we did\" list, *because* visible responsiveness is the\n   cheapest way to build desire.\n6. **Calibrate trust honestly.** Show the measured error rate from your evals and teach how to verify\n   (citations, confidence cues), which calibrates trust instead of inflating it.\n\n### 6. Reinforce and measure\n| Indicator type | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Leading | Weekly active users in the target group, share of eligible tasks started with the agent, override and edit rates |\n| Lagging | Cycle time, quality, cost per case |\n\n- **Usage without outcome improvement** usually means people use it but the workflow around it has not\n  changed (or the agent is not adding value).\n- **Outcome improvement without usage** means something other than the agent is driving the outcome, so\n  crediting the agent would be wrong.","example":"### Break-even calculator: when is the agent's path rational?\nPlug in timings from user interviews (these are the illustrative figures from the scenario below) to see\nwhere the agent's path starts beating the manual one on the user's own metric.\n\n```python\ndef t_manual(pages, per_page=0.6):\n    return per_page * pages\n\ndef t_ai(pages, fixed=10, per_page=0.25):\n    # fixed: read the agent's summary, open and check each cited section\n    # per_page: the spot reading that remains\n    return fixed + per_page * pages\n\nbreak_even = 10 / (0.6 - 0.25)          # 0.6p = 10 + 0.25p\nprint(f\"break-even ~ {break_even:.0f} pages\")   # ~29 pages\n\nfor p in (12, 30, 80):\n    ai, man = t_ai(p), t_manual(p)\n    print(f\"{p:3} pages: agent {ai:4.0f} min, manual {man:4.0f} min ->\",\n          \"use agent\" if ai < man else \"manual wins\")\n# 12 pages: agent 13 vs manual 7  -> manual wins\n# 80 pages: agent 30 vs manual 48 -> use agent\n# Cutting `fixed` (e.g. inline citations) lowers the break-even further.\n```\n\n### Adoption metrics straight from the agent's event log\nIf every agent run logs who it served, whether the task was eligible and what the user did with the\noutput, the adoption dashboard is a group-by. The rows here are illustrative.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# one row per agent run: team, eligible task?, user action on the output\nruns = [\n    (\"team_a\", True, \"accepted\"), (\"team_a\", True, \"edited\"), (\"team_a\", True, \"accepted\"),\n    (\"team_b\", True, \"rejected\"), (\"team_b\", True, \"rejected\"), (\"team_b\", True, \"edited\"),\n]\neligible_tasks = {\"team_a\": 4, \"team_b\": 9}   # from the case system, not the agent\n\nstats = defaultdict(lambda: {\"runs\": 0, \"override\": 0})\nfor team, eligible, action in runs:\n    if eligible:\n        stats[team][\"runs\"] += 1\n        stats[team][\"override\"] += action == \"rejected\"\n\nfor team, s in stats.items():\n    share = s[\"runs\"] / eligible_tasks[team]\n    print(f\"{team}: {share:.0%} of eligible tasks via agent, \"\n          f\"override {s['override'] / s['runs']:.0%}\")\n# team_a: 75% of eligible tasks via agent, override 0%\n# team_b: 33% of eligible tasks via agent, override 67%  -> talk to team_b's lead first\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: an insurer's claims-summary agent\nA hypothetical insurer rolls out a claims agent to 120 adjusters. It retrieves the claim file, calls a\npolicy-lookup tool and writes a cited summary.\n\nUsage starts at about 70% of adjusters in week one and decays to about 25% by week six. The offline eval looks fine, so the team interviews ten adjusters and reads the\nagent's logs instead of retraining.\n\n**Diagnosis by ADKAR stage:**\n\n| Stage | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Knowledge | Fine: adjusters know how to use the agent |\n| Desire | Low: scored on claims closed per day, and on short files reading the summary plus checking it takes longer than reading the file |\n| Trust (via leads) | Two team leads tell their teams not to rely on it after a story spread about a summary that missed a prior-injury note |\n| Ability | Weak: the summary opens in a separate tab, so verification means switching windows |\n\n**The arithmetic** (illustrative figures from the interviews):\n- **Manual:** about 0.6 minutes per page.\n- **Agent path:** about 10 minutes fixed overhead plus about 0.25 minutes per page of remaining spot reading.\n- **Break-even:** 0.6p = 10 + 0.25p, so p ≈ 29 pages.\n- **Short file:** on a 12-page file the agent's path costs 13 minutes against 7 by hand.\n- **Long file:** on an 80-page file it costs 30 against 48.\n- **Verdict:** adjusters who abandoned the agent were, on average, handling shorter files: they were right.\n\n**Interventions follow the diagnosis:**\n1. **Eligibility:** the router sends files to the agent only when they are over 30 pages, where it saves\n   time.\n2. **Trust:** the missed-note bug is reproduced from the trace, fixed, added to the eval set and\n   announced; the team publishes its golden-set result (say, a material omission in 1 of 25 long files)\n   alongside the instruction to check cited sections for medical history.\n3. **Leads:** the two skeptical leads join the pilot review and become champions.\n4. **System:** the QA checklist accepts \"summary verified\" as a valid review step.\n5. **Ability:** citations move inline, which cuts the fixed verification overhead and lowers the\n   break-even further.\n\n**Outcome:** usage on eligible files recovers and stays. Cycle time on long files becomes the metric\nreported to the sponsor, rather than the share of all adjusters using the agent, which would have\npressured people to use it on short files where it costs time.","patterns":["**Eligibility gating in the router** — when the agent only beats the manual path on some tasks, route by a rule (for example a feature flag on document length over the break-even page count) instead of sending everything. Monitor share of *eligible* tasks done the new way and time per task on each path.","**Segmented adoption dashboard from agent events** — from launch, log each run's user action (accept, edit, reject, override) with team and task type attached, and chart per team rather than as one company-wide line. Monitor team-by-team variance with no technical cause, which points at leads or local incentives.","**Pre-agreed J-curve review date** — before rollout, agree in writing with the sponsor the learning period, the protected targets for the pilot cohort, and the date of the first go/kill review. Monitor the trend over that window, not single weeks.","**\"You said, we did\" changelog tied to the eval set** — publish a short, regular list mapping user-reported agent failures to the fix shipped and the eval case added, in the channel users already read. Monitor feedback volume and override rate after each fix lands.","**Scorecard update as a launch gate** — do not widen the agent beyond the pilot until SOPs, QA checklists and KPIs name the new path. Monitor reversion to the manual path during busy periods as the early warning.","**Clean human fallback** — when the agent errors, times out or hits a step budget, hand the case back to the user's queue with what it found so far. Monitor fallback rate per week; a spike erodes trust faster than any announcement rebuilds it."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent that drafts, looks things up and proposes actions only creates\n  value if people start their work from its output and stop doing the task the old way. A perfect tool loop\n  nobody routes work through is overhead.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Eligibility rules in the router (which tasks the agent may take),\n  approval gates and override buttons, citations and trace views in the UI, and the event log that records\n  accept, edit and reject per user and team.\n- **What you control.** Instrument the target behavior from day one, gate the agent to tasks where it beats\n  the manual path, make verification cheap (inline citations, one-click source open), and ship visible fixes\n  for what users report.\n- **What you can skip.** The organizational-psychology literature. Treat ADKAR as a debugging checklist for\n  low usage, and keep the break-even arithmetic; that covers most agent rollouts.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Rolling out a support or claims agent to a frontline team.** The behavior statement, eligibility rule\n  and override telemetry decide whether usage survives week six.\n- **Widening an agent's autonomy.** Moving from \"draft for approval\" to \"act, then notify\" is a change for\n  the approvers, with its own J-curve and trust event risk.\n- **Recovering a stalled agent pilot.** ADKAR per team, plus the break-even arithmetic, usually finds a\n  cheaper fix than a model upgrade.\n- **Replacing a manual approval step with an agent check.** QA checklists and KPIs must name the agent's\n  step, or reviewers keep doing both.","mini_project":"**Adoption telemetry and a break-even gate for a small agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make an agent's adoption measurable and route it only to tasks where it beats the manual path.\n- **Steps:** build or reuse a summarizer agent with one retrieval tool; log one event per run with user,\n  team, task size, eligibility and the user's action (accept, edit, reject); simulate two teams with\n  different override behavior; compute share of eligible tasks and override rate per team; add a router\n  rule that sends only tasks above a break-even size to the agent.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-team table showing eligible-task share and override rate, the router rule\n  is driven by a computed break-even, and you can name which ADKAR stage each team's numbers point to."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Agent usage decaying for a group\"] --> A{\"Aware of why?\"}\n    A -->|\"no\"| A1[\"Sponsor explains the why\"]\n    A -->|\"yes\"| D{\"Want to use it?\"}\n    D -->|\"no\"| D1[\"Fix incentives, KPIs, job clarity\"]\n    D -->|\"yes\"| K{\"Know how?\"}\n    K -->|\"no\"| K1[\"Training and verification drills\"]\n    K -->|\"yes\"| B{\"Able in real work?\"}\n    B -->|\"no\"| B1[\"Fix integration and verify cost\"]\n    B -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Reinforce: metrics, SOPs, champions\"]","caption":"Walk ADKAR in order for one group and stop at the first stage that fails; the fix sits next to it, not\nfurther down the list."},"interview":[{"q":"What is ADKAR, and why is it more useful as a diagnostic than as a plan?","a":"- **Definition:** five individual-level stages: Awareness, Desire, Knowledge, Ability, Reinforcement.\n- **Why diagnostic:** you work on the first blocked stage for a given group, because each stage needs a\n  different fix; training (Knowledge) cannot repair Desire, and enthusiasm cannot repair a missing\n  integration (Ability).\n- **So** running every stage as a ceremony for every group wastes effort on stages that are already fine."},{"q":"One team uses your agent heavily and an identical team barely touches it. How do you debug that?","a":"- **Rule out technical causes first:** same agent version and prompt, same tool permissions and data, same\n  integrations, similar task mix; compare tool-call error and fallback rates per team in the traces.\n- **Then look at people:** what the team lead says about the agent, how that team's KPIs are set, and\n  whether a trust event happened there.\n- **Fix locally:** bring the lead into the pilot review, protect targets during learning, and recheck\n  team-level usage and outcome a few weeks later."},{"q":"When would you not mandate use of an agent?","a":"- **When the new path loses on the user's own metric.** If `t_ai > t_manual` for a task, a mandate forces\n  slower work and breeds compliance theatre.\n- **When the error rate is not yet established**, because removing the manual fallback leaves no safe\n  path when the agent is wrong.\n- **Mandate later**, once incentives, SOPs and workflow already favor the new path and the old one is\n  being retired deliberately."},{"q":"Which events would you make your agent emit so adoption is measurable, and which numbers go on the dashboard?","a":"- **Events:** per run, the user, team, task type, whether the task was eligible, and the user's action on\n  the output (accept, edit, reject, override), plus fallbacks to the manual path.\n- **Leading:** share of eligible tasks started with the agent in the target group, override and edit\n  rates, weekly active users in that group. **Lagging:** cycle time, quality and cost per case, against the\n  pre-launch baseline.\n- **Leave off or demote:** company-wide logins and training completion, because they measure exposure,\n  not behavior, and pressure people to use the agent where it costs time."},{"q":"How do you keep a sponsor from cancelling during the J-curve dip?","a":"- **Predict it in writing before launch:** expected dip, learning period length, and the review date.\n- **Protect the pilot cohort's targets** for that period so the dip does not punish early adopters.\n- **Report the trend** and the fixes shipped (\"you said, we did\"), so the sponsor judges direction rather\n  than a single bad week."},{"q":"Users say your agent is \"slower than doing it myself\". What do you change in the agent, not the training?","a":"- **Measure the claim:** time from agent output to user action, split by task size, against the manual\n  baseline; it is often true on short tasks.\n- **Cut verification cost:** inline citations that open the exact passage, fewer claims per output, and a\n  summary of what the agent checked via tools.\n- **Gate eligibility:** route only tasks above the break-even size to the agent, and keep the rest manual."}],"tradeoffs":["**Phased pilot vs speed.** A pilot surfaces workflow problems cheaply but delays org-wide value; skip it only when the change is small and reversible.","**Retiring the old path vs keeping a fallback.** Retiring forces adoption and ends dual running, but removes the fallback; keep it while the agent's error rate is still being established.","**Heavy executive sponsorship vs headcount fear.** It speeds legitimacy, but a sponsor seen as imposing cuts can turn the change into a headcount fight; it flips back to an asset once the sponsor has stated plainly what happens to roles.","**Mandating usage vs real adoption.** It gets the numbers up quickly, but produces compliance theatre unless incentives and workflow change first.","**Champion network vs people's time.** It costs time, which pays off at scale and is overkill for a ten-person team.","**More agent autonomy vs adoption.** Letting the agent act without approval saves the most time but raises the cost of each visible mistake; widen autonomy only after override rates are low and stable."],"pitfalls":["**Treating training as the whole change plan.** Symptom: high course completion with usage decaying after launch week.","**Measuring logins instead of the target behavior.** Dashboards look healthy while cycle time and quality do not move.","**Leaving old KPIs in place.** Shows up as users reverting to the manual path whenever they are busy.","**Ignoring team leads and middle managers.** Usage varies sharply team by team with no technical explanation.","**Overselling accuracy at launch.** The first visible error triggers a trust collapse and a spike in overrides.","**Targeting \"everyone uses it for everything\".** Symptom: usage on tasks where the agent is slower, flat or worse outcomes, and resentment.","**Reviewing results during the learning dip.** Shows up as a sponsor wanting to cancel in week three when the trend was about to turn.","**Staying silent on job impact.** Revealed by rumors, union or works-council questions, and slow passive resistance.","**Not logging the user's action on agent output.** Symptom: you know runs per day but not whether anyone used the result. Fix: emit accept, edit and reject events with team and task type from the first release."],"when":"- **Use when** an agent changes how a group of people does its daily work, which is almost every\n  enterprise deployment beyond a developer tool. Effort scales with the number of people affected, how\n  much their metrics and roles change, and how costly mistakes are.\n- **Start during scoping, not after launch.** The behavior statement and impact assessment belong next to\n  the success metrics and the agent's event schema.\n- **Measure** the target behavior and business outcome, not logins: share of *eligible* tasks done the new\n  way, override and edit rates, time per task on the new path versus the old, and usage segmented by team\n  (team-by-team variance with no technical cause points at leads or local incentives).\n- **Fix first** if you cannot write the behavior sentence or show the new path beats the old on the user's\n  own metric; do that before planning communications.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is whether you treat low adoption as a diagnosis problem (which ADKAR stage is\n  blocked, for whom) rather than as a call for more training or a better model.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments the product so the target behavior is measurable, and builds the trust affordances (citations, confidence cues, easy override) users need.","architect":"Owns the design of the human workflow around the system: where review sits, what the fallback is, and which metrics define success.","fde":"Sits with users, diagnoses which stage of change is blocked for which team, and brings concrete workflow and incentive fixes to the sponsor."},"check":{"q":"Six weeks after launch, usage of your AI drafting tool has halved, but offline evals are unchanged. The sponsor wants to fund a model upgrade. What do you do first?","a":"I would not assume the model is the cause, because evals are flat and decay after a launch spike is the classic adoption pattern. I would segment usage by team and task type, interview a handful of users and team leads, and map what I find onto ADKAR to find the first blocked stage. Typical causes are incentives (old KPIs), workflow friction (verification is slow), a trust event (one bad output went viral) or a lead discouraging use. Each has a cheaper fix than a model upgrade. I would come back to the sponsor with the diagnosis, the targeted fixes and the metric that will show whether they worked."},"quiz":[{"q":"In ADKAR terms, what is the main risk of responding to low adoption with more training sessions?","options":["Training sessions are too expensive to run for most teams","Training addresses Knowledge, which may not be the blocked stage if users lack Desire or Ability","Training reduces Awareness by overwhelming users with detail","Training should only happen after the Reinforcement stage"],"answer":1,"explanation":"ADKAR is diagnostic: work on the first stage that is blocked. If users understand the tool but are scored on a metric it hurts, or cannot verify outputs easily, more training changes nothing."},{"q":"Which indicator best shows that the intended behavior change has happened?","options":["Number of logins per week across the company","Course completion rate for the launch training","Share of eligible tasks that are started with the AI output in the target group","Number of positive comments in the launch announcement channel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logins and completions measure exposure, not behavior. The share of eligible tasks actually done the new way maps directly to the behavior statement, and can be paired with outcome metrics."},{"q":"Why do AI tools need explicit trust calibration as part of change management, more than ordinary software?","options":["Because AI tools are always less accurate than the manual process they replace","Because regulators require users to sign off on every AI output","Because probabilistic outputs make users either redo the work or let errors through unless they learn when to rely on it","Because AI tools cannot show any evidence for their outputs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deterministic software is right or broken; AI output is right most of the time. Under-trust doubles the work and over-trust lets errors pass, so users need honest error rates and a way to verify."}]},{"key":"adoption/user-training","sources":["Kirkpatrick, 1994 — Evaluating Training Programs: The Four Levels"],"why":"- **Agents break the old training contract.** Traditional training teaches where the buttons are, because\n  the software behaves the same way every time and correctness is the vendor's job. With an agent, two\n  users clicking the same button can get different results, and the same input can produce a strong\n  answer one day and a weaker one after a model update.\n- **Correctness is now partly the user's job**, because the user is often the last check before an output\n  or action reaches a customer or a record. Probabilistic tools punish both blind trust and blanket\n  distrust; users who never learn to check either let errors through or quietly stop using the agent.\n- **So users need four new things:** recognize task types where the agent is reliable and where it is\n  not; verify fast (open the cited source, check the number against the record); know what not to paste\n  in (customer PII into an unapproved tool, for example); and flag a bad output so it reaches the team\n  that can fix it.\n- **The naive alternatives fail.** A recorded feature demo creates awareness and nothing else, and much\n  of it is forgotten quickly if not practised soon. \"It's a chat box, it's intuitive\" leaves each user to\n  discover failure modes alone, usually by being burned in front of a customer.\n- **One big launch session is not enough.** It covers too much, before users have the context to absorb\n  it, and is never repeated when the agent changes.","how":"### 1. Derive the curriculum from the behavior change\n- **Start from the target behavior** in the change plan (\"support staff start each reply from the agent's\n  draft and check every policy claim against the linked article\").\n- **Work backwards** to what a user must know and be able to do.\n- **Everything else is optional**, *because* attention is the scarce resource in training.\n\n### 2. Teach the mental model and the failure modes\n- **Explain the agent in the user's terms:** \"it searches the help center, reads the five most relevant\n  articles and drafts a reply from them; if the answer is not in those articles, it may guess.\"\n- **That one sentence predicts the main failure** (confident answers on topics not in the knowledge base)\n  and tells users what to check.\n- **Name the tools it can call** and which ones change something (send, refund, update a record), because\n  those are the actions users must approve with care.\n- **Show real failures from your evaluation set**, *because* people calibrate trust from examples far\n  better than from a stated accuracy number.\n\n### 3. Practise on real tasks, with seeded errors\n- **Use the users' own kinds of cases** in hands-on exercises.\n- **Run a verification drill:** each person gets a set of agent outputs, some with realistic seeded errors,\n  and accepts, edits or rejects each. It builds the skill that matters most and produces a measurable\n  score.\n- **Use role-specific tracks.** The reviewer, the frontline user and the team lead need different drills.\n\nScore the drill on *two* numbers, not one, *because* there are two ways to be miscalibrated:\n\n| Metric | Formula | Bad value means |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Catch rate** | seeded errors caught ÷ seeded errors | Low: over-trust |\n| **False-reject rate** | correct outputs rejected or rewritten ÷ correct outputs | High: under-trust; the user does the task twice and will eventually decide the tool is useless |\n\n- **Catch rate alone rewards the wrong behavior.** A user who rejects everything scores 100% on catch rate.\n- **The goal:** high catch with low false-reject, plus a verification time per item that fits the real\n  workflow.\n\nSeed realistic errors, not obvious ones:\n- **Realistic teaches; garbled does not.** An invented refund window that looks like a real policy\n  teaches something; a garbled sentence does not, because the model rarely fails that way. Draw error\n  types from the eval set's actual failure modes, including wrong tool arguments (the wrong account, the\n  wrong date).\n- **Mind the base rate.** A drill might be 25% wrong to give enough practice, but production may be wrong\n  a few times in a hundred, and people checking rarely-wrong output stop looking (vigilance falls when\n  errors are rare).\n- **So teach verification as a fixed, cheap habit** tied to claim types (\"always check numbers, dates and\n  policy terms against the citation\") rather than a judgment about whether a draft \"feels off\".\n\n### 4. Cover data handling and escalation explicitly\n- **State the data rules:** what may and may not go into the agent, what its tools can read, and why.\n- **State the bad-output path:** the feedback button, what to write, and when to escalate to a human\n  expert.\n- **Give it its own segment.** *Because* these rules carry compliance risk, they deserve a dedicated,\n  short, testable segment rather than a slide buried in the middle.\n\n### 5. Deliver in the workflow, then reinforce\n1. **Short sessions close to go-live.**\n2. **Job aids inside the tool:** tooltips and a one-page \"when to trust, when to check\" card.\n3. **Office hours and champions** in each team.\n4. **Spaced reinforcement beats one long session**, *because* skills not used soon after training fade\n   quickly (the spacing effect is one of the more robust findings in learning research).\n5. **Train-the-trainer** lets the customer's own staff carry the program after handoff.\n\nTraining cannot fix a slow check: if verifying a claim takes three clicks and a second window, people\nskip it under time pressure. The cheapest training improvement is often an inline citation the engineer\nadds.\n\n### 6. Measure with the four levels, and retrain on change\n| Kirkpatrick level | Question | Agent evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Reaction | Did they find it useful? | Survey |\n| 2. Learning | Can they do the drill? | Catch and false-reject rates |\n| 3. Behavior | Do usage logs show the target behavior? | Citation-open and edit rates |\n| 4. Results | Did the business metric move? | QA error rate, handle time |\n\n- **Organizations measure levels 1 and 2 far more often** than 3 and 4, yet 3 and 4 are the ones that\n  show whether the program worked.\n- **Retrain on change.** When the model, prompt, tool set or knowledge base changes enough to shift\n  behavior, update the failure-mode examples and tell users what is different, *because* their calibrated\n  trust is now calibrated to an old agent.","example":"### Scoring a verification drill on both numbers\nA drill result is a list of (was the output wrong?, did the user reject or rewrite it?). Score both\ncalibration directions, not just catches.\n\n```python\ndef score_drill(items):\n    \"\"\"items: list of (seeded_error: bool, user_rejected_or_rewrote: bool).\"\"\"\n    errs = [r for e, r in items if e]\n    good = [r for e, r in items if not e]\n    catch = sum(errs) / len(errs)               # over-trust if low\n    false_reject = sum(good) / len(good)        # under-trust if high\n    return round(catch, 2), round(false_reject, 2)\n\n# 20 drafts, 5 with seeded errors (as in the scenario below)\nbefore = [(True, c) for c in [1, 1, 0, 0, 0]] + [(False, r) for r in [1] * 4 + [0] * 11]\nafter  = [(True, c) for c in [1, 1, 1, 1, 0]] + [(False, r) for r in [1] * 1 + [0] * 14]\nreject_all = [(True, True)] * 5 + [(False, True)] * 15\n\nprint(score_drill(before))      # (0.4, 0.27)  catches 2 of 5, rewrites 4 of 15\nprint(score_drill(after))       # (0.8, 0.07)  catches 4 of 5, rewrites 1 of 15\nprint(score_drill(reject_all))  # (1.0, 1.0)   perfect catch, useless behavior\n```\n\n### Building a drill set from the agent's eval results\nThe drill should reuse the agent's real failures, not invented ones. This sketch samples failed and passed\neval cases at a chosen error share and records the failure type, so users practise on what the agent\nactually gets wrong.\n\n```python\nimport random\n\n# one record per eval case: agent output, whether the grader passed it, and the failure type\n# (stand-in data: 120 cases, every sixth one failed; load your real eval results instead)\nFAILS = [\"invented_policy\", \"wrong_plan\", \"missing_caveat\"]\neval_results = [\n    {\"id\": i, \"output\": f\"draft {i}\", \"passed\": i % 6 != 0,\n     \"failure\": None if i % 6 else FAILS[(i // 6) % 3]}\n    for i in range(1, 121)\n]\n\ndef build_drill(results, n=20, error_share=0.25, seed=7):\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    bad = [r for r in results if not r[\"passed\"]]\n    good = [r for r in results if r[\"passed\"]]\n    k = round(n * error_share)                       # 5 seeded errors in 20\n    items = rng.sample(bad, k) + rng.sample(good, n - k)\n    rng.shuffle(items)\n    return items\n\ndrill = build_drill(eval_results)\nprint(sum(not r[\"passed\"] for r in drill), \"errors of\", len(drill))   # 5 errors of 20\nprint(sorted({r[\"failure\"] for r in drill if r[\"failure\"]}))\n# ['invented_policy', 'missing_caveat', 'wrong_plan']\n# Version the drill next to the eval set; rebuild it when the agent's failure mix shifts.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a 60-person support team gets a reply-drafting agent\nA hypothetical support team of 60 staff is getting an agent that searches the help center and drafts\ngrounded replies. The eval shows drafts are usually accurate on documented topics but sometimes invent\npolicy details on undocumented ones. Training is three 45-minute sessions in the go-live week, by team.\n\n| Session | Content |\n|---|---|\n| One | Mental model (\"it only knows the help center; if the answer is not there it may guess\") plus four real eval failures |\n| Two | Verification drill: 20 drafts, 5 with seeded errors (an invented refund window, a wrong plan name, a missing caveat) |\n| Three | What customer data may be pasted into free-text fields, the feedback button, when to hand off to a tier-2 specialist |\n\n| Drill metric | Before coaching | After coaching |\n|---|---|---|\n| Seeded errors caught | 2 of 5 on average | 4 of 5 on a second drill set |\n| Correct drafts rewritten \"to be safe\" | 4 of the 15 | 1 of 15 |\n\n- **What changed:** a walk-through of \"click the citation, check every number and policy claim\", plus an\n  explicit rule for what to check and what to leave alone.\n- **Why report both numbers:** the rewrites are the under-trust that makes the agent feel slower than\n  typing, and improving catch rate by making people rewrite everything would be a regression.\n- **Reinforcement:** a pinned checklist in the drafting panel, two champions per team, and office hours\n  twice a week for a month.\n- **Level 3:** logs show the share of replies sent with zero edits on undocumented topics falling (the\n  desired behavior), while overall draft use rises.\n- **Level 4:** the team reports handle time and the QA error rate to the sponsor.\n- **Retraining:** when the knowledge base is restructured two months later, a 15-minute refresher shows\n  what changed. (All figures are illustrative.)","patterns":["**Drill sets built from eval failures** — before go-live, sample real failure cases from the agent's eval set, mix them with correct outputs, and keep the set versioned next to the eval data. Monitor catch and false-reject rates per cohort, and refresh the set when the eval's failure modes shift.","**Verification telemetry** — log whether a user opened the cited source or the tool-call detail before accepting an output, per claim type where possible. Monitor citation-open rate on high-risk claims (numbers, dates, policy terms); a fall after launch week signals vigilance decay.","**Release-note-driven refreshers** — tie every model, prompt, tool or knowledge-base change that alters agent behavior to a short user-facing \"what changed\" note and, if large, a refresher session. Monitor override and complaint rates in the week after each release.","**In-product job aids** — ship the \"when to trust, when to check\" card and tooltips inside the agent's UI, not in a wiki. Monitor whether new users reach target behavior without a live session.","**Feedback button that writes eval cases** — route each flagged output, with its trace, into a triage queue that can promote it to the eval set. Monitor how many flagged items become eval cases and how long each takes to reach a fix.","**Train-the-trainer with score checks** — hand the customer's champions the drill set, the mental-model script and the scoring sheet. Monitor drill scores from customer-run cohorts to catch quality drift after handoff."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Users are the human-in-the-loop step you designed. If they approve every\n  agent action unchecked, your approval gate is decoration; if they rewrite every draft, the agent saves\n  nothing. Training is how that gate gets calibrated.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Your eval set supplies the drill material, your traces and\n  citations are what users verify against, and your feedback button and logs are how bad outputs come back\n  as new eval cases.\n- **What you control.** One sentence of mental model that predicts the agent's main failure, drills built\n  from real eval failures, verification affordances (inline citations, a view of which tools ran), and a\n  \"what changed\" note for every prompt, tool or model release.\n- **What you can skip.** Instructional-design theory. Keep the two drill numbers (catch and false-reject),\n  the four Kirkpatrick levels and the base-rate warning.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Launching a drafting or support agent with human approval.** Drills calibrate the approval step so it\n  catches real errors without rewriting good drafts.\n- **Adding a write tool to an existing agent.** Each new state-changing action needs a mental-model update,\n  new drill items and a release note.\n- **Handing an agent over to a customer team.** Train-the-trainer with the drill set and scoring sheet\n  keeps verification quality from drifting after you leave.\n- **Recovering trust after a model or prompt change.** A short refresher built from the new eval failures\n  recalibrates users faster than a full retrain.","mini_project":"**A seeded-error drill generated from your agent's evals (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn an agent's eval results into a scored verification drill for its users.\n- **Steps:** run a small RAG or drafting agent over 50-100 eval questions and grade each output; sample a\n  20-item drill at a 25% error share from failed and passed cases; have two people (or two scripted\n  \"users\" with different rules) accept, edit or reject each item; score catch and false-reject with\n  `score_drill`; write a one-line \"always check\" rule from the failure types you saw.\n- **Done when:** you have a versioned drill file, a score table for both users, and a short note on which\n  failure types were missed most and what UI change would make them cheaper to verify."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    B[\"Target behavior from change plan\"] --> C[\"Curriculum: only what supports it\"]\n    C --> M[\"Mental model and real failures\"]\n    M --> D[\"Seeded-error drill\"]\n    D --> S{\"High catch, low false-reject?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| M\n    S -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Go-live: job aids, champions\"]\n    R --> L[\"Measure behavior and results\"]\n    L -->|\"behavior flat\"| U[\"Check UI cost of verifying\"]\n    L --> X{\"Model, prompt, tools or KB changed?\"}\n    X -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Refresher on what changed\"]\n    F --> D","caption":"The program is a loop, not a launch event: measurement at levels 3 and 4 and every agent change feed back\ninto the drills and examples."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is training users of an agent different from training for ordinary enterprise software?","a":"- **Behavior is not fixed.** The same input can give different outputs, and quality shifts after model,\n  prompt or tool updates, so a feature tour describes a moving target.\n- **Correctness moves to the user.** The user is often the last check or the approver of an agent action,\n  so the core skill is recognizing plausible but wrong output and verifying it quickly, not finding\n  buttons."},{"q":"Design a verification drill for a contract-clause extraction agent.","a":"- **Material:** real contracts of the users' kind, outputs from the actual agent, with errors seeded from\n  the eval set's real failure modes (wrong party, wrong date, missed exception), not garbled text.\n- **Scoring:** catch rate and false-reject rate, plus time per item, so neither over-trust nor\n  rewrite-everything passes.\n- **Habit taught:** always check dates, amounts and party names against the cited clause, regardless of\n  how confident the output looks."},{"q":"Drill scores are excellent, but QA still finds unchecked agent errors in production. What is happening?","a":"- **Base-rate gap:** the drill was perhaps 25% wrong, production a few in a hundred, and vigilance drops\n  when errors are rare.\n- **Drill too easy:** obvious seeded errors produce near-perfect scores that do not transfer.\n- **Verification too slow in the real UI:** if checking takes extra clicks and windows, people skip it.\n- **Fix:** realistic seeds, a fixed claim-type checking habit, inline citations, and telemetry on\n  citation opens."},{"q":"You ship a new tool to your agent that can issue refunds. What changes in user training?","a":"- **Mental model update:** say plainly that the agent can now propose a refund, from which inputs, and\n  what it cannot see, because users must know which actions change real state.\n- **New drill items:** seed realistic wrong refunds from eval runs (wrong amount, wrong order, refund\n  outside policy) and score catch and false-reject on approvals.\n- **Release note and telemetry:** a short \"what changed\" note, then watch approval time and override rate\n  on refund proposals in the week after launch."},{"q":"When would you skip live training entirely?","a":"- **Low-risk, low-stakes tools**, such as an internal summarizer whose output never reaches a customer or\n  record, where a job aid and a short video are enough.\n- **But not** when users are the last check on regulated or customer-facing output, because there the\n  verification skill must be practised and measured."},{"q":"How do you show a sponsor that training worked?","a":"- **Go beyond levels 1 and 2:** survey and drill scores show reaction and learning only.\n- **Level 3 from logs:** citation-open rate and edit rate on high-risk claim types moving in the target\n  direction.\n- **Level 4 against baseline:** QA error rate and handle time, compared with before training."}],"tradeoffs":["**Live, hands-on sessions vs scale.** They build real verification skill but cost user hours; recorded material scales better when the agent is low-risk.","**Role-specific tracks vs preparation cost.** They fit each job closely but multiply preparation work; one shared track is fine when roles use the agent identically.","**Showing real failures vs scaring adopters.** It calibrates trust, but can scare off adopters if presented without the verification method that handles them.","**Training close to go-live vs schedule pressure.** It improves retention but compresses the schedule; train too early and people forget before they can practise.","**Customer-run train-the-trainer vs quality.** It scales and survives handoff, but quality drifts unless you supply materials and check drill scores."],"pitfalls":["**Teaching features instead of failure modes.** Symptom: users who know every button yet forward wrong answers unchecked.","**Measuring only satisfaction surveys.** They look positive while usage logs show the target behavior never appeared.","**Never retraining after model, prompt or tool changes.** Revealed by a sudden rise in overrides or complaints after an update.","**Treating data-handling rules as an aside.** Discovered later when sensitive data shows up in prompts or logs.","**Training once at launch with no reinforcement.** Shown by usage and drill scores sliding over the following weeks.","**Seeding drills with obvious errors.** Revealed by near-perfect drill scores alongside QA still finding unchecked policy mistakes in production.","**Scoring only the catch rate.** Shows up as heavy editing of correct drafts and handle time rising instead of falling."],"when":"- **Use when** users are expected to rely on agent output or approve agent actions in work that matters,\n  especially when they are the last check before the output reaches a customer, a record or a decision.\n- **Scale effort with error cost and user count.** A low-risk internal summarizer may need a job aid and a\n  short video; a drafting agent for regulated customer communication needs drills, data-handling rules\n  and measured verification skill.\n- **Measure at behavior and results level, not just reaction:** drill catch rate and false-reject rate\n  (learning), citation-open rate and edit rate on high-risk claim types in the logs (behavior), and QA\n  error rate and handle time (results).\n- **If behavior metrics do not move**, check the UI cost of verifying before adding more sessions.\n- **Interviews:** a strong answer explains why agent training must teach verification and failure modes,\n  and how you would tell whether it worked.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the in-product affordances training relies on: inline citations, feedback buttons, tooltips and logging that shows whether verification happens.","architect":"Decides where human review sits and which outputs require it, which in turn sets what each role must be trained to check.","fde":"Runs the sessions with the customer's real cases, builds the seeded-error drills, and trains internal champions who own training after handoff."},"check":{"q":"Training satisfaction scores are high, yet QA finds agents are sending AI drafts with policy errors. What is wrong with the program, and how would you change it?","a":"The program is probably measuring reaction, not learning or behavior, and teaching what the tool does rather than how it fails. I would add a verification drill with seeded, realistic policy errors to measure whether agents can catch them, teach a concrete checking method (open the citation, check every policy claim and number), and show real failures from the eval set. Then I would track behavior in logs, such as edit rates on undocumented topics, and the QA error rate as the result. I would watch the false-reject rate too, so the fix does not swing\nagents into rewriting every draft. I would also check whether the UI makes verification fast, because a slow check gets skipped regardless of training."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which Kirkpatrick level is shown by usage logs where agents now open citations before sending drafts?","options":["Reaction","Learning","Behavior","Results"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Behavior is whether people apply the skill on the job, which logs can show. Learning would be a drill score in training; Results would be the change in the business metric such as QA errors."},{"q":"Why are seeded-error drills more useful than a quiz on product features for AI tools?","options":["Feature quizzes are harder to grade automatically","They train and measure the skill users most need: catching plausible but wrong outputs in realistic cases","They prove the model is accurate enough to deploy","They remove the need for human review in production"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Users are often the last check, so recognizing wrong outputs is the critical skill. A drill measures it directly; it says nothing about model accuracy and does not replace review."},{"q":"After a major model upgrade, what should happen to user training?","options":["Nothing, as long as the interface has not changed","Users should be retrained from scratch with the full original program","Update the failure-mode examples and give a short refresher on what behaves differently","Training should pause until the new model's accuracy reaches 100%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Users' trust is calibrated to the old system's behavior. A targeted refresher on what changed recalibrates it cheaply; a full retrain wastes time and waiting for perfection never ends."}]},{"key":"adoption/workshops","sources":["Gray, Brown & Macanufo, 2010 — Gamestorming","Lipmanowicz & McCandless, 2013 — The Surprising Power of Liberating Structures"],"why":"- **Agent knowledge lives in people's heads.** Which cases are hard, what \"good\" looks like for an answer,\n  where the workflow really breaks, which data and actions are allowed. It is spread across subject-matter\n  experts, operators, IT, security and a sponsor who rarely sit together.\n- **The slow alternatives produce gaps.** Interviews one at a time are slow and produce contradictions you\n  discover only when the agent ships. Email produces short answers from the busiest people and silence\n  from the rest.\n- **Quality criteria for generative output are often tacit.** Two experts can look at the same draft and\n  disagree on whether it is acceptable, and neither has written the rule down. You cannot build an eval,\n  a prompt or a training program until that disagreement is surfaced and resolved.\n- **Same examples, same time, is fastest.** Putting the experts in front of the same real examples at once\n  is the quickest way to surface and resolve the disagreement.\n- **Naive formats fail differently.** An open \"brainstorm\" gets dominated by the most senior or loudest\n  voice and ends with ideas nobody owns. A presentation dressed up as a workshop (you talk, they listen)\n  captures nothing. A good workshop is designed backwards from its artifact, with structure that makes\n  everyone's knowledge visible before it is debated.","how":"### 1. Fix the output and the decision first\n- **Write down what will exist at the end:** \"a rubric with 4 to 6 criteria that 5 claims experts agree\n  on\" or \"a top-3 use-case shortlist with an owner and data source each\".\n- **For agent work, name the downstream file:** the eval set, the judge prompt or the tool and approval\n  spec that the artifact will feed.\n- **Design everything backwards from it**, *because* a workshop without a defined artifact drifts into\n  discussion.\n- **Budget the agenda from the artifact, not the calendar slot.** If the output needs 20 examples labelled\n  and every disagreement discussed, estimate how many will split and how long each takes. A three-hour\n  slot that needs four hours of discussion produces a half-finished rubric and a \"we'll continue by email\"\n  that never converges.\n\n### 2. Choose participants for knowledge and authority\n- **Invite** the people who do the work, the people who can decide, and anyone with a veto (security,\n  data owner, legal) for the parts that concern them, such as which tools the agent may call.\n- **Keep the working group small**, often under about ten, *because* participation per person falls fast\n  as groups grow.\n- **Split audiences.** Separate sessions beat one huge one when audiences need different things.\n\n### 3. Prepare real material and pre-work\n- **Bring the customer's actual documents, tickets or agent outputs**, anonymized as needed.\n- **Send short pre-work** (\"list your three most painful cases\").\n- **Why:** real material grounds the conversation in facts, *because* people argue less about a specific\n  ticket than about abstractions.\n\n### 4. Structure: diverge, then converge\n1. **Silent individual work first** (sticky notes, labeling, writing) before any discussion, *because* it\n   stops the first senior opinion from anchoring everyone else.\n2. **Share in small groups, then plenary**, for example with the 1-2-4-All pattern (alone, pairs, fours,\n   whole group).\n3. **Cluster and converge with explicit criteria:** dot voting, an impact-versus-feasibility grid, or\n   discussing only the disagreements.\n4. **Keep a parking lot** so off-topic items are captured without derailing the session.\n\nAgree the decision rule before the decision, *because* a group that has not agreed how to decide will\nargue about the process as soon as the outcome is close:\n\n| Rule | How it works |\n|---|---|\n| Owner decides | The accountable owner decides after hearing everyone |\n| Consent | Adopt unless someone has a reasoned objection |\n| Vote | Majority, with the owner breaking ties |\n| Hard constraints | Legal, regulatory, security are not voted on; the owner rules and the group designs around it |\n\n### Measure agreement, not just whether people nodded\n- **Measure twice.** For labelling and rubric sessions, compute agreement on the silent first pass and\n  again after the rubric.\n- **Raw percent agreement flatters you when one label dominates.** If 90% of outputs are acceptable, two\n  raters who say \"accept\" to everything agree 90% of the time while disagreeing on every case that\n  matters.\n- **Chance-corrected statistics fix this:** Cohen's kappa for two raters, Fleiss' kappa for more.\n- **In the room, a simpler readout works:** the count of items where everyone agrees, plus the list of\n  split items, which is the agenda for the discussion.\n- **If agreement does not rise after the rubric**, the criteria are still ambiguous or the experts\n  genuinely want different things; escalate to whoever owns the definition of \"good\".\n- **The same math checks your LLM judge later:** compare the judge's labels with the experts' consensus\n  using kappa, not raw agreement.\n\n### 5. Facilitate actively\n- **The facilitator owns time**, makes quiet participants heard, and stops solution debates that belong\n  elsewhere.\n- **Outsider advantage.** On a customer engagement the FDE is often both facilitator and domain outsider,\n  which is useful: naive questions expose tacit rules.\n\n### 6. Capture live and follow up fast\n- **Write outputs into the artifact during the session**, not from memory later.\n- **Send a summary within a day or two** with decisions, open questions, owners and dates, *because*\n  agreement decays quickly and the summary becomes the record people refer to.\n\n### Common agent workshop types\nEach has a different artifact, so do not merge them.\n\n| Type | Artifact | Where it lands in the agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| Use-case discovery | Candidates ranked by value and feasibility | Which agent to build first |\n| Workflow mapping | As-is and to-be, where AI sits and who reviews | Tool list, step order, approval gates |\n| Rubric and labeling | Agreed definition of good output, labeled examples | Golden eval set, judge prompt |\n| Enablement or prompting | Users able to work with the tool | Adoption, feedback quality |","example":"### Measuring agreement in a labeling session\nPercent agreement can look excellent while two raters disagree on every case that matters; kappa exposes\nit, and the split list is the discussion agenda.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef percent_agreement(a, b):\n    return sum(x == y for x, y in zip(a, b)) / len(a)\n\ndef cohens_kappa(a, b):\n    n = len(a)\n    po = percent_agreement(a, b)\n    ca, cb = Counter(a), Counter(b)\n    pe = sum(ca[k] * cb[k] for k in set(a) | set(b)) / (n * n)\n    return (po - pe) / (1 - pe) if pe < 1 else 1.0\n\n# 20 outputs, 18 truly acceptable. Rater A accepts everything;\n# rater B accepts 18 and rejects the 2 that matter.\na = [\"accept\"] * 20\nb = [\"accept\"] * 18 + [\"reject\"] * 2\nprint(percent_agreement(a, b))          # 0.9: looks great\nprint(round(cohens_kappa(a, b), 2))     # 0.0: no agreement beyond chance\n\ndef unanimous(rows):\n    \"\"\"rows: one list of labels per item, one label per rater.\"\"\"\n    split = [i for i, r in enumerate(rows) if len(set(r)) > 1]\n    return len(rows) - len(split), split   # agenda = the split items\n\nrows = [[\"accept\"] * 5] * 9 + [[\"accept\", \"edit\", \"accept\", \"reject\", \"edit\"]] * 11\nagree, agenda = unanimous(rows)\nprint(agree, len(agenda))               # 9 unanimous, 11 to discuss\n```\n\n### From workshop labels to an LLM-judge check\nAfter the workshop, the expert consensus labels become the reference for the agent's automated grader.\nReuse `cohens_kappa` above to decide whether the judge prompt built from the rubric can be trusted. The\nlabels here are illustrative.\n\n```python\n# consensus label per fresh draft after the rubric, and the LLM judge's label on the same drafts\nexperts = [\"accept\"] * 6 + [\"edit\"] * 2 + [\"reject\"] * 2\njudge   = [\"accept\"] * 6 + [\"accept\", \"edit\"] + [\"reject\", \"accept\"]\n\nprint(percent_agreement(experts, judge))        # 0.8\nprint(round(cohens_kappa(experts, judge), 2))   # 0.58\nmisses = [i for i, (e, j) in enumerate(zip(experts, judge)) if e != j]\nprint(misses)                                    # [6, 9]: judge passed drafts experts would not\n# Read the misses against the rubric, tighten the judge prompt, and re-run before\n# letting the judge gate releases of the agent.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a rubric for an agent's claim-denial letters\nA hypothetical insurer needs acceptance criteria for claim-denial letters drafted by a claims agent. The\nteam runs a three-hour rubric workshop with five senior claims handlers, a compliance officer and the FDE\nas facilitator.\n\n**Agenda, designed backwards from the output** (times illustrative):\n\n| Block | Minutes |\n|---|---|\n| Framing and decision rule (compliance rules on legal requirements, the claims director's delegate on everything else) | 15 |\n| Silent labeling | 30 |\n| Reveal the label grid | 15 |\n| Split cases, roughly 5 minutes each | about 60 |\n| Draft criteria | 30 |\n| Relabel | 20 |\n| Owners and open questions | 10 |\n\n- **The budget only works if about 10 to 12 cases split**, which is why the facilitator picks the 20\n  drafts to include obvious passes and obvious fails as well as hard cases.\n- **Pre-work:** each handler sends two letters they consider excellent and two they would reject.\n- **First pass:** everyone labels the same 20 agent drafts silently as accept, edit or reject. The five\n  handlers agree unanimously on only 9 of the 20.\n- **Discussion:** rather than debate all 20, the group discusses just the 11 split cases.\n\nThe disagreements cluster into four themes:\n1. Whether the letter cites the exact policy clause.\n2. Whether the tone is acceptable for a bereaved claimant.\n3. Whether appeal rights are stated. Compliance rules these are mandatory, turning the theme from\n   preference into a hard requirement.\n4. Whether the letter speculates about facts not in the file.\n\n**Result:** four criteria with a pass and a fail example each. Relabeling 10 fresh drafts with the rubric,\nunanimous agreement rises to 8 of 10. Output: a written rubric, 30 labeled examples for the golden set,\ntwo open questions with owners, and a follow-up summary sent the next morning. (Figures are\nillustrative.)\n\n**What the agent team does with it:** the mandatory appeal-rights rule becomes a deterministic check on\nevery letter, the other three criteria go into the judge prompt, and the 30 labeled examples become the\nregression set run on every prompt or model change.","patterns":["**Labeling tool instead of sticky notes for rubric sessions** — when the output is labels, have each expert label in a shared sheet or annotation tool with hidden columns, then reveal the grid. Monitor the unanimous count and the split list live; that list sets the discussion agenda.","**Agenda budget from the split estimate** — before the session, pilot-label a few items yourself to estimate the split rate, then size the discussion block as split items times minutes per item. Monitor whether discussion overruns; if it does, cut scope rather than extend by email.","**Workshop output straight into the eval set** — commit the labeled examples and rubric to the agent's eval repository the same week, with the workshop date and participants recorded. Monitor agreement between the rubric-based LLM or human graders and the expert labels on later batches.","**Hard constraints as code, preferences as rubric** — anything the owner ruled mandatory (appeal rights, a forbidden action) becomes a deterministic check or a tool permission, not a judge criterion. Monitor the check's failure rate per release.","**Decision log with owners** — record every decision, its decision rule, the owner and any open question in one shared document sent within a day or two. Monitor how many settled decisions get reopened over the following month.","**Veto holders booked for their segment only** — schedule security, legal or the data owner for the part of the agenda that concerns them, such as tool permissions, rather than the whole day. Monitor late objections after the session."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Your agent's eval set, judge rubric, tool boundaries and approval gates\n  all encode what experts consider \"good\" and \"allowed\". That knowledge is tacit and contested; a rubric or\n  workflow workshop is the fastest way to make it explicit enough to code.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Workshop labels become golden eval cases, the rubric becomes the\n  grading prompt for an LLM judge, and the to-be workflow map becomes the agent's tool list, step order\n  and human-review points.\n- **What you control.** Bring real agent outputs (not hypotheticals), have experts label silently first,\n  measure agreement with a chance-corrected statistic, and commit the rubric and labels to the eval repo\n  the same week.\n- **What you can skip.** Facilitation games beyond silent work, 1-2-4-All and dot voting. The agreement\n  math and the artifact-first agenda matter most.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Defining \"good\" for an agent's outputs.** A rubric and labeling workshop produces the golden set and\n  the judge criteria in one afternoon.\n- **Deciding tool permissions and approval gates.** A workflow-mapping session with the veto holders\n  settles which actions the agent may take alone.\n- **Picking the first agent to build.** A discovery workshop ranks candidate workflows by value and\n  feasibility, with an owner and data source each.\n- **Calibrating an LLM judge.** Expert consensus labels from the session are the reference the judge is\n  measured against.","mini_project":"**From silent labels to a validated judge (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn a small labeling exercise into a rubric and check whether an LLM judge built from it\n  agrees with people.\n- **Steps:** generate 20 outputs from a small agent; have three people (or yourself on three separate\n  passes) label each accept, edit or reject silently; list unanimous and split items; write a 4-criterion\n  rubric from the splits; relabel 10 fresh outputs; write a judge prompt from the rubric and compare its\n  labels with the consensus using `cohens_kappa`.\n- **Done when:** you have the rubric file, the first-pass and relabel agreement numbers, the judge's\n  kappa against consensus, and a list of the judge's disagreements with a note on each."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    O[\"Define artifact and decision rule\"] --> P[\"Pick people with knowledge and veto\"]\n    P --> M[\"Real agent outputs plus pre-work\"]\n    M --> S[\"Silent individual labeling\"]\n    S --> R[\"Reveal grid: unanimous vs split\"]\n    R --> D[\"Discuss only split items\"]\n    D --> C[\"Draft criteria, owner rules on hard constraints\"]\n    C --> L[\"Relabel fresh items\"]\n    L --> Q{\"Agreement rose?\"}\n    Q -->|\"no\"| E[\"Escalate to owner of quality\"]\n    Q -->|\"yes\"| F[\"Rubric and labels into eval repo\"]\n    F --> G[\"Summary with owners in 1-2 days\"]","caption":"Follow the path from artifact to follow-up; the \"no\" branch is what happens when relabeling shows\nagreement did not rise, and the last two steps are what turn the session into agent eval data."},"interview":[{"q":"What distinguishes a workshop from a long meeting?","a":"- **A defined artifact:** a rubric, ranked shortlist, workflow map or labeled set exists at the end, with\n  owners.\n- **Structure designed backwards from that artifact:** silent individual work, explicit convergence and an\n  agreed decision rule, because unstructured discussion drifts and gets anchored by seniority.\n- **Fast follow-up:** a written summary within a day or two, so decisions do not decay."},{"q":"Two raters agree 90% of the time. Is your rubric working?","a":"- **Not necessarily.** If 90% of outputs are acceptable, two raters who accept everything also agree 90%\n  while disagreeing on every case that matters.\n- **Use a chance-corrected statistic:** Cohen's kappa for two raters, Fleiss' kappa for more.\n- **And look at the split items directly**, because they are where the tacit rules differ."},{"q":"How do you turn a rubric workshop into an automated eval for your agent?","a":"- **Split the output:** rules the owner ruled mandatory become deterministic checks; preference criteria\n  become the LLM-judge prompt, each with its pass and fail example.\n- **Validate the judge** against the expert consensus labels with kappa, and read every disagreement\n  before trusting it to gate releases.\n- **Commit the labels** as a regression set and re-run it on every prompt, tool or model change."},{"q":"Your workshop produced a rubric, but a month later teams are reopening every decision. What went wrong?","a":"- **Likely causes:** slow or missing follow-up summary, a veto holder who was not in the room, or no\n  decision rule agreed up front.\n- **Also check** whether the session ratified a decision already made, which yields an output nobody\n  defends.\n- **Fix:** a decision log with owners sent within a day or two, and the veto holders booked for their part."},{"q":"You need to decide which tools an agent may call and where a human approves. How would you run that workshop?","a":"- **Artifact first:** a to-be workflow map listing each step, the tool it calls, read or write, and who\n  approves write actions.\n- **People:** the operators who do the work, the process owner, and security and the data owner for the\n  tool-permission segment, because they hold the veto.\n- **Real cases:** walk five recent tickets through the map silently, then discuss only where people placed\n  the approval differently; the owner rules on hard constraints."},{"q":"When would you not run a workshop?","a":"- **When one expert can answer by message**, because pulling eight people into a room costs far more than\n  one reply.\n- **When the decision is already made** and only needs communicating; a \"workshop\" to ratify it is detected\n  quickly and costs trust.\n- **When the agent question is narrow**, such as one tool's argument format, a short review with the tool's\n  owner is cheaper than a session."}],"tradeoffs":["**In-person vs remote.** In-person builds trust and energy but costs travel and calendar time; remote works well for short, focused labeling sessions.","**Decision-makers in the room vs junior candor.** Their presence lets the session commit, but can silence junior experts unless silent individual work comes first.","**Tight structure vs surprise.** It produces predictable artifacts but can miss surprising insights; leave a slot for open discussion when exploring a new domain.","**Broad attendance vs depth.** It builds buy-in yet reduces depth per person; split into focused sessions when more than about ten people need to contribute.","**Full day vs two half-days.** A full day gets much done at once, but fatigue erodes the afternoon; two half-days often produce better output."],"pitfalls":["**No defined output.** The session ends with energy but no artifact; symptom: a follow-up email that only says \"great session\".","**Starting with open discussion.** The most senior voice anchors everyone; the output mirrors one person's view.","**Hypothetical examples instead of real agent outputs.** Revealed later when the rubric fails on actual cases.","**Missing the veto holder.** A security or compliance objection arrives weeks later and invalidates agreed decisions, such as a tool the agent was going to call.","**Slow follow-up.** Within a week participants remember different decisions and reopen settled questions.","**Ratifying a decision already made.** Participants detect it quickly; symptom: polite silence and an output nobody defends later.","**No agreed decision rule.** Revealed when the last hour is spent arguing about who gets to decide rather than about the criteria."],"when":"- **Use when** the knowledge you need is spread across several people, they are likely to disagree, or\n  you need a joint commitment rather than individual opinions: discovery, workflow redesign, defining\n  quality criteria, labeling a first golden set, and user enablement.\n- **Avoid when** a single expert can answer by message, or when the decision is already made and only\n  needs communicating.\n- **Measure** by the artifact and by whether decisions held a month later; for labelling sessions, also by\n  whether agreement measurably rose between the first pass and the relabel.\n- **Interviews:** describe the output you would design for and how you would stop anchoring.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Prepares real examples and tooling for labeling sessions, and turns the agreed rubric and labels into eval cases and prompt changes.","architect":"Uses workflow and discovery workshops to settle boundaries: where AI sits, who reviews, which systems and data are in scope.","fde":"Designs and facilitates the sessions on site, extracts tacit expert rules through naive questions, and owns the follow-up summary."},"check":{"q":"Your client's experts keep disagreeing about whether the AI's answers are \"good enough\". How would you design a workshop to resolve this, and why that structure?","a":"I would design backwards from a written rubric plus a labeled example set. Experts would first label the same real outputs silently, which makes disagreement measurable and stops anchoring on the most senior person. We would then discuss only the cases where labels split, cluster the reasons into criteria, and let the person with authority (often compliance or the process owner) rule on which criteria are mandatory. Finally we would relabel fresh examples with the rubric to check that agreement improved. The output feeds the eval set, and a summary with owners goes out within a day."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why start a workshop segment with silent individual work before group discussion?","options":["It shortens the session by skipping discussion","It captures everyone's view before the first opinion, often the most senior, anchors the group","It lets the facilitator grade participants individually","It is required for remote sessions only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Anchoring and deference to seniority distort group output. Writing or labeling alone first makes every view visible, and the following discussion can focus on genuine disagreements."},{"q":"In a labeling workshop, five experts agree on 9 of 20 outputs. What is the most efficient next step?","options":["Replace the experts with a single senior reviewer","Average the labels and move on","Discuss only the split cases and turn the reasons for disagreement into explicit criteria","Ask the model to label the cases instead"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The split cases contain the tacit rules that differ between experts. Turning them into criteria makes the rubric explicit; averaging or delegating hides the disagreement that will resurface later."},{"q":"What best indicates that a discovery workshop succeeded?","options":["Participants rated it highly in the feedback form","The session ran to the planned agenda exactly","A defined artifact exists with owners, and the decisions are still holding weeks later","Every attendee spoke at least once"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A workshop is a means to an artifact and a durable decision. Satisfaction and agenda adherence can be high in a session that produced nothing anyone acts on."}]},{"key":"adoption/stakeholder-management","sources":[],"why":"- **Agent deployments touch many groups.** The sponsor funds it, a process owner's team uses it, IT runs\n  the integration, security and legal review the data flow and tool access, a data owner controls\n  source-system access, procurement handles the contract, and in some regions a works council or union must\n  be consulted when work changes.\n- **Each can slow or stop the project, and each defines success differently.** The sponsor wants a\n  business metric, users want less tedium and no blame, security wants no new exposure, the data owner\n  wants no surprises.\n- **Sending company data to a model, and letting it act through tools, triggers reviews** (data\n  classification, residency, vendor risk, access control) that ordinary tools may not, and those reviewers\n  have real veto power.\n- **Probabilistic quality needs expectation management.** A sponsor who heard \"it works\" in the demo will\n  treat a 5% error rate as a failure unless someone set that expectation early; expectations drift away\n  from what the agent can deliver.\n- **The naive approaches fail.** Talking mostly to the sponsor and friendly champion optimizes for people\n  already on your side, so blockers surface when they block (at the security review or go-live, when\n  changes cost most). A weekly email to everyone is noise to most and too shallow for the few who decide.","how":"### 1. Identify everyone, including the quiet vetoes\n- **List people and groups, not departments.** For each, ask: can they approve, block, fund, or are they\n  affected?\n- **Probe for the roles agent projects forget:** data owners for every system a tool touches, security,\n  privacy, legal, compliance, IT operations, team leads of end users, and employee representatives.\n- **Ask each stakeholder \"who else should I talk to?\"**, *because* informal vetoes are often invisible on\n  the org chart.\n\n### 2. Map influence, interest and stance\nThe power-interest grid (usually attributed to Mendelow) sorts people into four groups:\n\n| | Low interest | High interest |\n|---|---|---|\n| **High power** | Keep satisfied | Manage closely |\n| **Low power** | Monitor | Keep informed |\n\n- **Add stance** (supporter, neutral, skeptic, blocker), plus what each person needs to believe to support\n  the project and what they fear.\n- **Why the grid:** engagement effort is finite, so the grid tells you where to spend it.\n- **Stance is the attribute that changes.** Record the evidence for it (\"asked twice about data\n  residency\") rather than a label, and expect to revise it.\n\nFor each important stakeholder, write down what they are measured on and what this project does to that\nmeasure:\n\n| Stakeholder | Scorecard | What the project does to it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Security lead | Rewarded for incidents that did not happen | Gains nothing from your launch |\n| Team lead | Judged on throughput | The pilot dents it for a few weeks |\n| Data owner | Blamed if their data leaks | Leak risk through someone else's system |\n\n- **Most \"irrational\" resistance becomes predictable** once you see the project from their scorecard.\n- **The fix is usually to change what the project costs them** (a complete review package, a protected\n  target during the pilot, a narrower data scope or a read-only tool), not to argue harder about the\n  benefits.\n\n### 3. Define decision rights\n- **For each key decision** (scope, data access, tool permissions, go-live, rollback), agree who is\n  responsible, accountable, consulted and informed (RACI).\n- **Why:** it prevents the classic stall where three people each think another one approves the release,\n  *because* an agent go-live usually needs sign-off from several functions.\n\n### 4. Sequence by lead time, not by friendliness\n- **Some approvals have long, fixed lead times:** a security or vendor-risk review queue, a data-access\n  request, a works-council consultation, a procurement cycle.\n- **Lay them out like a project plan and work backwards from go-live**, *because* the latest-starting\n  long-lead review sets the real launch date regardless of how fast the engineering goes.\n- **Worked case:** go-live is in ten weeks and the security queue is running at six, so the review package\n  must be submitted by week four, which means the data-flow design and tool list must be stable before then.\n\n### 5. Plan engagement per person\n| Who | Engagement |\n|---|---|\n| Manage-closely stakeholders | One-to-ones and early drafts |\n| Security and legal | Architecture, data-flow and tool-permission documentation before they ask |\n| Users and team leads | Workshops and pilots |\n| Executives | Short decision-focused updates |\n\nSecurity and legal get the package early *because* a reviewer who receives a complete package early is\nfar less likely to block late.\n\n### 6. Escalate with options, not complaints\n1. **Escalate only after one-to-one work fails**, through the sponsor.\n2. **State the blocker's concern fairly**, with two or three options (narrow scope, add a control, delay)\n   and their costs.\n3. **Tell the blocker before you escalate.**\n4. **Frame it as a decision.** \"They are in the way\" costs you the relationship for the rest of the\n   project; \"we need a decision between these options\" usually gets one.\n\n### 7. Maintain the map\n- **Keep a register** with owner, stance, last contact and next action; review it weekly.\n- **Update it when people change roles.**\n- **Build more than one senior supporter.** When a sponsor leaves, the successor inherits a project they\n  did not choose and may cut it.","example":"### A stakeholder register with an overdue-attention check\nA minimal stakeholder register, the kind an FDE keeps alongside the agent project plan, with a check that\nflags where attention is overdue. The rule encodes the grid: high-power people who are not supporters, or\nwhom nobody has spoken to recently, need action this week.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import date\n\nstakeholders = [\n    # name, role, power 1-5, interest 1-5, stance, last contact\n    (\"Priya\", \"VP Ops (sponsor)\",         5, 5, \"supporter\", date(2026, 10, 14)),\n    (\"Marc\",  \"CISO delegate\",            5, 2, \"skeptic\",   date(2026, 9, 2)),\n    (\"Lena\",  \"Data owner, claims DB\",    4, 2, \"neutral\",   date(2026, 9, 20)),\n    (\"Tom\",   \"Team lead, adjusters\",     3, 5, \"blocker\",   date(2026, 10, 1)),\n    (\"Ana\",   \"Adjuster (champion)\",      2, 5, \"supporter\", date(2026, 10, 16)),\n]\n\ndef quadrant(power, interest):\n    if power >= 4:\n        return \"manage closely\" if interest >= 4 else \"keep satisfied\"\n    return \"keep informed\" if interest >= 4 else \"monitor\"\n\ndef actions(today, stale_days=21):\n    for name, role, p, i, stance, last in stakeholders:\n        q, age = quadrant(p, i), (today - last).days\n        flags = []\n        if p >= 4 and stance != \"supporter\":\n            flags.append(f\"high power, {stance}\")\n        if stance == \"blocker\":\n            flags.append(\"blocker: find out what they fear\")\n        if p >= 4 and age > stale_days:\n            flags.append(f\"no contact for {age} days\")\n        if flags:\n            print(f\"{name:5} {role:24} [{q}] -> \" + \"; \".join(flags))\n\nactions(date(2026, 10, 20))\n```\n\nWhat the check flags on this data:\n\n| Who | Flag | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Marc | Skeptic with a veto, 48 days without contact | Send the data-flow and tool-permission package and book a review |\n| Lena | Neutral data owner, 30 days | Confirm access terms for the claims-lookup tool |\n| Tom | Team lead blocking adoption | One-to-one about what he fears, usually blame for agent errors or his team's metrics |\n\nThe sponsor and the champion need nothing this week.\n\n### Which reviews does the agent's tool list trigger?\nThe tool inventory drives the approval plan. This sketch maps each tool to the reviews it triggers and\nback-schedules them from go-live. Lead times are the ones from the scenario below; the tool list is\nillustrative.\n\n```python\nGO_LIVE_WEEK = 10\nLEAD_WEEKS = {\"security\": 6, \"data_access\": 3, \"works_council\": 8}   # 3 and 8 illustrative\n\ntools = [\n    # name, system, writes?, touches personal data?\n    (\"search_claims\",   \"claims DB\",  False, True),\n    (\"get_policy\",      \"policy API\", False, False),\n    (\"update_claim\",    \"claims DB\",  True,  True),\n]\n\ndef reviews_for(tool):\n    name, system, writes, personal = tool\n    out = {\"security\"} if writes or personal else set()\n    if personal:\n        out.add(\"data_access\")\n    return out\n\nneeded = set().union(*(reviews_for(t) for t in tools))\nneeded.add(\"works_council\")          # the agent changes how adjusters work\nfor r in sorted(needed, key=lambda r: GO_LIVE_WEEK - LEAD_WEEKS[r]):\n    print(f\"{r:14} submit by week {GO_LIVE_WEEK - LEAD_WEEKS[r]}\")\n# works_council  submit by week 2\n# security       submit by week 4\n# data_access    submit by week 7\n# Dropping update_claim (read-only pilot) would not remove security here,\n# but it usually makes that review shorter and the package smaller.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: back-scheduling approvals from go-live\nGo-live for a claims agent is in ten weeks. Lay out each long-lead approval and find its latest start; the\nsecurity figure comes from the case above, the others are illustrative.\n\n| Approval | Lead time (weeks) | Latest submission week | Prerequisite |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Security review queue | 6 | 4 | Data-flow design and tool list stable |\n| Data-access request | 3 (illustrative) | 7 | Data scope agreed with owner |\n| Works-council consultation | 8 (illustrative) | 2 | Impact on roles written down |\n\n- **The binding constraint is the works council**, not engineering: it must start in week 2, so the\n  role-impact statement is due before any build is finished.\n- **Security is next:** the data-flow design and the agent's tool permissions must stabilize by week 4,\n  which forces architecture decisions early.\n- **Friendliness is irrelevant to the order.** Engaging the friendliest reviewer first would leave the\n  project waiting in a queue after the build is done.","patterns":["**Approval lead-time plan** — at kickoff, ask each reviewing function for its current queue length and put every approval on the project plan as a dependency with a latest-start date. Monitor the gap between planned and actual submission dates each week.","**Review package ready before the ask** — prepare the architecture and data-flow diagram, data classification, storage location and retention, vendor terms, access controls and logging as one bundle, plus the agent's tool inventory with read or write scope, approval gates and kill switch. Monitor the number of reviewer follow-up questions and the time from submission to decision.","**Least-privilege tools as a negotiating lever** — when a reviewer balks, offer a read-only pilot, a narrower data scope or a human approval on every write before arguing benefits. Monitor which tool scopes each review approved, and re-submit only the delta when you widen them.","**Register with automated staleness flags** — keep the register in a sheet or tracker and run a weekly check like the one above (high power and not a supporter, or no contact past a threshold). Monitor how many high-power entries are stale at each weekly review.","**RACI in the project charter** — write the RACI for scope, data access, tool permissions, go-live and rollback into the charter the sponsor signs. Monitor decisions that slip past their due date, which usually point at an unclear accountable owner.","**Eval-anchored expectations** — include the current eval error rate, the baseline and the mitigation in every sponsor update from the first demo onward. Monitor whether the sponsor can restate the expected error rate unprompted."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent that reads company data and calls tools that change records\n  triggers more reviews than a chatbot: data access per tool, credentials, vendor risk, audit and rollback.\n  Each reviewer holds a veto and has a queue, and the queue often sets your launch date.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The tool inventory (read vs write, which system, which\n  credentials), the data-flow diagram from user to model to tools, approval gates, audit logs and the kill\n  switch are what reviewers read.\n- **What you control.** Design tools with narrow scopes so reviews are easier, prepare the review package\n  before anyone asks, back-schedule approvals from go-live, and put eval error rates in every sponsor\n  update so expectations stay real.\n- **What you can skip.** Stakeholder theory beyond the power-interest grid and RACI. A register with a\n  weekly staleness check covers most agent projects.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Getting a tool-using agent through security review.** A complete package with the tool inventory and\n  controls, submitted ahead of the queue, avoids the two-weeks-before-launch veto.\n- **Widening an agent from read-only to write actions.** Each new tool scope goes back to the reviewers\n  and data owners, on their lead times.\n- **Agents that change frontline work.** Team leads and employee representatives are stakeholders whose\n  consultation can set the launch date.\n- **Keeping a sponsor through an agent pilot.** Eval-anchored expectations and a second senior supporter\n  protect the project from one bad week or one reorg.","mini_project":"**A review plan generated from your agent's tool list (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn an agent's tool definitions into a stakeholder and approval plan.\n- **Steps:** list the tools of an agent you have built (or a sample of 4-6) with system, read or write,\n  credential and data sensitivity; map each to the reviews and data owners it triggers; assign\n  illustrative lead times and back-schedule from a go-live week; build a five-person register and run the\n  staleness check; draft a one-page review package outline.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of tools to reviewers, a latest-submission week per approval, the\n  register flags for this week, and one change (a narrower tool scope) that shortens the plan."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Stakeholder in register\"] --> P{\"High power?\"}\n    P -->|\"yes\"| PI{\"High interest?\"}\n    P -->|\"no\"| LI{\"High interest?\"}\n    PI -->|\"yes\"| MC[\"Manage closely\"]\n    PI -->|\"no\"| KS[\"Keep satisfied\"]\n    LI -->|\"yes\"| KI[\"Keep informed\"]\n    LI -->|\"no\"| MO[\"Monitor\"]\n    MC --> ST{\"Supporter and recent contact?\"}\n    KS --> ST\n    ST -->|\"no\"| A[\"Act this week: package, one-to-one\"]\n    ST -->|\"yes\"| W[\"Weekly register review\"]\n    A --> W\n    KI --> W\n    MO --> W\n    W -.->|\"next week\"| S","caption":"Each stakeholder is routed by power and interest; for high-power people, stance and recent contact decide\nwhether this week needs action. The weekly review loops everyone back through, since stance and roles change."},"interview":[{"q":"What is a power-interest grid, and what do you add to it for an agent project?","a":"- **Definition:** a two-by-two of power and interest giving four strategies: manage closely, keep\n  satisfied, keep informed, monitor; it tells you where finite engagement effort goes.\n- **Add stance with evidence** (supporter, neutral, skeptic, blocker) and what each person is measured on,\n  because resistance becomes predictable from their scorecard.\n- **For agents specifically**, probe for the data owner of every system a tool touches, security, privacy\n  and works councils, who hold vetoes but rarely appear in the first list."},{"q":"What goes in the security review package for an agent that can write to a customer system?","a":"- **Data flow:** what leaves the company, to which model provider, stored where and for how long, plus\n  vendor terms and data classification.\n- **Tool inventory:** each tool, the system it calls, read or write, the credential it uses and its scope,\n  and which actions need human approval.\n- **Controls:** audit logs of every tool call, rate limits, a kill switch and the rollback path for a\n  wrong write, with eval results on the write actions."},{"q":"A team lead is quietly steering his team away from the pilot. How do you handle it?","a":"- **One-to-one first**, to learn what he fears: blame for agent errors, or the pilot denting his team's\n  throughput.\n- **Change what the project costs him:** a protected target during the pilot, a clear rule on who owns\n  agent errors, involvement in the pilot review.\n- **Escalate only with options** if that fails, and tell him before you do."},{"q":"Your go-live date is set by engineering estimates. Why might that be wrong?","a":"- **Approvals have fixed lead times** (security queue, data access, procurement, works council) that do\n  not shrink when engineering speeds up.\n- **The latest-starting long-lead review sets the real date.** With a six-week security queue and go-live in\n  ten weeks, the package is due in week four.\n- **So** back-schedule approvals first and let them drive when designs and the tool list must be stable."},{"q":"Security approved a read-only agent. Product now wants it to update records. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Treat it as a new review**, not a tweak: send the delta (new tool, scope, credential, approval gate,\n  rollback) to the same reviewer before building it into the release.\n- **Offer staged options:** human approval on every write first, then wider autonomy once eval and\n  override data show it is safe.\n- **Re-plan the date** around that reviewer's queue and tell the sponsor now, not at go-live."},{"q":"When is a full stakeholder map overkill?","a":"- **A small internal tool used by one team**, with no new data access and no security or compliance\n  review, needs only a short list of approvers.\n- **It flips back** as soon as the tool crosses team boundaries or sends company data to a model, because\n  those bring vetoes you cannot see from inside the team.\n- **For agents, the trigger is usually the first tool** that reads another team's system or writes\n  anywhere, because that brings in a data owner and a security reviewer."}],"tradeoffs":["**Formal mapping vs time.** Mapping everyone catches hidden vetoes but costs time; a small internal tool with one team needs only a short list.","**Engaging blockers early vs momentum.** It surfaces objections while they are cheap but can slow momentum; skip only when their approval is genuinely not required.","**Single strong sponsor vs resilience.** One sponsor makes decisions fast but is a single point of failure if that person moves; cultivate a second senior supporter.","**Tailored messages vs consistency.** Tailoring improves relevance but risks inconsistent stories; keep one source of truth that every message draws from.","**Open error rates vs alarm.** Sharing the error rate sets realistic expectations but can alarm cautious stakeholders unless paired with the mitigation and the baseline.","**Broad tool access vs review speed.** Write tools across several systems make a more capable agent but pull in more reviewers and longer queues; start narrow and widen with evidence."],"pitfalls":["**Talking mainly to supporters.** Symptom: a surprise \"no\" from security or legal at the go-live review.","**Leaving decision rights implicit.** Revealed when go-live waits for weeks because nobody knows who signs off.","**Ignoring team leads of end users.** Shows up as adoption varying sharply between teams with the same agent.","**Letting the sponsor's expectations drift from the eval results.** A normal error rate is then read as project failure.","**Never updating the map after reorganizations.** Discovered when a new executive cancels a project they never heard of.","**Engaging long-lead reviewers by friendliness rather than lead time.** Revealed when the build is done and the project waits weeks in a review queue.","**Adding a write tool after security signed off.** Symptom: a second review, or a reviewer who feels misled. Fix: treat any new tool or wider scope as a change that goes back to the reviewer."],"when":"- **Use when** an agent crosses team boundaries, needs data access from another owner, calls tools in\n  systems you do not own, or requires security or compliance review, which covers most enterprise work.\n- **Timing:** build the map in the first week of scoping and review it weekly through rollout and\n  hypercare.\n- **Measure indirectly:** no late vetoes, decisions made on schedule, approvals submitted ahead of each\n  reviewer's lead time, and sponsors whose expectations match the evals.\n- **Scale down** for a small internal tool used by one team: a short list of approvers is enough.\n- **Interviews:** show that you look for blockers and veto holders first, not just sponsors.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the artifacts reviewers need, such as data-flow diagrams, eval results and access logs, and answers technical objections precisely.","architect":"Owns the decision rights for architecture, data access and security sign-off, and makes sure reviewers see designs before they harden.","fde":"Maintains the map inside the customer, finds informal vetoes by asking who else matters, and converts skeptics through one-to-ones."},"check":{"q":"Two weeks before go-live, the customer's security team, never formally engaged, asks to review the system and signals concern. How do you respond, and what would you have done differently?","a":"Now: treat it as legitimate, not an obstacle. I would meet them immediately with a complete package (architecture and data-flow diagram, data classification, where data is stored and for how long, vendor terms, access controls, logging) and ask for their specific concerns in writing so we can resolve them one by one. I would tell the sponsor honestly that go-live may move. Earlier: security has a veto on any system that sends company data to a model, so they belong in the high-power quadrant from week one, with their review lead time on the plan and the data-flow design reviewed before integration was built. I would also ask what they are accountable for, because a package that answers their specific exposure questions moves faster than one that argues the business value."},"quiz":[{"q":"On a power-interest grid, a security reviewer who rarely engages but can block go-live belongs where?","options":["Monitor, because their interest is low","Keep informed, because they only need status updates","Keep satisfied, because high power means their objection can stop the project","Manage closely, because every reviewer must attend weekly meetings"],"answer":2,"explanation":"High power with low interest means \"keep satisfied\": give them what they need to approve, early and complete. Monitoring them risks a late veto; weekly meetings waste their time."},{"q":"Why define a RACI for the go-live decision on an AI deployment?","options":["To make the sponsor solely responsible for any errors","Because go-live usually needs several functions, and without explicit decision rights the release stalls","Because RACI documents are required by most AI regulations","To replace the need for security and compliance reviews"],"answer":1,"explanation":"AI go-lives typically need sign-off from operations, security, compliance and IT. A RACI makes the accountable approver explicit, so nobody waits for an approval nobody believes is theirs."},{"q":"A team lead is openly discouraging his team from using the tool. What is the best first move?","options":["Escalate to the sponsor to mandate usage on his team","Exclude his team from the rollout","Meet him one-to-one to learn what he fears, such as blame for AI errors or damage to team metrics","Send his team additional training materials"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blockers usually have a concrete concern. Understanding it allows a real fix; mandating through the sponsor tends to produce compliance theatre, and more training does not address his objection."}]},{"key":"adoption/exec-communication","sources":["Minto, 1987 — The Pyramid Principle"],"why":"- **Executive attention is the bottleneck, not your work.** Executives allocate money, people and\n  political cover across many initiatives. They see your project for a few minutes a week, usually\n  between meetings, and decide from that whether to keep funding, expand or intervene.\n- **Engineers default to chronological, bottom-up reporting:** what we did, what we found, finally what it\n  means. That mirrors how the work happened but forces the reader to do the synthesis, and the conclusion\n  often arrives after their attention has gone.\n- **Technical framing hides the meaning.** \"Retrieval recall@10 improved from 0.71 to 0.83\" or \"tool-call\n  success is up\" leaves the executive unable to tell whether this is good news for the business.\n- **Agents make it harder in three ways.** Quality is a rate, not a yes or no, so \"it works\" and \"it\n  fails\" both mislead; results are uncertain early, so honest ranges beat false precision; and agents that\n  act carry reputational and compliance risk, so bad news that arrives late destroys trust faster than the\n  bad news itself.\n- **The naive alternatives both fail.** Dumping the full status report is not read; polishing everything\n  green is believed until it is not.","how":"### 1. Lead with the answer\n- **BLUF (bottom line up front):** put the conclusion and any ask in the first two lines.\n- **Minto's Pyramid Principle gives the structure:** state the governing point, support it with a few\n  grouped arguments, support each with evidence. *Because* the reader may stop at any point, each level\n  should stand on its own.\n- **SCQA frames the opening when context is needed:** situation (what is true), complication (what\n  changed), question (what that raises), answer (what you recommend).\n\n### 2. Speak in business outcomes and baselines\n- **Translate agent metrics into the KPI the executive owns**, against the baseline measured before\n  deployment: \"average handling time on long claims is down from 42 to 31 minutes\" rather than \"summary\n  ROUGE improved\" or \"task completion rate is up\".\n- **Why:** the executive's question is always \"is this worth it\", so every number should connect to value,\n  cost or risk.\n- **Show the arithmetic.** The translation is usually one line, and showing it makes the number checkable:\n\n```\nvalue per month   = volume × time saved per item × loaded cost per hour\nescaped errors    = volume × error rate × (1 − catch rate at review)\n```\n\nA per-item saving means little to an executive; \"about 220 adjuster-hours a month\" lets them compare it\nwith the cost of the system (model and tool spend per run included) and with their other options.\n\n### 3. Make uncertainty and error rates concrete\n- **Express quality per volume and with consequence:** \"about 3 in 100 drafts need a material\n  correction; all are caught at human review; zero reached customers this month\".\n- **Give ranges when the sample is small**, and say what would change the estimate.\n- **Why:** executives make risk decisions, so they need the size and containment of the failure, not just\n  the average.\n\n| Framing | What the executive can decide from it |\n|---|---|\n| \"97% accurate\" | Nothing |\n| \"3% error rate\" | Sounds small |\n| 3% of 20,000 drafts a month | 600 corrections |\n| Review catches 95% of them | About 30 a month reach customers |\n\nWhether 30 is acceptable is the executive's decision, and they cannot make it from \"97% accurate\".\n\n**Be explicit about the sample.** Zero failures in 50 cases is still consistent with a true failure rate\nas high as about 6%. The \"rule of three\" gives roughly 3/n as the upper 95% bound when no failures are\nseen, so say \"no failures yet in 50 cases\" rather than \"no failures\".\n\n### 4. Ask for a decision explicitly\n- **State** the decision, the options, your recommendation, the cost of each, and the date by which it must\n  be made.\n- **Why:** \"FYI\" items get read and forgotten, so an update without a clear ask cannot unblock you. For\n  agents, typical asks are widening autonomy, adding a tool, or funding a missing integration.\n\n### 5. No surprises: escalate early, pre-wire big messages\n- **Report risks while they are still yellow**, with a mitigation.\n- **Pre-wire difficult news.** Before a steering meeting, brief the sponsor one-to-one, *because*\n  executives react badly to learning something in front of peers that they could have known earlier.\n- **Agent incidents count.** A wrong write action or a leaked field reaches the sponsor from you first,\n  with what happened, the blast radius and the fix.\n\n### 6. Fix the format, the cadence and the status rules\n- **One page, stable structure:** headline, status with trend, KPI versus baseline and target, top risks,\n  decisions needed. It lets the reader compare week to week.\n- **Detail goes in an appendix** for those who want it: eval breakdowns, tool-call error rates, traces.\n\nDefine what each status colour means at the start, in terms the sponsor agreed, for example:\n\n| Colour | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Green | On track for the target date and KPI with no unmitigated risk |\n| Amber | At risk, mitigation in place, no decision needed yet |\n| Red | Will miss without a decision from you |\n\n- **Why define them:** an undefined colour is chosen by the author's optimism, so status drifts toward\n  green until it jumps to red. That is how watermelon reporting happens without anyone intending to\n  mislead.\n- **Amber with a mitigation is a normal, healthy state for a pilot**; reporting it is what earns belief in\n  your greens.\n\n### 7. Match the channel to the decision\n| Channel | Works when |\n|---|---|\n| Written pre-read | The reader needs to think before deciding |\n| Live discussion | Options must be negotiated |\n| Two-line message | A yes/no that is already pre-wired |\n\nSend pre-reads early enough to be read, and open the meeting with the decision, not a recap of the\ndocument.","example":"### Turning eval numbers into executive numbers\nThese three helpers produce every number in a typical agent steering update; the inputs are the\nillustrative figures used in this lesson.\n\n```python\ndef value_hours(volume, minutes_saved):\n    return volume * minutes_saved / 60\n\ndef escaped_errors(volume, error_rate, catch_rate):\n    return volume * error_rate * (1 - catch_rate)\n\ndef zero_failure_upper_bound(n, exact=False):\n    # rule of three: ~3/n is the 95% upper bound when 0 failures are seen in n\n    return 1 - 0.05 ** (1 / n) if exact else 3 / n\n\nprint(value_hours(1_200, 42 - 31))                   # 220.0 adjuster-hours a month\nprint(20_000 * 0.03)                                 # 600.0 corrections a month\nprint(round(escaped_errors(20_000, 0.03, 0.95)))     # 30 reach customers a month\nprint(f\"{zero_failure_upper_bound(50):.0%}\")             # 6%\nprint(f\"{zero_failure_upper_bound(50, exact=True):.1%}\") # 5.8%\n```\n\nMultiply `value_hours` by the loaded cost per hour to get the money figure for the value line.\n\n### From agent run logs to the update's quality and cost lines\nAgent telemetry carries more than accuracy: how many runs finished, how many escalated to a person, and\nwhat each run cost. This sketch turns a month of run records into the lines an executive reads. All\nfigures are illustrative.\n\n```python\nruns = 1_200                      # agent runs on long claims this month\ncompleted = 1_050                 # finished without handing back to a person\nescalated = runs - completed      # handed to an adjuster by design (low confidence, tool error)\nmaterial_errors = 36              # found at adjuster review in completed runs\nreached_claimant = 0\ncost_per_run = 0.40               # model plus tool spend, in dollars\n\nprint(f\"{completed / runs:.0%} of long claims handled end to end; \"\n      f\"{escalated} routed to adjusters by design\")          # 88%; 150 routed\nprint(f\"about {round(100 * material_errors / completed)} in 100 summaries needed a \"\n      f\"material correction; {reached_claimant} reached a claimant\")   # about 3 in 100\nprint(f\"run cost about ${runs * cost_per_run:,.0f} a month\")  # about $480\n# Put the escalations in the update as containment, not failure:\n# they are the agent declining work it should not do alone.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: rewriting a steering update\nA hypothetical engineer drafts a steering update for the VP of Operations about a claims-summary agent\npilot.\n\n**Before (chronological, technical):**\n\n> \"This sprint we migrated to a new embedding model, rebuilt the index, fixed a PDF parsing bug affecting\n> scanned files and ran the eval suite. Recall@10 went from 0.71 to 0.83. We are also seeing some issues\n> with handwritten notes. Usage is around 60%. Next sprint we will look at OCR options.\"\n\nThe VP cannot tell whether the pilot is on track, what the numbers mean or what is being asked.\n\n**After (pyramid structure):**\n\n| Line | Content |\n|---|---|\n| **Bottom line** | The pilot is on track to hit its target, and I need a decision on OCR budget by 3 November. |\n| **Value** | Handling time on long claims is down from 42 to 31 minutes against a 30-minute target, across the 40 adjusters in the pilot. At about 1,200 long claims a month that is roughly 220 adjuster-hours a month (1,200 × 11 minutes). |\n| **Quality** | About 3 in 100 summaries need a material correction; all were caught at adjuster review and none reached a claimant. |\n| **Risk** | About one file in eight contains handwritten notes, which the agent cannot read today, so those files fall back to manual review. |\n| **Decision** | Fund a specialist OCR service (estimated cost in the appendix) to extend coverage to those files, or accept manual handling for them. I recommend funding it, because it is the largest remaining gap to the target. |\n\nThe technical details (embedding model, recall@10, tool-call errors) move to an appendix. (All figures\nare illustrative.)","patterns":["**Fixed one-page template with agreed colour rules** — at kickoff, agree the green, amber and red definitions with the sponsor and write them at the bottom of every update. Monitor how often a red appears without a preceding amber; each one is a reporting failure.","**Metric translation script over agent telemetry** — keep one sheet or notebook that turns eval results and run logs (completions, escalations, corrections, cost per run) into the update's business numbers using the value and escaped-error formulas, rerun for every update. Monitor its inputs (volume, catch rate at review) for drift, since they move the headline numbers.","**Pre-wire before every steering meeting with bad news** — a one-to-one with the sponsor ahead of the meeting, sharing the bottom line, the options and your recommendation. Monitor whether steering meetings end with a decision rather than a request for more analysis.","**Agent incident notes within a day** — when the agent takes a wrong action or exposes data, send the sponsor a short note: what happened, how many cases, whether it was contained, the fix and the eval case added. Monitor time from detection to sponsor note.","**Decision log with due dates** — each ask in an update carries options, a recommendation, costs and a decision date, tracked in a log the sponsor can see. Monitor decisions arriving after their date.","**Small-sample wording rule** — when an eval or pilot has seen no failures, report \"no failures yet in n cases\" plus the rule-of-three bound of about 3/n. Monitor for absolute claims (\"no errors\") in drafts before they go out."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent projects are funded on business outcomes, but your dashboards show\n  task completion, tool-call success, escalation rate and cost per run. If you cannot translate those into\n  hours saved and errors contained, the sponsor cannot defend the budget.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Eval results, run logs and token or cost telemetry feed a small\n  translation script; the steering update, go/no-go memo and quarterly value review are its outputs.\n- **What you control.** The formulas (value per month, escaped errors), how you state small-sample\n  results, the colour rules you agree with the sponsor, and how early you report an agent failure.\n- **What you can skip.** Presentation craft and slide design. BLUF, one value number with its arithmetic\n  and an honest error statement do most of the work.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Weekly steering update for an agent pilot.** Eval and run telemetry become one value number, one\n  contained-error statement and one ask.\n- **Asking to widen an agent's autonomy or tool scope.** A decision memo with options, error rates in\n  volume terms and the cost of each option.\n- **Reporting an agent incident.** A same-day note with blast radius, containment, fix and the new eval\n  case keeps the sponsor's trust.\n- **Quarterly value review.** Hours saved and cost per run against the pre-deployment baseline justify the\n  next phase of funding.","mini_project":"**An auto-generated one-page update from agent telemetry (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** produce a BLUF steering update directly from an agent's eval results and run logs.\n- **Steps:** collect (or simulate) a month of runs with completion, escalation, corrections found at\n  review and cost per run; record a manual baseline time per task; write a script that computes value\n  hours, escaped errors, the rule-of-three bound for any zero-failure slice, and run cost; render a\n  one-page update with bottom line, value, quality, risk and decision lines, and an appendix of technical\n  metrics.\n- **Done when:** the script regenerates the page from new data in one command, every number shows its\n  arithmetic, and a non-technical reader can restate the status and the ask after one read."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    E[\"Agent evals and run telemetry\"] --> T[\"Translate to KPI vs baseline\"]\n    T --> Q[\"Error rate as volume and containment\"]\n    Q --> B[\"Bottom line and ask first\"]\n    B --> N{\"Bad news or red?\"}\n    N -->|\"yes\"| P[\"Pre-wire sponsor one-to-one\"]\n    N -->|\"no\"| C{\"Decision type\"}\n    P --> C\n    C -->|\"needs thought\"| R[\"Written pre-read\"]\n    C -->|\"negotiate options\"| L[\"Live discussion\"]\n    C -->|\"pre-wired yes or no\"| M[\"Two-line message\"]","caption":"Read top-down as the order of an update; the \"yes\" branch is the pre-wire path for bad news, and the last\nsplit picks the channel by the kind of decision."},"interview":[{"q":"What is BLUF, and why does it matter more for executives than for peers?","a":"- **Definition:** bottom line up front, the conclusion and any ask in the first two lines, with supporting\n  arguments and evidence below (the Pyramid Principle).\n- **Why executives:** they see the project for minutes between meetings and may stop reading at any point,\n  so the conclusion must survive a partial read.\n- **Peers** still get the conclusion first, but with the technical depth included rather than moved to an\n  appendix."},{"q":"Explain a recall@10 improvement from 0.71 to 0.83 in your agent's retrieval step to a VP of Operations.","a":"- **Do not lead with recall.** Lead with the KPI it moved, such as handling time on long claims against\n  the pre-deployment baseline.\n- **Give one value number with its arithmetic**, for example volume times minutes saved, so it can be\n  checked and compared with the system's cost.\n- **Close with the main risk and any decision needed**; recall@10 goes to the appendix."},{"q":"Your agent's eval saw zero failures in 50 cases. What do you tell the sponsor?","a":"- **\"No failures yet in 50 cases\", not \"no failures\".** By the rule of three, the true failure rate could\n  still be as high as about 3/n, here roughly 6%, at 95% confidence.\n- **Translate it to volume:** at production volume, a few percent could mean many cases a month, which is\n  the number the sponsor needs for a risk decision.\n- **Say what would tighten the estimate**, such as a larger eval set before expansion."},{"q":"Your agent made a wrong write to a customer record last night. How do you tell the sponsor?","a":"- **Before anyone else does**, one-to-one or a short written note the same day: what happened, how many\n  records, whether any customer saw it, and whether it is contained.\n- **Then cause and fix:** the trace that shows why, the guard added (approval gate, argument check,\n  narrower tool scope) and the eval case that now covers it.\n- **Close with any decision:** for example pausing write actions until the fix ships, with the cost of\n  pausing."},{"q":"Your weekly status has been green for six weeks and is about to go red. What went wrong, and what now?","a":"- **Likely cause:** undefined colour rules, so optimism chose green and ambers were never reported\n  (watermelon reporting).\n- **Now:** pre-wire the sponsor one-to-one, lead with the bottom line, the gap in business terms, the\n  options with costs and a decision date.\n- **Prevent recurrence:** agree written green, amber and red definitions with the sponsor and treat amber\n  with a mitigation as normal."},{"q":"When would you not send a written pre-read?","a":"- **When options must be negotiated live**, because a document cannot trade off positions between\n  executives in real time.\n- **When it is a pre-wired yes or no**, where a two-line message is enough and a document adds delay.\n- **Otherwise** send it early enough to be read, and open the meeting with the decision."}],"tradeoffs":["**Conclusion first vs unfinished analysis.** Leading with the conclusion saves executive time but needs the analysis finished; when it is not, say what you know and when you will know more.","**Business metrics vs technical nuance.** Translation makes value visible but hides nuance; keep an appendix for technical reviewers who need it.","**Early risk reporting vs premature intervention.** It builds trust but may trigger intervention; pair each risk with a mitigation and an owner.","**Fixed one-page format vs flexibility.** It enables week-to-week comparison but can feel rigid when a crisis needs a dedicated note.","**Pre-wiring vs an extra meeting.** It avoids public surprises but adds a meeting; skip it only when the news is routine."],"pitfalls":["**Chronological reporting that buries the conclusion.** Symptom: executives asking \"so is this on track?\" after every update.","**Model or agent metrics without a business translation.** Revealed when steering discussions never touch value.","**Watermelon status (green outside, red inside).** It hides problems until they explode, destroying trust in later updates.","**Updates without an explicit ask.** Blockers wait weeks because nobody realized a decision was needed.","**A single accuracy number without volume, consequence or uncertainty.** Leads to over-confidence or panic.","**Undefined status colours.** Shows up as a project reported green for weeks that turns red in a single update.","**Claiming \"no errors\" from a small sample.** Revealed when the first production failures arrive and the executive feels misled.","**Reporting escalations as failures.** Symptom: the sponsor pushes to cut hand-offs, raising autonomy where the agent is weakest. Fix: present by-design escalations as containment, with the rate per case type."],"when":"- **Use for** any communication with someone who decides about funding, scope or risk but does not do the\n  work: steering updates, escalations, go/no-go recommendations, autonomy or tool-scope changes and\n  quarterly value reviews.\n- **With peers who need detail**, still lead with the conclusion but include the technical depth.\n- **Measure** whether decisions arrive on time, whether executives can restate the status and the ask\n  after reading (ask the sponsor to play it back), and whether their understanding of status matches\n  reality: a red never preceded by an amber is a reporting failure, not just a project one.\n- **Interviews:** you may be asked to explain a technical result to an executive; lead with the outcome and\n  the ask, give one number tied to value, and state the main risk.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns eval and telemetry output into the few business-facing numbers an update needs, with honest error rates and ranges.","architect":"Frames trade-offs (cost, risk, time) as explicit options with a recommendation so executives can decide quickly.","fde":"Writes the customer's steering updates, pre-wires the sponsor before difficult meetings, and escalates risks while they are still recoverable."},"check":{"q":"Your pilot's accuracy is below the target agreed with the customer's executive, but a fix is likely. How do you communicate this?","a":"Early and directly, before the steering meeting, in a one-to-one with the sponsor. I would lead with the bottom line: we are below target, here is the gap in business terms (what share of cases need correction and whether any reached customers), here is the cause, here is the fix with a realistic date and its uncertainty, and here is the decision I need, such as extending the pilot by three weeks or narrowing scope to the case types already above target. I would not bury it or polish it green, because finding out later costs far more trust than the gap itself."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should come first in an executive status update?","options":["A chronological list of work completed since the last update","The technical metrics with their definitions","The conclusion and any decision you need from the reader","The methodology used to evaluate the system"],"answer":2,"explanation":"BLUF and the Pyramid Principle put the governing point first because executives may stop reading at any time. Chronology and methodology belong lower or in an appendix."},{"q":"Which phrasing best communicates AI quality to an executive?","options":["\"Our F1 score improved by 0.12 this sprint\"","\"The model is highly accurate\"","\"About 3 in 100 drafts need correction; all were caught at review and none reached customers\"","\"The model hallucinates sometimes, as all LLMs do\""],"answer":2,"explanation":"Executives make risk decisions, so they need the rate in volume terms plus whether errors are contained. Raw metrics, vague praise or fatalism give them nothing to decide with."},{"q":"Why brief the sponsor one-to-one before presenting bad news at a steering meeting?","options":["To get permission to leave the bad news out of the meeting","So the sponsor is not surprised in front of peers and can help shape the response","Because steering committees cannot discuss risks formally","To shorten the steering meeting agenda"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pre-wiring keeps trust and turns the sponsor into an ally for the proposed fix. Hiding the news would violate the no-surprises rule that makes executive communication credible."}]},{"key":"adoption/usage-analytics","sources":["Rodden, Hutchinson, Fu, 2010 — Measuring the User Experience on a Large Scale (HEART framework)","Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments"],"why":"- **A healthy system can still go unused.** A deployed assistant can be technically fine while nobody\n  relies on it; only behavioural events against a defined eligible population show that.\n- **Request and login counts mislead.** Requests rise when one enthusiastic team uses the tool heavily\n  while forty others ignore it, or when users regenerate the same answer five times because the first\n  four were wrong. Logins count people who opened the page, not people who got anything done.\n- **Raw counts have no denominator.** 2,000 weekly users is a success among 2,500 eligible agents and a\n  failure among 40,000.\n- **AI output is probabilistic, so the signal comes after the response.** Did the user send the draft\n  unchanged, rewrite half of it, copy one paragraph, or close the panel? These downstream actions are the\n  cheapest large-scale quality signal you will get; thumbs buttons are clicked by a small, unrepresentative\n  fraction, mostly when annoyed.\n- **Reading prompt logs or traces is not a substitute.** Traces are keyed by request, not by user journey,\n  and copying raw prompts into an analytics warehouse spreads sensitive customer content to a system with\n  broad access.","how":"### 1. Define the eligible population and the unit of value\n- **Eligible population:** who *could* use the feature (licensed seats, agents in the pilot queues).\n- **Unit of work:** a ticket answered, a contract reviewed, a query resolved.\n- **Every rate needs both.** Adoption is active users ÷ eligible users, not active users alone. Depth is\n  units of work done with AI ÷ units of work done in total.\n\n### 2. Design an event schema around outcomes, not clicks\nA small, stable vocabulary beats hundreds of ad-hoc events.\n\n| Event | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **exposure** | The feature was shown or offered (needed for any funnel denominator) |\n| **invocation** | The user deliberately asked for AI help |\n| **response** | The system returned output, carrying the `request_id` that joins to the trace |\n| **disposition** | Accepted as-is, accepted after edit (with an edit-size measure), regenerated, discarded, escalated to a human |\n| **explicit feedback** | Thumbs, ratings, free-text reasons |\n\n- **Common fields on every event:** pseudonymous user ID, account or tenant, feature name, prompt/model\n  version and a timestamp.\n- **`request_id` is the design choice that matters most.** It lets you go from \"acceptance dropped on\n  Tuesday\" to the exact traces, prompt version and retrieved documents behind the dropped requests,\n  *without* storing content in the analytics store.\n\n### Agent runs add task-level events\nFor an agent that plans and calls tools, one model response is not the unit of work; the run is.\n\n| Event | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **run_started / run_finished** | One delegated task, with a run ID that joins to the trace, plus steps and tool calls used |\n| **action_proposed** | The agent asks to take a consequential action (send, refund, write a record) |\n| **action_decision** | Approved as proposed, edited before approval, or rejected at the approval gate |\n| **human_takeover** | The user stopped the run and did the task by hand |\n\n- **Run outcome is the agent's disposition.** Completed unaided, completed after edits, or taken over are\n  the agent equivalents of sent-as-is, edited and discarded.\n- **Normalise by completed tasks, not calls.** Tool calls and steps per completed task expose cost and\n  flailing that raw request counts hide, because a looping agent inflates usage.\n\n### Edit size, defined precisely\n- **Why define it:** \"edited\" alone lumps a fixed typo together with a full rewrite.\n- **Compute it in the client,** where the draft and the sent text are both already present, for example as\n  `1 − similarity(draft, sent)` with a character- or token-level diff ratio. Emit only the number.\n- **Bucket it** (0, under 10%, 10–50%, over 50%) so the dashboard shows the shape of the distribution,\n  because the mean hides the difference between \"most drafts need a light touch\" and \"half are fine,\n  half are rewritten from scratch\".\n\n### 3. Prefer implicit signals, calibrate them with explicit ones\n- **Coverage:** disposition events are emitted for every response, so they avoid the self-selection bias\n  of explicit ratings.\n- **Not bias-free:** \"abandoned\" has to be inferred from a timeout or a closed panel, and users copy text\n  out in ways the UI may not see.\n- **Noisy:** a user may accept a mediocre draft because fixing it is quicker than writing from scratch.\n- **So treat them as proxies.** \"Accepted unchanged\", \"edited by more than half\" and \"regenerated\" are\n  proxies. Periodically check them against human-labelled samples, so you know what a 60% acceptance rate\n  means for *this* workflow.\n\n### 4. Build the standard views\n| View | Definition | What it tells you |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Funnel** | eligible → exposed → tried once → used in 3+ distinct weeks | Whether the problem is awareness, first experience or ongoing value |\n| **Cohort retention** | of users who first used it in week *w*, the share still using it in week *w+n* | Flattening above zero is a habit; decay toward zero is a novelty curve |\n| **Stickiness and depth** | weekly-active ÷ monthly-active, and AI-assisted share of all work items | How habitual and how central the tool is |\n| **Breadth** | active users by team, region or role | Where adoption is lumpy, which it usually is |\n| **Quality proxies over time** | acceptance, edit size and regeneration rate, sliced by prompt version | Whether a release helped or hurt |\n\n- **Checklist:** frameworks such as Google's HEART (Happiness, Engagement, Adoption, Retention, Task\n  success) are a useful check that the views cover the whole journey.\n\n### 5. Check noise and mix before reading a change\n- **Every rate moves by chance,** because it is a proportion over a finite count.\n- **Standard error of one rate** *p* over *n* events: about `sqrt(p(1−p)/n)`.\n- **Standard error of a difference** between two periods or prompt versions: about\n  `sqrt(p₁(1−p₁)/n₁ + p₂(1−p₂)/n₂)`.\n- **Rule of thumb:** treat a move smaller than roughly two standard errors as unproven. Small pilots make\n  this bite: at 50 drafts a week, acceptance can swing ten points with nothing changed.\n- **Mix:** an aggregate rate is a weighted average over segments, so it moves when the weights move even\n  if no segment changes. If a new team with naturally harder tickets joins in the week a prompt ships,\n  overall acceptance falls and the prompt gets blamed.\n- **Slice by team or cohort first.** A real regression shows up *within* segments; a mix shift shows flat\n  segments and a moving total.\n\n### 6. Keep it clean and lawful\n- **Exclude internal, test and service accounts,** or the vendor team's own testing inflates week one.\n- **Hash or tokenize user IDs.**\n- **Keep prompt and response text in the access-controlled tracing store,** not in analytics.\n- **Agree retention periods and works-council or employee-monitoring constraints** with the customer\n  *before* instrumenting.\n- **Aggregate to team level by default,** because in many organisations per-employee productivity\n  tracking is a legal and political issue.","example":"### Agent scenario: did agent v4 make users take over more often?\nA support agent drafts replies, looks up orders and proposes refunds behind an approval gate. Weekly\naggregates come from `run_finished` events only, so no prompt or tool content is involved. Numbers are\nillustrative.\n\n```python\nfrom math import sqrt\n\n# Weekly aggregates per agent version from run_finished events (no prompt or tool content).\n# completed = finished unaided, edited = finished after user edits, taken_over = user did it by hand.\nweeks = {\n    \"v3\": {\"runs\": 600, \"completed\": 390, \"edited\": 120, \"taken_over\": 90, \"tool_calls\": 4_200},\n    \"v4\": {\"runs\": 600, \"completed\": 330, \"edited\": 132, \"taken_over\": 138, \"tool_calls\": 5_700},\n}\n\ndef diff_se(p1, n1, p2, n2):\n    return sqrt(p1 * (1 - p1) / n1 + p2 * (1 - p2) / n2)\n\nfor v, w in weeks.items():\n    done = w[\"completed\"] + w[\"edited\"]\n    print(v, f\"takeover={w['taken_over'] / w['runs']:.2f}\",\n          f\"tool_calls_per_completed_task={w['tool_calls'] / done:.1f}\")\n\np3 = weeks[\"v3\"][\"taken_over\"] / weeks[\"v3\"][\"runs\"]\np4 = weeks[\"v4\"][\"taken_over\"] / weeks[\"v4\"][\"runs\"]\nse = diff_se(p3, 600, p4, 600)\nprint(f\"diff={p4 - p3:.2f} se={se:.3f} z={(p4 - p3) / se:.1f}\")\n# v3 takeover=0.15 tool_calls_per_completed_task=8.2\n# v4 takeover=0.23 tool_calls_per_completed_task=12.3\n# diff=0.08 se=0.023 z=3.6\n```\n\n| Reading | Conclusion |\n|---|---|\n| Takeover 0.15 → 0.23 on 600 runs each | about 3.6 standard errors: not noise |\n| Tool calls per completed task 8.2 → 12.3 | a hint, not proof, that the agent loops or retries tools before users give up |\n| Next step | slice by team to rule out mix, then pull taken-over runs by run ID and read their trajectories |\n\n### Cohort retention and draft disposition in SQL\nTwo Postgres queries over an `ai_events` table for a support-reply drafting assistant. The first computes\nweekly cohort retention on a deliberate action (`draft_requested`), excluding internal users; the second\ntracks what agents did with the drafts.\n\n```sql\nWITH active AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT e.user_id, date_trunc('week', e.ts)::date AS wk\n  FROM ai_events e JOIN users u ON u.user_id = e.user_id\n  WHERE e.feature = 'draft_reply'\n    AND e.event_type = 'draft_requested'      -- an intentional action, not an impression\n    AND NOT u.is_internal\n),\ncohort AS (SELECT user_id, min(wk) AS cohort_wk FROM active GROUP BY user_id),\nsize   AS (SELECT cohort_wk, count(*) AS n FROM cohort GROUP BY cohort_wk)\nSELECT c.cohort_wk,\n       (a.wk - c.cohort_wk) / 7                              AS weeks_since_first_use,\n       count(DISTINCT a.user_id)                             AS active_users,\n       round(count(DISTINCT a.user_id)::numeric / max(s.n), 3) AS retention\nFROM cohort c\nJOIN active a ON a.user_id = c.user_id\nJOIN size s   ON s.cohort_wk = c.cohort_wk\nGROUP BY c.cohort_wk, weeks_since_first_use\nORDER BY c.cohort_wk, weeks_since_first_use;\n\n-- Disposition of drafts per week, sliced by prompt version.\nSELECT date_trunc('week', ts)::date AS wk, prompt_version,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_shown')          AS shown,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_sent_unchanged') AS sent_as_is,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_sent_edited')    AS sent_edited,\n       count(*) FILTER (WHERE event_type = 'draft_regenerated')    AS regenerated\nFROM ai_events\nWHERE feature = 'draft_reply'\nGROUP BY 1, 2 ORDER BY 1, 2;\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: prompt v7 regression or mix shift?\nReading the two queries together is the point. Illustrative numbers:\n\n| Step | Finding |\n|---|---|\n| Week-4 retention of the first cohort | 0.35 |\n| `sent_as_is / shown` before vs after prompt v7 | fell from 0.42 to 0.30, about 500 drafts on each version |\n| Standard error of the difference | `sqrt(0.42·0.58/500 + 0.30·0.70/500) ≈ 0.03` |\n| Size of the move | a 12-point drop is roughly four standard errors: not noise |\n| Same drop with 50 drafts per version | error about 0.095, so the drop would prove nothing |\n| Slice by team | every team fell, and regenerations rose |\n\n- **If only the newly onboarded billing team were low** and the others flat, it would be mix.\n- **Because every team fell,** it is a quality regression to chase in the traces by `request_id`, not an\n  adoption problem.\n\n### Client-side edit size and a significance check in Python\nEdit size is computed where both texts exist and only the bucket is emitted; the same file checks\nwhether a change in acceptance is bigger than noise.\n\n```python\nfrom difflib import SequenceMatcher\nfrom math import sqrt\n\ndef edit_size(draft, sent):\n    return 1 - SequenceMatcher(None, draft, sent).ratio()   # 0 = unchanged, 1 = rewritten\n\ndef bucket(x):\n    if x == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n    if x < 0.10:\n        return \"under 10%\"\n    if x <= 0.50:\n        return \"10-50%\"\n    return \"over 50%\"\n\ndef diff_se(p1, n1, p2, n2):\n    return sqrt(p1 * (1 - p1) / n1 + p2 * (1 - p2) / n2)\n\ndraft = \"Thanks for reaching out. Your refund was issued today.\"\nsent = \"Thanks for reaching out! Your refund was issued today and should arrive in 3-5 days.\"\nx = edit_size(draft, sent)\nprint(round(x, 2), bucket(x))             # 0.23 10-50%  (emit the bucket, never the text)\n\nfor n in (500, 50):                       # drafts per prompt version\n    se = diff_se(0.42, n, 0.30, n)\n    z = (0.42 - 0.30) / se\n    print(n, round(se, 3), round(z, 1), \"real\" if abs(z) >= 2 else \"unproven\")\n# 500 0.03 4.0 real\n# 50 0.095 1.3 unproven\n```","patterns":["**Versioned agent event contract** — when several agents, features or teams emit events, keep the run, action-decision, takeover and disposition vocabulary in one schema file (for example JSON Schema) validated in the collector; quarantine unknown event types instead of silently accepting them. Monitor the rejected-event rate after every client release.","**Approval-gate analytics** — for agents that propose consequential actions, emit a decision event for every proposal (approved, edited, rejected) tagged with the tool name and agent version. Monitor the rejection rate per tool; one tool with a rising rejection rate points to a bad argument or a bad plan, not a bad model.","**Join key, not content** — whenever prompts may hold customer data, put `request_id` and `prompt_version` on every analytics event and keep text only in the tracing store. Monitor the share of response events whose `request_id` has a matching trace; a falling join rate means broken instrumentation.","**Exclusion at ingestion** — from day one of a pilot, flag internal, test and service accounts (an `is_internal` column on the users table, as in the SQL above) and filter in one shared view. Monitor the share of events from flagged accounts, so vendor testing never reaches the sponsor dashboard.","**Noise band on every rate** — on any dashboard leadership reads, show the event count and a two-standard- error band next to each rate, and grey out slices below a minimum *n*. Monitor *n* per slice per week.","**Staged prompt or agent-version rollout with a comparison** — ship a new prompt, tool set or agent version to a fraction of users or teams first and compare acceptance, edit-size buckets, regeneration and takeover rates against the old version by segment (see A/B testing). Monitor the per-segment difference, not just the total.","**Proxy calibration sampling** — on a fixed cadence, have reviewers label a random sample of responses or agent runs per disposition bucket, reading the trajectory, not just the final answer. Monitor agreement between \"accepted unchanged\" and \"labelled good\" over time."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent can finish every run cleanly in its traces while users quietly\n  take over a quarter of the tasks or reject its proposed actions. Disposition events are the cheapest\n  large-scale signal of whether people trust the agent's work.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The agent UI and approval gate emit events, an event collector and\n  warehouse store them, and the tracing store keeps prompts, tool calls and results. A `request_id` or run\n  ID is the only bridge between the two.\n- **What you control.** A task-level event vocabulary (runs, proposed actions, approvals, takeovers), the\n  eligible population and unit of work, client-side edit size, noise bands, segment slicing, and which\n  content never leaves the tracing store.\n- **What you can skip.** Heavy experimentation theory. The two standard-error formulas below are enough to\n  tell a real agent regression from noise in a pilot.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Agent pilots with a sponsor review.** Takeover rate, breadth by team and week-4 retention show whether\n  the agent is trusted, not just whether it ran.\n- **Releasing a new agent version.** Per-segment takeover and approval rates with noise bands decide\n  whether v4 is a regression or a mix shift.\n- **Approval-gated actions.** Rejection rate per tool shows which tool's arguments or plans users do not\n  trust, so the fix lands on that tool.\n- **Cost control.** Tool calls and steps per completed task catch looping agents that look like rising\n  adoption on a volume chart.","mini_project":"**Instrument a toy agent with run-level analytics (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** turn agent runs into disposition rates you can compare across two agent versions honestly.\n- **Steps:** build a small tool-calling agent with two tools and an approval step; emit `run_finished`,\n  action-decision and takeover events (IDs, versions, counts only) to a local SQLite table; simulate or\n  label 100+ runs for two prompt versions; compute takeover rate, approval rate and tool calls per\n  completed task, each with a standard error.\n- **Done when:** you have a per-version table with two-standard-error bands, a verdict of \"real\" or\n  \"unproven\" for the takeover difference, and no prompt or tool content stored in the events table."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    UI[\"Agent UI and approval gate\"] -->|\"run outcome, action decision, takeover\"| EV[\"Event collector\"]\n    UI -->|\"prompts, tool calls, results\"| TR[(\"Tracing store, access-controlled\")]\n    EV -->|\"drop internal, hash user IDs\"| WH[(\"Analytics warehouse\")]\n    WH --> V[\"Funnel, cohorts, breadth, quality proxies\"]\n    V --> N{\"Move over 2 standard errors?\"}\n    N -->|\"no\"| W[\"Treat as noise\"]\n    N -->|\"yes\"| S{\"Within segments too?\"}\n    S -->|\"no\"| MX[\"Mix shift\"]\n    S -->|\"yes\"| RQ[\"Drill into traces by request_id\"]\n    RQ -.-> TR\n    L[\"Human-labelled samples\"] -.->|\"calibrate proxies\"| V","caption":"Agent behaviour events and agent content take separate paths; the `request_id` (run ID) is the only\nbridge, and labelled samples of runs keep the proxies honest."},"interview":[{"q":"How is usage analytics different from tracing, and why do you need both?","a":"- **Traces record what the system did** per request: prompt, retrieved documents, model calls, latency.\n  Analytics records what people did with the output, keyed by user and journey.\n- **You need both because they answer different questions.** A healthy trace dashboard cannot tell you\n  that forty teams never opened the feature, and an analytics dashboard cannot tell you which retrieved\n  document caused a bad draft.\n- **Join them by `request_id`** so content stays in the access-controlled tracing store."},{"q":"Acceptance fell eight points the week a new prompt shipped. How do you work out whether the prompt is to blame?","a":"- **Check noise first:** compute the standard error of the difference from the event counts; under about\n  two standard errors, the move is unproven.\n- **Then check mix:** slice by team and cohort. Flat segments with a moving total means a population\n  change, such as a new team onboarding, not a prompt regression.\n- **If segments fell too,** look at regeneration and edit-size buckets, then drill into a sample of\n  affected traces by `request_id` and compare prompt versions."},{"q":"When would you not collect per-user analytics?","a":"- **When works councils, regulators or the customer's own policy object** to per-employee monitoring;\n  aggregate to team level and accept that individual cohort analysis is lost.\n- **When the pilot is too small** for per-user rates to mean anything; a handful of users produces noise\n  that invites the wrong conclusions about individuals.\n- **The cost:** you lose individual funnels and retention, so lean harder on breadth by team and on\n  disposition rates."},{"q":"How many drafts do you need before you trust a five-point change in acceptance?","a":"- **Use the difference formula:** with acceptance near 0.4, each version contributes about 0.24/*n* to the\n  variance, so the standard error is about sqrt(0.48/*n*).\n- **Two standard errors at five points** requires sqrt(0.48/*n*) ≤ 0.025, so roughly 770 drafts per\n  version.\n- **At 50 drafts a week,** that is months of data, so in small pilots report direction and wait, or use\n  human-labelled samples instead."},{"q":"Your agent's traces look healthy, but users take over about a quarter of runs. What analytics do you add?","a":"- **Run-level dispositions:** a `run_finished` event with outcome (completed, completed after edits, taken\n  over), steps and tool calls, carrying the run ID so each takeover joins to its trajectory.\n- **Approval-gate decisions per tool,** to see whether takeovers follow rejected actions on one tool.\n- **Then slice and read:** check noise and mix by team, and read a sample of taken-over trajectories to\n  name the failure modes before changing prompts."},{"q":"Design usage analytics for an agent that issues refunds behind an approval gate.","a":"- **Events:** exposure, invocation, run started and finished, action proposed, action decision (approved,\n  edited with an edit-size bucket, rejected), human takeover and explicit feedback, each with pseudonymous\n  user ID, tenant, agent and prompt version, and timestamp.\n- **Rates with denominators:** approvals ÷ proposals, takeovers ÷ runs, refunds completed ÷ eligible\n  tickets, each with a two-standard-error band and *n*.\n- **Privacy:** the refund reason text and customer data stay in traces; analytics holds IDs, versions,\n  tool names and outcomes only."}],"tradeoffs":["**Rich disposition events vs UI work.** They give unbiased coverage of every response, but need front-end work in the customer's UI; when you cannot touch the UI, you fall back to sparse explicit feedback.","**Per-user granularity vs monitoring concerns.** It enables cohort and funnel analysis, but raises employee-monitoring concerns; where works councils or regulators object, team-level aggregates are the right default.","**Small fixed vocabulary vs nuance.** It keeps dashboards comparable across releases, at the cost of missing feature-specific nuance; add events only when a decision depends on them.","**Content alongside events vs data spread.** It makes ad-hoc analysis easy but copies sensitive data widely; keep content in the tracing store and join by `request_id` unless the data is non-sensitive.","**Run-level vs step-level events.** Run outcomes keep dashboards small and readable, but hide which step or tool failed; emit step detail to traces and only summary counts (steps, tool calls) to analytics, unless a decision needs per-tool rates."],"pitfalls":["**Counting requests instead of users and work items.** Symptom: a rising usage chart while most eligible teams show zero activity in a breadth view. Fix: normalise by eligible users and work items.","**Reporting adoption without a denominator.** Leadership celebrates \"1,200 users\" until someone asks how many were licensed and the answer is 9,000.","**Leaving internal and test accounts in.** Week-one usage looks spectacular and then \"collapses\" when the vendor team stops testing.","**Treating thumbs-down rate as quality.** It moves with UI placement and annoyance, so it disagrees with acceptance and edit-size trends and with human review.","**Blaming a prompt for a mix shift.** Overall acceptance drops in the week a new team onboards, yet every segment's rate is flat when sliced.","**Turning acceptance rate into a target.** Drafts get shorter and blander, acceptance climbs, and downstream outcomes such as reopen rate or customer satisfaction slip.","**Logging raw prompts into the product analytics tool.** Discovered at a security review, after customer data has spread to a system with broad access.","**Counting agent tool calls as adoption.** Usage \"doubles\" after a release because the agent now loops through retries, while completed tasks are flat. Fix: report tool calls per completed task and takeover rate next to any volume number."],"when":"- **Instrument before the pilot starts,** not after someone asks for numbers. You cannot reconstruct\n  disposition events afterwards, and the first weeks are when the funnel is most informative.\n- **Use it to separate three diagnoses:** \"the system is wrong\", \"people are not using it\" and \"people use\n  it but it doesn't help\". They lead to engineering, training and scoping work respectively.\n- **Minimal dashboard:** weekly active ÷ eligible, AI-assisted share of work items, week-4 cohort retention,\n  and accepted-unchanged, edit-size and regeneration rates sliced by prompt version and team. For an\n  agent, add takeover rate, approval-gate rejection rate and tool calls per completed task.\n- **Avoid when** it is standing in for outcome measurement: high usage proves engagement, not value (see\n  adoption and value-realization measurement).\n- **Interviews:** expect to design the event schema for an AI feature and say which implicit signals you\n  would trust and why.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Emits disposition events with a `request_id` that joins to traces, versions every prompt in the event payload, and writes the cohort and funnel queries.","architect":"Decides where content lives versus where behavioural events live, sets the pseudonymisation and retention policy, and standardises the event vocabulary across features.","fde":"Negotiates what may be tracked inside the customer's environment, gets UI hooks into their tools, and reads the breadth view to find the teams that need help."},"check":{"q":"A customer's AI drafting tool shows weekly requests up 40% month over month, and the sponsor wants\nto declare success. What would you check before agreeing, and why?","a":"I would check whether the growth is broad or concentrated, and whether it reflects value or\nretries. First, normalise by eligible users and look at breadth: are more teams active, or is one team\nusing it harder? Second, check requests per completed work item and the regeneration rate. A jump in\nretries after a prompt or model change inflates requests while quality falls. Third, check cohort\nretention, to see whether new users stick or decay after a novelty week. Fourth, check disposition:\nsent-as-is and edit-size trends. Only broad, retained usage with stable or improving acceptance supports\nthe claim. Even then it shows engagement, and value still needs an outcome comparison."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are disposition events (accepted, edited, regenerated, discarded) usually preferred over thumbs ratings as the main quality signal?","options":["They are more accurate than human judgement of output quality","They are recorded for every response, so they avoid the self-selection bias of the few users who click ratings","They do not require any changes to the user interface","They can be computed from the model's log-probabilities alone"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Implicit dispositions cover every response. Ratings come from a small, skewed subset, often annoyed users. Dispositions are still noisy proxies and need calibrating against labelled samples, and they do require UI instrumentation."},{"q":"What is the main reason to carry a `request_id` on analytics events instead of copying prompt and response text into the analytics store?","options":["Analytics tools cannot store long text fields","It makes the cohort retention query run faster","It lets you drill from a behavioural anomaly to the exact trace while keeping sensitive content in the access-controlled tracing store","It is required by the OpenTelemetry specification"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The join key gives you both views without duplicating customer content into a widely accessible system. Speed and storage limits are not the reason, and no telemetry standard mandates this specific design."},{"q":"A cohort chart shows each weekly cohort's usage falling steadily toward zero by week six. What does this most likely indicate?","options":["A logging bug that drops events from older users","A novelty effect: people try the feature but do not find enough ongoing value to form a habit","Healthy adoption, because new cohorts keep arriving each week","That the eligible population was defined too broadly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Retention that decays toward zero instead of flattening is the classic novelty curve. New cohorts arriving can hide it in a total-users chart, which is exactly why cohort views exist."}]},{"key":"adoption/adoption-and-value-realization-measurement","sources":["Kohavi, Tang, Xu, 2020 — Trustworthy Online Controlled Experiments","Angrist, Pischke, 2009 — Mostly Harmless Econometrics"],"why":"- **Every deployment is sold on a projection.** An ROI model says something like \"25% faster claims\n  handling across 200 adjusters\". Ninety days later someone has to say how much of that showed up.\n- **Adoption gets reported as value.** \"80% of adjusters used it this month\" says nothing about whether\n  claims got faster. The tool could be used and ignored, or used and slower.\n- **Before/after claims credit for everything that changed.** A new intake form, a seasonal dip in claim\n  complexity or a staffing change all move the number. Early adopters also tend to be the fast ones, so\n  adopter-versus-non-adopter comparisons measure who chose the tool, not what it did.\n- **Self-reported savings are optimistic.** Hours \"saved\" that are not redeployed to backlog, volume or\n  reduced overtime never reach a P&L.\n- **The forcing problem is attribution under business conditions.** You rarely get a clean lab experiment\n  but still owe the sponsor a defensible number, a range around it, and an explanation of the gap. That\n  explanation decides the next move: more training, product fixes, or re-scoping.","how":"### 1. Write the value equation before go-live\nMake the ROI model's chain explicit so each link can be measured separately:\n\n```\nrealized value ≈ eligible volume × adoption share × effect per assisted unit × conversion − run cost\n```\n\n| Factor | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| *Adoption share* | Fraction of eligible work actually done with AI assistance, not the fraction of users who logged in |\n| *Effect per assisted unit* | Change in the outcome metric (minutes, error rate, revenue) on that work |\n| *Conversion* | Whether the gain becomes money or capacity: freed hours absorbed into backlog, avoided hires, reduced overtime |\n\n- **Decompose *because* a total shortfall is useless on its own.** You need to know which factor fell\n  short to know what to fix.\n\n### Reading the equation for an agent\n- **Adoption share** is the share of eligible tasks routed to the agent (its attempt share), not the share\n  of users who opened the agent UI.\n- **Effect per assisted unit** is net per attempted run: completion rate × time saved on completed tasks,\n  minus takeover rate × the time humans spend reviewing and redoing taken-over runs. A takeover is not\n  assisted work; it is often extra work.\n- **Run cost** scales with steps and tool calls per task, so a looping agent erodes value even when its\n  completion rate holds.\n\n### 2. Define adoption as a ladder, not a number\n- **Breadth:** share of eligible users active weekly.\n- **Depth:** share of their eligible work items AI-assisted.\n- **Durability:** cohort retention past the novelty period.\n- **Targets by date,** for example 30/60/90 days.\n- **Adoption is the lever the delivery team controls most directly,** through training, workflow\n  integration and change management.\n\n### 3. Choose the strongest attribution design the situation allows\nFrom strongest to weakest:\n\n| Design | How | Caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Randomised holdout** | A random subset of users or cases keeps the old process | Clean but politically hard when the tool is popular |\n| **Staggered rollout with difference-in-differences** | Groups go live at different dates; compare each group's change with the change in groups not yet live | Removes shared trends such as seasonality or a process change, provided groups would have moved in parallel without the tool |\n| **Matched comparison** | Compare against similar teams that did not get the tool | Selection bias remains |\n| **Before/after against a baseline** | Compare with the pre-launch period | Valid only if nothing else changed; always report as the weakest evidence |\n| **Self-report surveys** | Ask users | Useful for satisfaction and for *why*, not for the size of the effect |\n\n- **Check that pre-period trends really were parallel** before trusting a DiD number.\n- **With many groups going live at different dates,** compare each group only against groups not yet\n  live. A single pooled regression that also uses already-live groups as controls can be biased when the\n  effect grows over time.\n- **Use intention-to-treat** (all work in the live group, used or not) for the deployment's real effect.\n  Dividing it by the adoption share gives a rough per-assisted-unit effect, under the assumption that\n  non-assisted work did not change.\n\n### 4. Put a range on the estimate honestly\n- **The tempting error bar is wrong.** Computed from thousands of claims, it comes out tiny, because it\n  ignores that treatment was assigned to *groups* (regions, teams), not individual claims, and groups\n  drift apart for reasons unrelated to the tool. With two regions you have, in effect, one comparison.\n- **Placebo tests:** run the same DiD calculation on pre-launch weeks, pretending a go-live happened when\n  it did not. The spread of those fake \"effects\" shows how much groups wander apart on their own; a real\n  effect has to stand clearly outside it.\n- **Unit-level variation:** when the rollout is staggered over many teams, compute the estimate per team\n  (or bootstrap by resampling teams, not claims) and report the spread across teams.\n- **Watch the effect over time.** Week-6 numbers can include novelty and extra attention from the launch\n  team, so re-check the per-assisted-unit effect at the 90-day point before it goes into a renewal.\n\n### 5. Pair every value metric with guardrails\n- **Faster but worse is not value.** Speed gains that raise error, rework, complaint or\n  compliance-exception rates do not count.\n- **Agree thresholds in advance,** so \"faster but worse\" is caught by the definition and not argued about\n  afterwards.\n\n### 6. Run a value-realization cadence\n1. **Report projected versus realized value by factor** at each checkpoint.\n2. **Give ranges, not point estimates,** and state the attribution method, the assumptions and the\n   guardrail status.\n3. **Assign each shortfall to an owner:** adoption gaps to enablement, effect gaps to engineering,\n   conversion gaps to the customer's operations leadership.","example":"### Agent scenario: net value of a ticket-resolution agent\nA support agent attempts half of 10,000 eligible tickets a week. The per-ticket effects (10 minutes saved\nwhen it completes, 3 minutes lost when a human takes over) must come from a comparison design like the\none below, not from the agent's own traces. All numbers are illustrative.\n\n```python\ntickets = 10_000                       # eligible tickets a week\nattempt_share = 0.50                   # share of tickets routed to the agent\nsaved_min, takeover_penalty_min = 10, 3\nrun_cost_usd, hourly_usd = 0.40, 40    # model + tool cost per run, loaded human cost per hour\n\ndef weekly_value(completion):\n    runs = tickets * attempt_share\n    resolved = runs * completion\n    taken_over = runs - resolved\n    net_min = resolved * saved_min - taken_over * takeover_penalty_min\n    return runs, net_min / 60, net_min / 60 * hourly_usd - runs * run_cost_usd\n\nfor completion in (0.5, 0.7, 0.9):\n    runs, hours, value = weekly_value(completion)\n    print(f\"completion={completion:.0%} runs={runs:.0f} net_hours={hours:.0f} value=${value:,.0f}/week\")\n# completion=50% runs=5000 net_hours=292 value=$9,667/week\n# completion=70% runs=5000 net_hours=508 value=$18,333/week\n# completion=90% runs=5000 net_hours=725 value=$27,000/week\n```\n\n- **Completion rate is the steepest lever:** moving from 50% to 70% nearly doubles net value, because each\n  takeover both loses the saving and adds review time.\n- **Run cost is small here** ($2,000 a week) but grows with steps per task, so track it per completed task.\n\n### Worked scenario: claims-intake assistant, staggered by region\nA claims-intake assistant for 200 eligible adjusters, each handling about 45 claims a week (9,000 claims a\nweek in total). All numbers are illustrative.\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Baseline, four weeks before launch | 40 minutes of handling time per claim |\n| Projection | a 25% cut (10 minutes) on assisted claims at 90% adoption: 10 × 0.9 × 9,000 = 81,000 minutes ≈ **1,350 hours a week** |\n| Rollout | region A live in week 0, region B in week 6, a natural comparison |\n| Region A, all claims, weeks 0–6 | 40 → 32 minutes (−8) |\n| Region B, not yet live | 41 → 38 minutes (−3); a simplified intake form shipped company-wide in week 2 |\n| Difference-in-differences | −8 − (−3) = **−5 minutes per claim** attributable to the assistant (pre-launch trends were flat and parallel) |\n| Placebo check | pretend go-lives in pre-launch weeks gave \"effects\" between −1 and +1 minute, so report about −4 to −6 minutes, not −5.00 ± 0.1 from the claim-level standard error |\n| Naive before/after, adopters only | −9 minutes: it included the form change and the fact that fast adjusters adopted first |\n\nDecomposition at week 6 in region A:\n- **Adoption share:** 65% of adjusters weekly active, using it on 70% of their claims, so about\n  0.65 × 0.70 ≈ 0.46 of claims (assuming similar volumes per adjuster).\n- **Effect per assisted claim:** 5 ÷ 0.46 ≈ **11 minutes** (about 9–13 across the range), in line with the\n  projected 10.\n- **Realized value at full rollout,** if region B reaches region A's adoption: 5 × 9,000 = 45,000 minutes\n  ≈ **750 hours a week** (600–900 across the placebo range), about 56% of projection (roughly 45–65%).\n- **The gap is adoption** (0.46 against a planned 0.9), not model quality. The next investment is\n  enablement: the 35% of adjusters who do not use it, and the 30% of claims active users still handle\n  unassisted.\n- **Guardrail:** claim reopen rate moved from 4.1% to 4.3%, inside the agreed ±0.5-point band.\n- **Conversion, checked separately:** the customer's backlog fell, so freed hours were absorbed rather than\n  lost to slack.\n\n### Value-equation calculator in Python\nThe same scenario as code, so the decomposition and range are reproducible at every checkpoint.\n\n```python\nclaims_per_week = 9_000                 # 200 adjusters x 45 claims\nprojected_h = 10 * 0.9 * claims_per_week / 60          # minutes -> hours\n\ndef did(treat_pre, treat_post, ctrl_pre, ctrl_post):\n    return (treat_post - treat_pre) - (ctrl_post - ctrl_pre)\n\neffect = did(40, 32, 41, 38)            # minutes per claim, intention-to-treat\nplacebo_spread = 1.0                    # fake \"effects\" ranged -1 to +1 minute\nlow, high = abs(effect) - placebo_spread, abs(effect) + placebo_spread\n\nadoption_share = 0.65 * 0.70            # breadth x depth\nfor name, e in ((\"low\", low), (\"mid\", abs(effect)), (\"high\", high)):\n    per_assisted = e / adoption_share\n    realized_h = e * claims_per_week / 60\n    print(f\"{name}: {per_assisted:.0f} min/assisted claim, \"\n          f\"{realized_h:.0f} h/week, {realized_h / projected_h:.0%} of projection\")\nprint(f\"projected {projected_h:.0f} h/week, DiD {effect} min/claim\")\n# low: 9 min/assisted claim, 600 h/week, 44% of projection\n# mid: 11 min/assisted claim, 750 h/week, 56% of projection\n# high: 13 min/assisted claim, 900 h/week, 67% of projection\n# projected 1350 h/week, DiD -5 min/claim\n```","patterns":["**Signed measurement plan before go-live** — during scoping, write the baseline window, the value equation (including agent run cost and takeover cost), the attribution design and the guardrail thresholds into one page the sponsor and finance agree. Monitor whether the baseline metric definition changes in the customer's systems mid-project.","**Rollout calendar as experiment design** — when a holdout is politically impossible, schedule regions or teams to go live weeks apart so not-yet-live groups are controls. Monitor a pre-period trend chart per group to confirm parallel trends before reading any DiD result.","**Placebo band on every estimate** — rerun the DiD on pre-launch weeks with fake go-live dates and report the realized estimate with that spread. Monitor the band width; if it is as large as the effect, say so.","**Factor table at 30/60/90 reviews** — show projected versus realized for adoption share, effect per assisted unit and conversion, each with a named owner and a date. Monitor adoption weekly and the effect monthly once volume accumulates.","**Guardrail band written into the definition** — for example reopen rate within ±0.5 points of baseline; a breach voids the value claim for that period. Monitor guardrail metrics on the same cadence as value.","**90-day re-check before renewal** — recompute the per-assisted-unit effect after the launch team's attention fades, because week-6 numbers can include novelty. For agents, also recompute run cost per completed task and takeover rate, since model or tool changes since launch can move both."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agents are sold on projections like \"the agent resolves 40% of tickets\n  end to end\". Traces can show the agent finished runs; only an attributed outcome comparison shows that\n  handling time, backlog or cost actually moved.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Run outcomes and takeovers from usage analytics feed the adoption\n  share; the customer's ticketing or claims system supplies the outcome; your provider bills and tool costs\n  supply the run-cost term.\n- **What you control.** The rollout calendar (staggered go-lives make attribution possible), the value\n  equation, guardrails such as reopen rate on agent-completed work, and how run cost and takeover cost are\n  counted.\n- **What you can skip.** Econometrics beyond difference-in-differences, placebo weeks and team-level\n  resampling; those three cover most agent deployments.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Renewing an agent deployment.** A factor table (adoption share, net effect, conversion, run cost)\n  with placebo ranges answers \"did the agent pay for itself?\" in terms finance accepts.\n- **Choosing the next investment.** When the gap is attempt share or takeovers, the fix is routing,\n  enablement or agent quality; when it is conversion, it belongs to the customer's operations.\n- **Deciding on more autonomy.** Guardrails on agent-completed work (reopen rate, error rate) decide\n  whether approval gates can be relaxed.\n- **Rolling an agent out across teams.** A staggered go-live calendar turns the rollout itself into the\n  comparison group.","mini_project":"**Build a value report for a simulated agent rollout (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** produce a defensible, ranged estimate of an agent's weekly net value.\n- **Steps:** simulate weekly handling times for two teams over 10 weeks, with the agent going live in\n  team A at week 5 and a shared drift in both; write a projected value up front; add agent run logs\n  (completed, taken over, tool calls, cost) and a weekly reopen rate as the guardrail; compute DiD, a\n  placebo spread from pre-launch weeks, adoption share and net value with the run-cost and takeover terms.\n- **Done when:** a one-page table shows projected versus realized value by factor with a range, the\n  guardrail status, and the single factor you would invest in next."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    P[\"Projected value from agent ROI model\"] --> R[\"Realized value net of run cost\"]\n    R --> G{\"Guardrails held?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| X[\"Not value: fix agent quality first\"]\n    G -->|\"yes\"| F{\"Which factor fell short?\"}\n    F -->|\"adoption share\"| EN[\"Enablement and workflow fit\"]\n    F -->|\"effect per assisted unit\"| ENG[\"Engineering: agent quality, tools, takeovers\"]\n    F -->|\"conversion\"| OPS[\"Customer operations leadership\"]\n    A[\"Attribution: holdout, staggered DiD\"] --> R\n    PL[\"Placebo weeks\"] -.->|\"realistic range\"| R","caption":"Each factor of the value equation is measured separately, so a shortfall routes to the team that can\nfix it; guardrails decide whether a gain counts at all."},"interview":[{"q":"Why keep adoption and value realization as separate measurements?","a":"- **Adoption is a leading indicator** of whether the intended people use the system for the intended work;\n  the delivery team can act on it weekly through training and workflow integration.\n- **Value is a lagging, attributed outcome** that needs a baseline or comparison group and weeks of volume.\n- **Merging them hides the diagnosis:** high usage can coexist with no outcome change, and a shortfall\n  cannot be routed to an owner unless the factors are measured separately."},{"q":"The sponsor refuses a holdout for a claims agent rolling out across four regions. How do you design the measurement?","a":"- **Stagger the go-live dates** so regions not yet live act as controls, and compute difference-in-\n  differences against only not-yet-live groups.\n- **Check parallel pre-period trends** per region before go-live, and run placebo DiDs on pre-launch weeks\n  to size the natural drift.\n- **Report intention-to-treat,** then divide by adoption share for a rough per-assisted-unit effect, with\n  guardrails agreed up front."},{"q":"When is a plain before/after comparison acceptable, and what does it cost you?","a":"- **Acceptable** for a small internal tool where a lighter, proportionate version is enough, and only when\n  you can show nothing else changed in the period.\n- **The cost:** any concurrent change (a new form, seasonality, staffing) is credited to the AI, so it is\n  always reported as the weakest evidence.\n- **Flip point:** once the number feeds a renewal or a budget decision, invest in a comparison group."},{"q":"A DiD over 9,000 claims a week gives minus 5 minutes with a standard error of 0.1. What do you report?","a":"- **Not ± 0.1,** because treatment was assigned by region, so with two regions you effectively have one\n  comparison; claim-level errors ignore group drift.\n- **Run placebo tests** on pre-launch weeks; if fake effects span −1 to +1 minute, report about −4 to −6.\n- **Translate the range into hours** (600–900 a week here) and lead with the conservative end for a renewal."},{"q":"How do you show that saved time actually reached the P&L?","a":"- **Measure conversion as its own factor:** backlog, volume handled per head, overtime or avoided hires,\n  owned by the customer's operations leadership.\n- **Self-reported hours are not enough,** because unredeployed time never reaches a P&L.\n- **In the claims example,** a falling backlog showed the freed hours were absorbed rather than lost to slack."},{"q":"Your agent completes 70% of the tickets it attempts. Why might its realized value still be disappointing?","a":"- **Takeovers cost time:** the 30% taken over can each add review and redo minutes, so net effect per task\n  is below the gross saving.\n- **Run cost and attempt share:** cost per run grows with steps and tool calls, and if the agent only\n  attempts half the eligible tickets, the adoption share caps the total.\n- **Attribution:** without a staggered rollout or holdout, the per-ticket saving itself is unproven, so\n  measure it with DiD before tuning prompts."}],"tradeoffs":["**Randomised holdout vs goodwill.** It gives the most credible effect estimate but withholds a useful tool from some users; when politics forbid it, a staggered rollout is the usual compromise.","**Intention-to-treat vs per-use effect.** ITT is honest about real deployment impact but understates per-use effect; divide by adoption share only when non-assisted work plausibly did not change.","**Ranges vs one number.** Reporting ranges and assumptions protects credibility with finance, but executives may prefer one number; give the conservative end as the headline when the decision is a renewal.","**Business outcomes vs speed of signal.** Outcomes take weeks to accumulate, so you also track leading indicators (adoption, acceptance) that move faster but prove nothing about value on their own.","**More agent autonomy vs measurable effect.** Letting the agent act without approval raises the saving per task, but makes errors land in outcomes before anyone sees them; keep approval gates until guardrails have held across a full checkpoint."],"pitfalls":["**Reporting usage as value.** The dashboard shows high weekly actives, but finance finds no change in cost per case and stops trusting the AI team's numbers.","**Before/after without a comparison group.** A large effect appears that coincides with a seasonal dip or a process change, and it shrinks or reverses the following quarter.","**Comparing adopters with non-adopters.** The effect looks huge because self-selected adopters were already the strongest performers, which shows up as a gap that predates go-live.","**Counting hours saved that are never redeployed.** Projected savings appear in slides, but headcount, overtime and backlog are unchanged, so the budget owner sees nothing.","**Claim-level error bars on a region-level rollout.** The reported range is implausibly tight, and the next region's result lands far outside it.","**Skipping guardrails.** Handling time improves while rework or complaints rise, discovered later through escalations instead of the measurement plan.","**Counting agent-completed runs as value.** The slide shows thousands of tasks \"done by the agent\", but takeovers, human review time and run cost were never subtracted, so finance sees a smaller or negative number. Fix: use the net effect and the run-cost term."],"when":"- **Set it up during scoping,** when you agree the baseline and the ROI model, and start reporting at the\n  first 30-day checkpoint.\n- **Mandatory for:** anything sold on a business case, renewals and expansions, and internal platform\n  teams defending budget.\n- **Lighter version:** for small internal productivity tools, an adoption ladder plus a baseline comparison\n  may be proportionate.\n- **Measure:** adoption weekly; value monthly or per checkpoint, once enough volume has accumulated.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"how would you prove this worked?\". The strong answer names the comparison group,\n  decomposes the gap, and names the guardrails.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the pipelines that join usage events to the customer's outcome data, and computes intention-to-treat and per-assisted-unit effects reproducibly.","architect":"Designs the rollout so attribution is possible (staggered regions, holdout cohorts) and owns the value equation that links system metrics to the business case.","fde":"Runs the 30/60/90 value reviews with the sponsor, explains shortfalls by factor, and turns the biggest gap into an action plan with named owners."},"check":{"q":"Ninety days in, realized value is 40% of the ROI projection. The sponsor asks whether the model is\nunderperforming. How do you answer?","a":"I decompose the shortfall instead of guessing. Using the value equation, I check adoption share\n(breadth × depth) against the plan, the effect per assisted unit from the comparison design, and whether\nfreed capacity was converted. If adoption is at 45% against a 90% plan while the per-assisted-unit effect\nmatches projection, the model is fine and the gap is enablement or workflow fit. If adoption is on plan\nbut the effect is small, it is a quality or task-fit problem for engineering. I state the attribution\nmethod, give a range and confirm the guardrails held. Then I propose the specific next lever with an owner and date."},"quiz":[{"q":"A staggered rollout shows the live region's handling time fell 8 minutes and the not-yet-live region's fell 3 minutes over the same weeks. What is the difference-in-differences estimate, and what does it assume?","options":["8 minutes, assuming the not-yet-live region is irrelevant","5 minutes, assuming both regions would have followed parallel trends without the tool","11 minutes, assuming the two changes add up","5 minutes, assuming adoption in the live region was 100%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DiD subtracts the shared trend (−3) from the treated change (−8), giving −5. Its key assumption is parallel trends. Adoption below 100% does not invalidate it; it makes the result an intention-to-treat estimate."},{"q":"Why is comparing outcomes of adopters versus non-adopters within the same team a weak attribution design?","options":["Because the two groups use different model versions","Because adopters generate more analytics events","Because users choose to adopt, so differences may reflect who adopted rather than what the tool did","Because non-adopters cannot have baseline measurements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Self-selection is the problem. Early adopters are often already faster or more engaged, so their advantage predates the tool. Randomisation or staggered rollout breaks that link."},{"q":"Realized value is half the projection, adoption share is half the planned level, and the per-assisted-unit effect matches the projection. What should the next investment target?","options":["Fine-tuning the model to improve output quality","Enablement and workflow integration to raise adoption among non-users","Revising the baseline measurement upward","Adding guardrail metrics to the dashboard"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The decomposition puts the entire gap in adoption, because each assisted unit delivers as projected. Improving the model would raise an effect that is already on target and leave the real shortfall untouched."}]},{"key":"adoption/continuous-improvement","sources":[],"why":"- **Quality does not stay where acceptance left it.** New users phrase things differently, the knowledge\n  base changes, the vendor updates the model behind an alias, and the business adds products the prompts\n  never mention. Quality drifts, and users quietly route around a tool they stop trusting.\n- **Reactive patching breaks things silently.** A complaint arrives, someone edits the prompt until that\n  example works, and it ships. LLM behaviour is coupled: an instruction added to fix French users'\n  replies can change tone or format for everyone else, and without a regression suite nobody notices\n  until the next complaint.\n- **Nothing measures frequency.** The loudest stakeholder's anecdote gets fixed before the failure that\n  hits two hundred users a week.\n- **Nobody can say whether it is getting better.** The team is busy but the acceptance rate is flat.\n- **The loop makes every stage measurable:** an input (classified failures), a gate (no regression on a\n  frozen suite) and an output (the targeted bucket shrinks in production). It is plan-do-check-act, with\n  test-driven bug fixing as the \"check\" step, adapted to probabilistic outputs and shifting dependencies.","how":"### 1. Observe\nPull signals from several sources, because each sees different failures:\n- **Disposition events** from usage analytics (regenerations, heavy edits, abandons) and explicit feedback.\n- **Escalations** to humans and **incidents**.\n- **Trace-level errors:** tool failures, timeouts, empty retrievals.\n- **Periodic human review of a random sample.** It matters *because* every other source is biased toward\n  failures that users notice and bother to report.\n\n### 2. Classify\n- **Build a stable taxonomy through error analysis:** read real traces and name the failure modes (\"cited\n  superseded policy\", \"wrong language\", \"tool called with wrong account ID\").\n- **For agents, classify the trajectory, not just the final answer:** wrong tool chosen, right tool with\n  bad arguments, a loop that hits the step budget, a required lookup skipped, or a stop before the task\n  was done. A correct final answer reached through a risky action is still a failure.\n- **Scale with an LLM classifier** once the categories are stable, and spot-check it.\n- **Counts per bucket per week** are the input to everything that follows.\n\n### 3. Prioritise\n- **Score:** weekly count × severity ÷ effort.\n- **Hard override:** anything harmful, non-compliant or money-losing is fixed first regardless of score,\n  *because* a pure frequency score will always rank a frequent cosmetic issue above a rare one that ends\n  the contract.\n\n### 4. Fix at the cheapest layer that works\nDiagnose which layer owns the failure before touching anything. In rough order of cost:\n\n| Order | Layer | Typical failure it owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | UX or training | The user asked for something out of scope |\n| 2 | Prompt or instructions | Wrong format, tone, language |\n| 3 | Retrieval and data | Wrong, stale or missing documents |\n| 4 | Tools and integrations | Wrong arguments, failing calls |\n| 5 | Guardrails | Policy-violating output |\n| 6 | Model choice | Capability gap |\n| 7 | Fine-tuning | Persistent behaviour the above cannot fix |\n\n- **Many \"model failures\" are retrieval or data failures,** and a prompt cannot fix a document that is not\n  in the index.\n- **Agents add a layer: the loop itself.** Loops and premature stops are often fixed in orchestration\n  (stopping conditions, step budgets, loop detection, a clearer tool error message the model can act on),\n  not in the system prompt. Tool descriptions and argument schemas sit in layer 4 and are a cheap,\n  frequent fix for wrong-tool and bad-argument buckets.\n\n### 5. Encode before you fix\n- **Add the failing production cases,** de-identified as needed, to the regression suite *before* changing\n  the system.\n- **Why first:** it proves the suite catches the failure, and keeps it caught after later changes.\n\n### 6. Gate, release, verify\n1. **Run the full frozen suite on the candidate.** Require improvement on the targeted slice and no\n   regression beyond noise on every other slice.\n2. **Give \"noise\" a number,** for two reasons: slices are small, so a pass rate over *n* cases has a\n   standard error of about `sqrt(p(1−p)/n)`; and LLM outputs and LLM judges vary between runs even on\n   identical inputs.\n3. **Compare paired,** because baseline and candidate run on the *same* cases. Count cases that flipped\n   pass→fail (*b*) and fail→pass (*c*). McNemar's test treats `(b − c)² / (b + c)` above about 3.84 as a\n   change unlikely to be chance at the 5% level. With only a handful of flips, use the exact binomial\n   version instead.\n4. **Handle flaky cases** by running each several times and scoring the majority, or the gate will block\n   and pass at random.\n5. **Ship behind a canary or shadow deployment,** then check online that the bucket shrank.\n6. **Use a *rate*** (failures per 1,000 conversations) compared with the bucket's own pre-release weeks,\n   not a raw count, because traffic growth makes a fixed bucket look bigger.\n7. **Version every prompt, retrieval config and model,** so a change in production metrics can be\n   attributed to a specific release.\n\n### 7. Run it on a cadence\n- **Weekly triage and a fixed release train** keep the loop moving without turning every complaint into\n  an emergency.\n- **External changes go through the same gate:** a vendor model update, a re-indexed knowledge base, or a\n  new user group.\n- **Close the loop with reporters** (\"you flagged X, fixed in release 14\"), because visible responses keep\n  feedback rates up.","example":"### Agent scenario: seeding trajectory buckets from traces\nBefore an LLM classifier is worth it, simple rules over tool-call spans seed the agent's failure buckets,\nand every failing run becomes a regression case. Run summaries are illustrative.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\n# Minimal run summaries from the tracing store: tool calls in order, plus the run outcome.\nruns = [\n    {\"id\": \"r1\", \"calls\": [(\"search_orders\", \"ok\"), (\"issue_refund\", \"ok\")], \"outcome\": \"done\"},\n    {\"id\": \"r2\", \"calls\": [(\"search_orders\", \"ok\")] * 6, \"outcome\": \"max_steps\"},\n    {\"id\": \"r3\", \"calls\": [(\"issue_refund\", \"error:bad_account_id\")], \"outcome\": \"taken_over\"},\n    {\"id\": \"r4\", \"calls\": [], \"outcome\": \"answered_without_tools\"},\n    {\"id\": \"r5\", \"calls\": [(\"search_orders\", \"ok\")] * 3, \"outcome\": \"max_steps\"},\n]\n\ndef bucket(run):\n    names = [name for name, _ in run[\"calls\"]]\n    if any(status.startswith(\"error:bad_\") for _, status in run[\"calls\"]):   # not timeouts\n        return \"tool called with bad arguments\"\n    if run[\"outcome\"] == \"max_steps\" and len(names) >= 3 and len(set(names[-3:])) == 1:\n        return \"loop: same tool repeated\"\n    if run[\"outcome\"] == \"answered_without_tools\":\n        return \"skipped required lookup\"\n    return \"ok\"\n\ncounts = Counter(bucket(r) for r in runs)\nfor name, n in counts.most_common():\n    print(f\"{name:32} {n}\")\nregression_cases = [r[\"id\"] for r in runs if bucket(r) != \"ok\"]\nprint(regression_cases)\n# loop: same tool repeated         2\n# ok                               1\n# tool called with bad arguments   1\n# skipped required lookup          1\n# ['r2', 'r3', 'r4', 'r5']\n```\n\n| Week | Loop bucket per 1,000 runs | What happened |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before fix | 18 | the order-search tool returned an empty list with no message, so the agent retried |\n| Fix (layer 4) | n/a | the tool now returns \"no orders found for this email\", and a loop detector stops after 3 identical calls |\n| Canary, 2 weeks | 6 | the bucket rate fell against its own pre-release weeks, so the fix is kept |\n\nThe rates are illustrative; the point is the unit, failures per 1,000 runs, not raw counts.\n\n### Weekly triage and release gate in Python\nBucket counts come from classified production feedback. The ranking applies the severity override, and\nthe gate compares per-slice pass rates on the same frozen regression suite.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Bucket:\n    name: str\n    weekly_count: int        # classified production failures this week\n    severity: int            # 1 cosmetic, 3 wrong answer, 5 harmful / non-compliant\n    effort_days: float\n    layer: str               # prompt | retrieval | tool | guardrail | model\n\ndef rank_key(b: Bucket):\n    # Severity-5 items jump the queue; the rest are ranked by count x severity / effort.\n    return (b.severity >= 5, b.weekly_count * b.severity / b.effort_days)\n\nbuckets = [\n    Bucket(\"wrong date format\", 300, 1, 0.5, \"prompt\"),           # score 600\n    Bucket(\"cites superseded policy\", 140, 3, 3.0, \"retrieval\"),  # score 140\n    Bucket(\"replies in English to French users\", 60, 2, 1.0, \"prompt\"),  # 120\n    Bucket(\"promises refund outside policy\", 12, 5, 2.0, \"guardrail\"),   # 30, but severity 5\n]\nfor b in sorted(buckets, key=rank_key, reverse=True):\n    print(f\"{b.name:38} layer={b.layer}\")\n\ndef release_ok(base: dict, cand: dict, target: str, tol: float = 0.02) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Pass rates per slice on the same frozen suite; tol should reflect slice sample size.\"\"\"\n    improved = cand[target] > base[target]\n    no_regression = all(cand[s] >= base[s] - tol for s in base)\n    return improved and no_regression\n\nbase = {\"refunds\": 0.81, \"billing\": 0.93, \"french\": 0.88, \"shipping\": 0.95}\ncand = {\"refunds\": 0.97, \"billing\": 0.94, \"french\": 0.84, \"shipping\": 0.95}\nprint(release_ok(base, cand, \"refunds\"))   # False: french fell 4 points, beyond tolerance\n```\n\n- **The refund guardrail goes first** despite the lowest score.\n- **The candidate fixes refunds but is blocked,** because the fix cost the French slice four points. That\n  is exactly the regression reactive patching would have shipped.\n\n### Worked scenario: is the French drop real?\nIllustrative numbers for the blocked release above.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Slice size | 250 cases, so 0.88 → 0.84 is ten net cases |\n| Paired flips | 13 pass→fail, 3 fail→pass |\n| McNemar statistic | `(13 − 3)² / 16 ≈ 6.3`, above 3.84, so block |\n| Same four points on a 25-case slice | one case: rerun or grow the slice, do not trust a fixed 2-point tolerance |\n| Root cause from a flipped case's trace | the new refund instruction (\"always state the policy in English legal terms\") overrode the language rule |\n\n### A paired gate that handles few flips\nThe fixed tolerance above is a simplification. This gate uses the flips directly and the exact binomial\ntest, which stays valid when flips are few.\n\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\ndef mcnemar(b, c):\n    \"\"\"b = pass->fail flips, c = fail->pass flips, same cases, baseline vs candidate.\"\"\"\n    n = b + c\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0.0, 1.0\n    chi2 = (b - c) ** 2 / n\n    k = min(b, c)                                   # exact two-sided binomial test\n    p_exact = min(1.0, 2 * sum(comb(n, i) for i in range(k + 1)) / 2 ** n)\n    return chi2, p_exact\n\ndef slice_gate(b, c, alpha=0.05):\n    chi2, p = mcnemar(b, c)\n    regressed = b > c and p < alpha\n    return (\"block\" if regressed else \"pass\"), round(chi2, 2), round(p, 3)\n\nprint(slice_gate(13, 3))    # ('block', 6.25, 0.021)  french slice, 250 cases\nprint(slice_gate(2, 1))     # ('pass', 0.33, 1.0)     one net case\nprint(slice_gate(4, 0))     # ('pass', 4.0, 0.125)    chi-square says block, exact test says not proven\n```","patterns":["**Paired gate on a frozen suite** — for every candidate prompt, tool-schema, retrieval or model change, run baseline and candidate agents on the same cases in CI and gate on per-slice flips (exact test when flips are few), not on the average. Score trajectories as well as final answers, for example \"called the lookup tool before refunding\". Monitor flips per slice and regression escapes per release.","**Repeat flaky cases and score the majority** — when a case passes and fails across identical runs, mark it flaky, run it an odd number of times and take the majority. Monitor the share of flaky cases; a rising share means the judge or the prompt is unstable.","**Canary with a bucket-rate check** — route a small share of traffic to the candidate, then compare the targeted bucket's failures per 1,000 conversations with its own pre-release weeks. Monitor that rate and roll back if it does not fall.","**Version stamps on every trace** — record prompt version, tool-schema version, retrieval or index version and model identifier on each agent run, so a production shift can be attributed to a release. Monitor metrics grouped by release, not by calendar week.","**Treat vendor and data changes as releases** — where the provider offers dated model snapshots, pin one instead of a floating alias, and run the full suite before moving; do the same before swapping a re-indexed knowledge base. Monitor suite results per snapshot.","**Random-sample review alongside reported failures** — review a fixed number of random conversations each week to catch failures users never report. Monitor the share of buckets discovered only by the sample."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent failures are coupled and multi-step: a prompt edit that stops one\n  loop can make the agent skip a required lookup elsewhere. Without a frozen suite and a paired gate,\n  every fix is a guess about the other trajectories.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Traces with tool-call spans feed the failure taxonomy; an eval\n  harness runs the regression suite in CI; canary or shadow routing and version stamps (prompt, tool\n  schemas, model, index) make production changes attributable.\n- **What you control.** The bucket taxonomy (wrong tool, bad arguments, loops, premature stop), the\n  layer you fix at, the gate's noise rules, and when vendor model or data changes count as releases.\n- **What you can skip.** Statistics beyond McNemar's paired test and a rate per 1,000 runs; that pair\n  covers most agent release decisions.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Hypercare for a newly launched agent.** Weekly trajectory triage turns takeovers and max-step runs\n  into ranked buckets with owners.\n- **Shipping prompt or tool-schema changes safely.** A paired gate on a frozen suite blocks the fix that\n  repairs refunds but breaks the French slice.\n- **Absorbing vendor model updates.** Pinned snapshots plus a full suite run make a model change a\n  release, not a surprise.\n- **Killing agent loops for good.** Loop and premature-stop buckets are fixed in tools and orchestration,\n  then verified as a rate per 1,000 runs.","mini_project":"**Run one full improvement cycle on a toy agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** take one failure bucket from production-style traces to a gated, verified fix.\n- **Steps:** build a small agent with two tools, one of which returns an empty result without a message;\n  generate 50+ runs and bucket them with rules like the example; add the failing runs to a regression\n  suite; fix at the cheapest layer; run baseline and candidate on the suite and apply the McNemar gate.\n- **Done when:** the suite shows the loop bucket fixed with no slice regressing beyond noise, and a\n  before/after table reports the bucket as failures per 1,000 runs."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    O[\"Observe: takeovers, agent traces, random sample\"] --> C[\"Classify trajectories into buckets\"]\n    C --> P[\"Prioritise: count x severity / effort\"]\n    P --> L[\"Pick cheapest layer that owns it\"]\n    L --> E[\"Encode cases in regression suite\"]\n    E --> F[\"Implement fix\"]\n    F --> G{\"Paired gate: target up, no slice down?\"}\n    G -->|\"no\"| F\n    G -->|\"yes\"| K[\"Canary or shadow release\"]\n    K --> V{\"Bucket rate per 1,000 fell?\"}\n    V -->|\"no\"| L\n    V -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Close loop with reporters\"]\n    R --> O","caption":"The loop has a measured input, a measured gate and a measured output; anything that fails the gate goes\nback to the fix step, not to production."},"interview":[{"q":"What does continuous improvement mean for an LLM system, and why is reactive prompt patching not enough?","a":"- **Definition:** a loop of observe, classify, prioritise, fix at the cheapest layer, encode, gate and\n  verify online, run on a cadence.\n- **Why patching fails:** LLM behaviour is coupled, so a fix for one complaint can break other slices;\n  nothing measures frequency; and nobody can say whether quality is improving overall."},{"q":"A fix passed the gate, but two weeks later the targeted bucket is no smaller in production. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Check the measurement first:** compare the bucket's rate per 1,000 conversations with its pre-release\n  weeks, not raw counts, and confirm the classifier still labels the bucket the same way.\n- **Check the release:** confirm via version stamps on traces that the new prompt or config is actually\n  serving the affected traffic.\n- **Check coverage:** read fresh failures in the bucket; if they differ from the encoded cases, the suite\n  captured a sub-pattern and the fix belongs at another layer, often retrieval."},{"q":"When would you move a fix out of the prompt, and what does that cost?","a":"- **Move it when** the same bucket returns after several prompt fixes, or when the root cause is data, a\n  missing document or a tool, because a prompt cannot fix a document that is not in the index.\n- **The cost:** retrieval, tool or fine-tuning changes take longer and need wider regression runs.\n- **Customer-specific failures** go into that customer's configuration and suite rather than shared prompts."},{"q":"Your French slice has 40 cases and dropped five points. Do you block the release?","a":"- **Five points on 40 cases is two cases.** The standard error at a pass rate near 0.88 is about\n  sqrt(0.88 × 0.12 / 40) ≈ 0.05, so the drop is about one standard error.\n- **Use the paired flips:** with a handful of flips, the exact binomial test rarely shows significance, so\n  rerun flaky cases, grow the slice and read the flipped traces.\n- **Block anyway if** a flipped case is a severity-5 failure, because the severity override applies to\n  gates too."},{"q":"The vendor updated the model behind the alias your agent calls. What should your process do?","a":"- **Treat it as a release:** run the full frozen suite and compare per slice against the previous model,\n  including trajectory checks, because tool-choice and stopping behaviour can shift even when answers look\n  similar.\n- **Prefer pinned snapshots** where the provider offers them, so the update happens when you choose.\n- **Watch online bucket rates** after the switch, because vendor changes are one of the main sources of\n  silent drift."},{"q":"After a tool-schema change, your agent hits the step budget far more often. Walk through the loop.","a":"- **Classify first:** pull max-step runs by version stamp and read trajectories; repeated identical calls\n  point to a loop, alternating tools to a planning failure.\n- **Fix at the cheapest layer:** often the tool description or its empty and error results, then a loop\n  detector in orchestration; encode the failing runs as regression cases before changing anything.\n- **Gate and verify:** paired gate on the suite, canary, then confirm the loop bucket per 1,000 runs fell."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast release train vs attribution.** It shortens time-to-fix but makes attribution harder; batch fewer changes per release when you need to know which change moved the metric.","**Prompt-layer fixes vs brittleness.** They are quick and cheap but accumulate brittle instructions; once the same bucket returns after several prompt fixes, move the fix to retrieval, tools or fine-tuning.","**Growing suite vs cost.** It catches more but gets slower and costlier with an LLM judge; prune duplicate and obsolete cases, and tier the suite into fast and full runs.","**Shared fix vs isolation.** Fixing one customer's failure in shared prompts helps everyone but risks others' behaviour; when the failure is truly customer-specific, put the fix in that customer's configuration and suite.","**Final-answer scoring vs trajectory scoring.** Checking only the answer is cheap and stable, but misses risky or wasteful paths; trajectory checks catch them at the cost of brittle expectations, so assert on required and forbidden tool calls rather than an exact sequence."],"pitfalls":["**Whack-a-mole prompt edits without a suite.** Symptom: complaints you already fixed reappear a few releases later. Fix: encode before you fix.","**Prioritising by who complained loudest.** The dashboard shows a large, steady bucket untouched for weeks while small anecdotes get fixed.","**Overfitting to the regression suite.** Offline pass rates climb every release while production acceptance and edit rates stay flat.","**Tracking bucket counts instead of rates.** A fixed failure appears to \"come back\" the month traffic doubles, while its rate per 1,000 conversations is still down.","**A gate that ignores run-to-run variance.** The same candidate passes Monday and fails Tuesday, and the team learns to rerun until green.","**Unversioned changes.** Production quality shifts and nobody can tell whether the prompt edit, the re-index or the vendor's model update caused it.","**Feedback that disappears into a backlog.** Feedback volume drops because users learn that reporting changes nothing.","**Fixing agent loops in the system prompt.** \"Do not call the same tool repeatedly\" is added, loops drop for a week, then return in another tool. Fix: repair the tool's empty or error result and add a loop detector or step budget in the orchestration layer."],"when":"- **Use when** an AI system is in production with real users, starting in hypercare and continuing for\n  the system's life.\n- **Slow the cadence, never stop it,** as failure buckets shrink, because inputs and dependencies keep\n  changing.\n- **Avoid when** prototyping early: the design is still moving and a frozen suite would freeze the wrong\n  behaviour.\n- **Measure the loop itself:** weekly failure rate by bucket, time from report to fix, regression escapes\n  per release, and production acceptance trend.\n- **Interviews:** describe the loop end to end and stress the gate and the online check.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the classifier and the gate, adds failing cases to the suite before each fix, and versions prompts and configs so production changes are attributable.","architect":"Owns the release process: eval gates, canary or shadow stages, which changes need full suites, and how customer-specific fixes are isolated from shared behaviour.","fde":"Runs the weekly triage with the customer, translates their complaints into failure buckets, and reports back which fixes shipped and what moved."},"check":{"q":"After six weeks of prompt fixes driven by user complaints, the offline suite pass rate is up from\n82% to 94%, but production acceptance is flat. What is likely happening and what do you change?","a":"The suite and production have diverged. The suite probably holds only the complaint cases, so the\nfixes overfit to them, while the bulk of production failures were never classified. Unreported failures\ncould also be growing, from drift or new users. I would review a random production sample to rebuild the\nfailure taxonomy, add representative cases rather than only complained-about ones, and check whether\nprompt patches have accumulated into conflicting instructions. Then I would prioritise by measured bucket\nfrequency and severity. Each release would be validated by an online check that the targeted bucket\nshrank, not only by offline pass rate."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why add the failing production cases to the regression suite before implementing the fix?","options":["It makes the release pipeline run faster","It proves the suite detects the failure, and it keeps the failure detected after later changes","It is needed so the LLM judge can learn from the examples","It prevents the vendor from changing the model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Encoding first is the test-driven pattern: a test that fails before the fix and passes after shows the suite actually covers the failure. That protects against regressions from future changes."},{"q":"A bucket of \"promises refunds outside policy\" has 12 weekly cases, while \"wrong date format\" has 300. Which prioritisation is sound?","options":["Date format first, because frequency × severity ÷ effort gives it the higher score","Refund promises first, because harmful or non-compliant failures override the frequency score","Whichever is cheaper to fix, because effort dominates","Neither, until the counts are statistically significant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pure scores rank frequent cosmetic issues above rare costly ones. A severity override puts harmful, non-compliant or money-losing failures first, then the score orders the rest."},{"q":"A candidate release raises the targeted slice from 81% to 97% but drops another slice by 4 points, beyond the agreed tolerance. What should happen?","options":["Ship it, because the average pass rate across slices went up","Ship it and add the dropped slice to next week's triage","Block it and rework the fix, because the gate requires no regression beyond noise on any slice","Widen the tolerance so the release passes"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Averages hide regressions for specific user groups. The per-slice no-regression rule exists to catch exactly this kind of coupled side effect before it reaches production."}]},{"key":"adoption/handoff-to-customer-teams","sources":[],"why":"- **Forward-deployed work ends.** The FDE moves to the next account, and the system has to keep working\n  without them.\n- **The failure is slow decay, not a dramatic outage.** Nobody knows why retrieval filters by region, so\n  someone \"simplifies\" it. Nobody can run the eval suite, so a vendor model update ships unchecked. An alert\n  fires into a channel nobody watches. A credential issued to the FDE's personal account expires. Six\n  months later the customer concludes \"the AI didn't work\", and the renewal goes with it.\n- **A document dump does not transfer a skill.** A wiki page, an architecture diagram and a recorded\n  walkthrough in the final week fail because operating an AI system is a skill, not a body of facts.\n  Reading about triaging a quality regression does not prepare someone to do it at 2 a.m.\n- **AI systems carry more tacit knowledge than ordinary services.** Why a prompt is phrased the way it is,\n  which eval slices are noisy, which failures are known and acceptable: much of that lives only in the\n  builder's head.\n- **The forcing problem:** move that knowledge, and the matching responsibility, to people who will be\n  accountable after the builder is gone.","how":"### 1. Define what is being handed off\nSplit ownership into explicit areas, because each needs different skills and often different people.\n\n| Area | Covers |\n|---|---|\n| **Run** | On-call, alerts, incident response, capacity and cost budgets |\n| **Change** | Prompt and config edits, re-indexing, dependency and model upgrades, all through the release gate |\n| **Quality** | The regression suite, failure triage, and the continuous-improvement cadence |\n| **Access and accounts** | Service identities, secrets, data permissions, audit, and who owns and pays for the model-provider and cloud accounts |\n| **Vendor interface** | What the vendor still supports after handoff, how to escalate, and with what SLAs |\n\n- **Accounts are dependencies.** A vendor-billed API account outlives the engagement unless it is moved.\n- **Write it as a responsibility matrix (RACI) that both sides sign.** An unclear boundary turns into\n  support tickets the vendor bounces back and forth with the customer.\n\n### Agent-specific items to hand over\n- **Tool inventory:** every tool the agent can call, the service identity it uses, its scopes, expiry\n  date and owner, because an agent's blast radius is the union of its tools' permissions.\n- **Control settings:** step budgets, approval gates for consequential actions, rate and cost limits, and\n  how to switch the agent off or to read-only mode during an incident.\n- **Eval suite with trajectory checks:** required and forbidden tool calls per case, so the customer can\n  gate prompt, tool-schema and model changes.\n- **Known agent failure modes:** the current buckets (loops, bad arguments, premature stops) with their\n  rates and the fixes already tried.\n\n### 2. Check the receiving team is real\n- **Confirm named people, allocated time and the needed skills** before planning the transfer. A handoff\n  to a team with no capacity is a handoff to nobody.\n- **If skills are missing,** the honest options are training plus a longer transition, or a\n  managed-service arrangement.\n- **Plan for at least two people per ownership area.** If every exit task is done by the one engineer who\n  paired with the FDE, the single point of failure has only moved from vendor to customer, and it\n  reappears the first time that person is on leave or changes job.\n- **Keep a skills matrix:** areas down the side, named people across, a mark when someone has *done* the\n  task. It makes the gap visible early.\n\n### 3. Make the artifacts operable, not just readable\n- **Code and config:** move infrastructure-as-code, prompts and configs into the customer's repositories\n  and CI.\n- **Signals:** route dashboards and alerts to the customer's on-call tooling, and write runbooks keyed to\n  specific alerts.\n- **Evals:** make the suite runnable in the customer's pipeline.\n- **Credentials:** re-issue every one to customer-owned service identities.\n- **Decision records** for the non-obvious choices, because the \"why\" is what stops a well-meaning\n  simplification from undoing a fix.\n- **Known-issues list and open backlog,** handed over explicitly.\n\n### 4. Transfer responsibility in stages\nEach stage moves the operator role one step, *because* people build operational skill by doing the task\nwith a safety net, not by reading about it.\n1. **FDE does, customer watches.**\n2. **They work as a pair.**\n3. **Customer does, FDE watches** (reverse shadowing).\n4. **Customer works alone,** FDE as backup.\n5. **Exit.**\n\n### 5. Exit on demonstrated capability\n- **Exit criteria are tasks with evidence.** For example: the customer team shipped a prompt change\n  through the gate, ran the suite and correctly interpreted a regression, resolved a game-day incident\n  using the runbook, rotated a secret, and ran a weekly triage without help.\n- **A date can be a target, but the criteria decide.**\n- **Evidence must be checkable by someone other than the FDE** (a merged pull request, a gate report, an\n  incident ticket), because the FDE has the strongest incentive to declare victory on schedule.\n\n### 6. Taper, then check in\n- **Hypercare window** with reducing FDE involvement.\n- **30- and 90-day check-ins** on the same adoption and quality metrics, so post-handoff decay shows up as\n  a trend and not as a churn surprise.\n- **Escalations back to the vendor per month:** a falling count means ownership has moved; a flat or\n  rising one means the customer is still operating the system through the vendor's people.","example":"### Agent scenario: audit the tool inventory before exit\nA support agent calls three tools. Before exit, every tool credential must belong to a customer service\nidentity, have enough life left, carry least-privilege scopes and have a runbook. Names are illustrative.\n\n```python\nFDE = {\"fde-anna\", \"fde-raj\"}\n\n# Agent tool inventory: (tool, credential owner, scopes, days to expiry, runbook linked)\ntools = [\n    (\"search_orders\", \"svc-support-agent\", [\"orders:read\"], 180, True),\n    (\"issue_refund\",  \"fde-raj\",           [\"refunds:write\"], 12, True),\n    (\"send_email\",    \"svc-support-agent\", [\"mail:send\", \"admin\"], 365, False),\n]\n\ndef tool_gaps(tools, min_days=30):\n    for name, owner, scopes, days, runbook in tools:\n        if owner in FDE:\n            yield f\"{name}: credential owned by {owner}, re-issue to a customer service identity\"\n        if days < min_days:\n            yield f\"{name}: credential expires in {days} days\"\n        if \"admin\" in scopes:\n            yield f\"{name}: admin scope, narrow to least privilege\"\n        if not runbook:\n            yield f\"{name}: no runbook for tool failures\"\n\nfor gap in tool_gaps(tools):\n    print(\"GAP\", gap)\n# GAP issue_refund: credential owned by fde-raj, re-issue to a customer service identity\n# GAP issue_refund: credential expires in 12 days\n# GAP send_email: admin scope, narrow to least privilege\n# GAP send_email: no runbook for tool failures\n```\n\n- **The refund tool is the outage waiting to happen:** it runs on a departing FDE's personal credential\n  that expires in 12 days, likely inside the hypercare window. Re-issuing it to a customer service\n  identity fixes both gaps at once.\n\n### Worked scenario: six-week handoff of a contract-review assistant\nA two-person FDE team hands the assistant to the customer's platform team (three engineers and one\noperations analyst) over six weeks. The timeline is illustrative.\n\n| Weeks | Stage | What happened |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1–2 | FDE does, customer watches | RACI agreed. Repos, Terraform and the prompt registry move into the customer's GitHub organisation. The model-API key and document-store credentials are re-issued to customer service accounts and personal keys revoked. Alerts route to the customer's PagerDuty rota with the FDE as secondary. |\n| 3–4 | Pair, then reverse shadow | A customer engineer adds the new indemnity clause type to the extraction prompt: twelve labelled contracts go into the suite first, the gate shows the \"governing law\" slice drop three points, they fix the instruction conflict and ship behind the canary. The analyst runs the weekly triage while the FDE observes. |\n| 5 | Customer alone, FDE backup | Game day: the FDE injects a failure that empties retrieval results for one business unit. The on-call engineer finds it from the alert and runbook in 22 minutes, traces it to a revoked index permission, and restores it. The runbook gets two corrections. |\n| 6 | Exit review | Each criterion is checked against evidence: change shipped through the gate, incident resolved, secret rotated, triage run unaided, decision records reviewed. |\n\n- **What the review found:** the skills matrix shows each task was done by only one engineer, and one item\n  fails outright: nobody has done a vendor model-version upgrade.\n- **Decision:** exit is moved a week. The customer runs the upgrade with the suite, and a second engineer\n  repeats the gated change and the secret rotation, before sign-off.\n- **Follow-up:** check-ins are booked for day 30 and day 90.\n\n### Readiness check from the skills matrix\nThe exit review as code: every required criterion needs evidence from at least two customer people, and\nwork the FDE did does not count. Names and ticket ids are illustrative.\n\n```python\nFDE = {\"fde-anna\", \"fde-raj\"}\n\ndone = [  # (exit criterion, area, performed by, evidence link)\n    (\"prompt change through gate\", \"change\",  \"priya\", \"PR-412 + gate report\"),\n    (\"prompt change through gate\", \"change\",  \"tom\",   \"PR-431 + gate report\"),\n    (\"game-day incident resolved\", \"run\",     \"tom\",   \"INC-88\"),\n    (\"secret rotated\",             \"access\",  \"priya\", \"CHG-19\"),\n    (\"weekly triage run unaided\",  \"quality\", \"maria\", \"triage notes wk5\"),\n    (\"model-version upgrade\",      \"change\",  \"fde-raj\", \"PR-440\"),\n]\nrequired = [\"prompt change through gate\", \"game-day incident resolved\", \"secret rotated\",\n            \"weekly triage run unaided\", \"model-version upgrade\"]\n\ndef readiness(done, required, min_people=2):\n    gaps = []\n    for crit in required:\n        people = {who for c, _, who, ev in done if c == crit and who not in FDE and ev}\n        if not people:\n            gaps.append(f\"{crit}: no customer evidence\")\n        elif len(people) < min_people:\n            gaps.append(f\"{crit}: only {sorted(people)}\")\n    return gaps\n\nfor g in readiness(done, required):\n    print(\"GAP\", g)\n# GAP game-day incident resolved: only ['tom']\n# GAP secret rotated: only ['priya']\n# GAP weekly triage run unaided: only ['maria']\n# GAP model-version upgrade: no customer evidence\n```","patterns":["**Customer-owned identities from day one** — when the build starts, create service accounts in the customer's identity provider and cloud, one per agent or per tool group with least-privilege scopes, and never use personal API keys even temporarily. Keep a credential inventory with owner and expiry date; alert well before expiry and after any personal key is found in use.","**Eval gate as a required CI check** — once prompts and configs live in the customer's repositories, make the regression suite a required status check on pull requests that touch them (branch protection in GitHub supports this). Monitor how many changes went through the gate per month after exit.","**Alert-keyed runbooks** — every alert links to its runbook section, written for the customer's on-call engineer. Monitor alerts fired without a runbook link and time to acknowledge on the customer's rota.","**Game day before exit** — inject a realistic failure (empty retrieval, revoked permission, provider outage, a tool returning errors that sends the agent into a loop) in a production-like environment with the FDE only observing. Include switching the agent to read-only or off. Measure time to resolve and fold every runbook correction back in.","**Skills matrix with two names per cell** — track who has *performed* each exit task, with links to the evidence. Monitor cells with fewer than two customer names; they are single points of failure.","**Vendor-escalation count after exit** — log every escalation back to the vendor team. Monitor the monthly count across the 30- and 90-day check-ins; it should fall."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent carries more moving parts than a chat feature: tool\n  credentials, approval gates, step budgets, a trajectory eval suite. Each one decays silently if nobody\n  on the customer side has operated it, and agents act on real systems when they decay.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Tool credentials in the customer's identity provider, the agent's\n  prompts and tool schemas in their repositories, the eval gate in their CI, and agent alerts (tool error\n  rate, step-budget hits, cost per run) in their on-call tooling.\n- **What you control.** Building on customer-owned service identities from day one, a tool inventory with\n  scopes and expiry, runbooks keyed to agent alerts, and exit criteria that include an agent game day.\n- **What you can skip.** Contract and commercial mechanics of the support boundary; leave those to the\n  account team, but make sure the RACI names who owns each tool.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **FDE exit from an agent deployment.** Staged transfer plus an agent game day proves the customer can\n  run, change and stop the agent without the builder.\n- **Internal platform team adopting an agent.** The same RACI, tool inventory and exit evidence apply when\n  a product team hands its agent to a central platform team.\n- **Credential and permission hygiene.** A tool inventory audit before exit catches personal keys, near\n  expiries and over-broad scopes.\n- **Choosing managed service instead.** When the receiving team cannot yet own tools and evals, the\n  readiness check makes that case with evidence.","mini_project":"**Write and test a handoff pack for a toy agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** make a small tool-calling agent operable by someone who did not build it.\n- **Steps:** list the agent's tools with owner, scopes and expiry; write two runbook sections keyed to\n  alerts (tool error rate, step-budget hits); add a switch that puts the agent in read-only mode; make the\n  eval suite runnable with one command; ask a colleague to resolve an injected tool outage using only the\n  pack.\n- **Done when:** the colleague restores service and flips read-only mode without help, the tool audit\n  prints no gaps, and you have folded their runbook corrections back in."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    R[\"RACI signed, receiving team confirmed\"] --> S1[\"FDE does, customer watches\"]\n    S1 --> S2[\"Pair\"]\n    S2 --> S3[\"Reverse shadow: customer does\"]\n    S3 --> S4[\"Customer alone, FDE backup\"]\n    S4 --> GD[\"Agent game day: tool outage, loop\"]\n    GD --> X{\"Every criterion evidenced by 2+ people?\"}\n    X -->|\"no\"| S4\n    X -->|\"yes\"| H[\"Hypercare taper\"]\n    H --> C[\"Day 30 and day 90 check-ins\"]\n    C -.->|\"escalations not falling\"| S4","caption":"Responsibility moves one stage at a time; the exit review checks evidence, and a failed criterion sends\nthe team back a stage instead of ending the engagement on the calendar date."},"interview":[{"q":"What exactly is transferred in a handoff, and why is documentation not enough?","a":"- **Ownership in five areas:** run, change, quality, access and accounts, and the vendor interface, written\n  as a signed RACI.\n- **Documentation transfers facts, not skill.** Operating an AI system means triaging regressions and\n  resolving incidents, which people learn by doing with a safety net.\n- **Tacit knowledge** (why a prompt is phrased a certain way, which slices are noisy) needs decision records\n  and paired work, not a final-week walkthrough."},{"q":"Design the exit criteria for handing off a RAG agent with tools.","a":"- **Tasks with checkable evidence:** a prompt change shipped through the gate, a regression correctly read,\n  a game-day incident resolved from the runbook, a secret rotated, a re-index or model upgrade run with the\n  suite, a weekly triage run unaided.\n- **Evidence a third party can verify:** merged pull requests, gate reports, incident tickets.\n- **Two people per area,** so the single point of failure does not move from vendor to customer.\n- **Agent-specific tasks:** a tool-schema change shipped through the trajectory gate, a tool credential\n  rotated, and the agent switched to read-only during a drill."},{"q":"What agent-specific risks do you check before handing an agent to a customer team?","a":"- **Tool permissions:** every tool on a customer service identity with least-privilege scopes, an owner\n  and an expiry date, because the agent can do anything its tools allow.\n- **Controls they can operate:** step budgets, approval gates, cost limits and a kill switch, each with a\n  runbook and exercised once by the customer.\n- **Quality ownership:** a trajectory eval suite that runs in their CI, and the current failure buckets\n  with rates, so their first model upgrade is gated."},{"q":"When would you not do a full handoff?","a":"- **When the receiving team has no allocated capacity or AI skills,** because a handoff to nobody decays.\n- **When the system still changes weekly,** since the vendor would be re-explaining constantly.\n- **The alternative** is a managed-service arrangement or a longer transition with training, chosen by the\n  sponsor with the risk written down."},{"q":"Three months after exit, vendor escalations are flat at around ten a month. What does that tell you and what do you do?","a":"- **Ownership has not moved:** the customer is still operating the system through the vendor's people.\n- **Classify the escalations** by ownership area to find which capability is missing, for example model\n  upgrades or quality triage.\n- **Run a targeted transfer** for that area: paired work, a drill, and evidence from two people, then\n  re-check at the next check-in."},{"q":"What usually causes an outage a few weeks after the FDE leaves?","a":"- **Credentials tied to the FDE personally:** the account is deprovisioned or a personal API key expires.\n- **Prevention:** re-issue every credential to customer-owned service identities in the first weeks and\n  revoke personal ones, with an inventory of owners and expiry dates.\n- **Detection:** alerts that route to the customer's on-call, not to a channel nobody watches."}],"tradeoffs":["**Staged, criteria-based transfer vs FDE time.** It costs weeks of FDE time but avoids decay after departure; a shorter handoff is reasonable only for simple, stable systems with experienced receiving teams.","**Full ownership vs managed service.** Full handoff frees the vendor but suits customers with real platform capability; when the customer lacks AI skills or the system still changes weekly, a managed-service arrangement is safer.","**Customer tooling vs vendor tooling.** Adopting their CI, monitoring and ticketing makes the system theirs, but costs migration effort and loses vendor-standard dashboards; the trade flips toward vendor tooling when the vendor will keep operating the system.","**Exhaustive documentation vs maintained documentation.** Exhaustive docs feel safe but go stale quickly; short runbooks keyed to alerts plus decision records for non-obvious choices are maintained more often.","**Handing over full agent autonomy vs keeping gates.** Removing approval gates at handoff cuts the customer's review load, but a new team learns the agent's failure modes slowly; keep gates on consequential tools until the customer has run a few improvement cycles."],"pitfalls":["**Credentials tied to the FDE's personal accounts.** Symptom: an outage weeks after departure, when the account is deprovisioned or a personal API key expires.","**A last-week document dump.** The receiving team cannot answer basic \"what do I do when X alerts\" questions at the exit review, or nobody holds one.","**Handing off to a team with no allocated capacity.** Alerts go unacknowledged and the triage cadence quietly stops within a month.","**Undocumented design rationale.** The customer \"simplifies\" a deliberate choice, such as a retrieval filter or prompt constraint, and an old failure returns.","**An eval suite that only runs on the FDE's laptop.** The first vendor model upgrade after handoff ships untested and quality shifts.","**Every exit task done by one customer engineer.** The system runs fine until that person leaves or is on holiday, then the next incident takes days.","**A vague support boundary.** Tickets bounce between vendor support and the customer team, and resolution times climb.","**Nobody knows how to stop the agent.** During the first post-exit incident the agent keeps taking a bad action while the team searches for the setting. Fix: a documented kill switch or read-only mode, exercised in the game day."],"when":"- **Plan it at the start of the engagement,** not the end. Who will own the system should shape early\n  choices: which repos, which CI, which monitoring stack, and whose identities.\n- **Use the full staged process** for any system with real users, consequential outputs, or ongoing change.\n- **Avoid the full process** for a throwaway POC; a short readme is enough.\n- **Measure success after handoff,** not at the exit meeting: incident time-to-resolve by the customer\n  team, changes shipped through the gate, triage continuity, and quality trends at 30 and 90 days.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is treating handoff as transfer of responsibility with exit criteria.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Moves code, prompts and infrastructure into customer-owned repos and identities, and makes the eval suite and gates runnable in the customer's CI without vendor-only tooling.","architect":"Draws the ownership boundary and support model, decides between full handoff and managed service, and keeps the design rationale in decision records.","fde":"Runs the graduated transfer, designs the game day, judges readiness against evidence, and negotiates a later exit when a criterion is not met."},"check":{"q":"The engagement contract ends in two weeks, but the customer team has not yet shipped a change\nthrough the release gate on its own. What do you do?","a":"I would not treat the contract date as proof of readiness. First, I make the gap visible to both\nsponsors: which exit criteria have evidence and which do not, and what risk each open item carries. In\nthe two weeks, I prioritise the highest-risk missing capability, usually a gated change and an incident\ndrill, by having the customer do them with me as backup. If they still are not ready, I propose\noptions: a short paid extension, a defined post-handoff support window with named escalation, or a\nmanaged-service arrangement for the parts they cannot yet own. Then the sponsor chooses with the risk\nwritten down."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why are handoff exit criteria best expressed as tasks the customer team has performed?","options":["Because contracts legally require task-based acceptance","Because operating an AI system is a skill, and only doing tasks shows the team can handle them unaided","Because tasks are easier to schedule than reviews","Because documentation cannot describe AI systems accurately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reading runbooks does not prove someone can triage a regression or resolve an incident. Demonstrated tasks with evidence do. Dates and documents are inputs to readiness, not proof of it."},{"q":"Weeks after an FDE leaves, the assistant stops working with authentication errors to the model API. What handoff step most likely was missed?","options":["Moving prompts into the customer's prompt registry","Running a game-day incident drill","Re-issuing credentials to customer-owned service identities and revoking personal ones","Writing decision records for the retrieval design"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Credentials issued to an individual's account break when that account is deprovisioned or the key expires. Re-issuing them to customer service identities is a core handoff step."},{"q":"When is a managed-service arrangement a better choice than a full handoff?","options":["When the customer lacks the skills or capacity to operate the system and it still changes frequently","When the customer has a mature platform team with on-call rotations","Whenever the system uses a third-party model API","When the documentation is complete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Full ownership needs a capable, staffed receiving team. Without one, or while the system is still changing quickly, the vendor operating it avoids handing responsibility to nobody."}]},{"key":"adoption/feature-request-triage","sources":[],"why":"- **Asks arrive constantly.** In workshops, in Slack, from the sponsor, and from end users during\n  training sessions; an FDE hears dozens a week.\n- **Saying yes in the field fails.** \"We can add that\" wins goodwill this week, but produces promises\n  product never made, custom branches that make every upgrade painful, and a roadmap set by one account.\n- **Forwarding everything unprocessed fails too.** It buries signal in noise: twenty differently worded\n  requests for the same capability look like twenty small asks instead of one large one.\n- **In AI products many asks are not features.** \"Make it more accurate on foreign-exchange questions\" is a\n  quality failure for the continuous-improvement loop. \"Let it handle bulk uploads\" may already exist, so\n  the need is training. \"Guarantee it never makes a mistake\" is a capability limit needing an expectation\n  reset and human-review design.\n- **Misrouting is costly.** Users keep suffering a quality bug while it sits in a product queue, or product\n  time goes to building what already exists. Triage lets the roadmap follow evidence instead of whoever\n  asks loudest, and stops field teams silently forking the product.","how":"### 1. Capture the problem, not the solution\nRecord each request in a standard form:\n\n| Field | Example question |\n|---|---|\n| Who asked | Role and account |\n| Job to be done | What are you trying to get done? |\n| Current workaround | What do you do today? |\n| Cost of no change | Blocks a deal, risks renewal, costs time |\n| Frequency | How often does this happen? |\n| Concrete example | One real case |\n\n- **Keep asking \"what would that let you do?\"** to get from \"add an Excel export button\" to \"finance\n  reconciles AI answers against the ledger monthly\".\n- **You need the problem *because*** clustering and solution design both depend on it; the customer's\n  proposed solution is one option among several.\n\n### 2. Classify what the ask really is\n| Type | What it is | Route |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Quality failure** | The system already tries and gets it wrong | Failure triage, a regression case, a fix through the release gate |\n| **Usage or knowledge gap** | The capability exists | Enablement; fix the docs or UI if many people miss it |\n| **Configuration** | Achievable with existing prompts, knowledge sources, settings or permissions | The field team does it |\n| **Customer-specific extension** | A real need unique to this customer, such as an integration with their ERP | Build on a supported extension point, never as a fork |\n| **Product gap** | A capability missing for a class of customers | Product backlog with evidence |\n| **Capability limit** | Not reliably achievable with current models | Reset expectations; propose human review, narrower scope or confidence thresholds |\n\nAsk the classifying questions in a fixed order, cheapest and most urgent first:\n1. **Does the system already attempt this?** Look at the traces for the requester's example. If it tries\n   and fails, it is a quality failure, which goes first *because* users are being hurt now. If it succeeds\n   when used correctly, it is a usage gap.\n2. **Can existing configuration do it?**\n3. **Can current models do it reliably?** Try it on a couple of dozen real examples before calling it a\n   product gap, because \"the model can't\" and \"nobody tried\" look the same from a ticket.\n4. **Is the need unique to this customer, or plausibly shared?**\n\n- **Split compound asks.** \"Make pricing answers right and never wrong\" is one quality failure plus one\n  capability limit, and each half has a different route.\n\n### What the types look like for an agent\n| Agent ask | Usual type |\n|---|---|\n| \"It keeps calling search and never answers\" | Quality failure: a loop bucket for the improvement loop |\n| \"Stop asking me to approve small refunds\" | Configuration, but only with a risk owner signing off on the approval threshold |\n| \"Connect it to our in-house CRM\" | Customer-specific extension: a new tool on the supported tool interface |\n| \"Let it file claims end to end with no human\" | Often a capability limit on reliability: narrower scope or human review |\n\n### 3. Deduplicate and cluster across customers\n- **Group by underlying problem, not wording.** One cluster with five accounts and concrete examples is far\n  stronger evidence than five separate tickets.\n- **A shared tagging taxonomy across all field teams** is what makes this possible.\n\n### 4. Score with explicit criteria\nA common framework is RICE, popularised by Intercom: Reach × Impact × Confidence ÷ Effort.\n\n| Input | Scale |\n|---|---|\n| **Reach** | Originally people or events per period; across customers, distinct accounts with the problem is a reasonable proxy |\n| **Impact** | Coarse: 3 massive, 2 high, 1 medium, 0.5 low, 0.25 minimal |\n| **Confidence** | A percentage that discounts guesses |\n| **Effort** | Person-weeks or person-months |\n\n- **Coarse scales are deliberate:** they stop the team arguing over decimals and make the score a ranking\n  aid, not a measurement.\n- **Add strategic fit and risk** (safety, compliance, maintenance burden).\n- **Record commercial weight (ARR, deal-blocking status) as a *separate* input,** not multiplied into the\n  score, *because* folding revenue in lets the biggest account set the roadmap without anyone deciding\n  that on purpose.\n\n### 5. Route and decide\n- **Field fixes happen now.**\n- **Product gaps go to the product owner, who decides;** the FDE supplies evidence, not commitments.\n- **Declines get a written reason** and, where possible, a workaround.\n- **Recurrence rule:** when the same customer-specific extension has been built for several customers,\n  propose productizing it.\n\n### 6. Close the loop\n- **Tell every requester what happened and why, including \"no\".**\n- **A visible outcome keeps customers reporting real problems,** instead of escalating the same ask to\n  their executive sponsor.","example":"### Agent scenario: triaging four asks about a claims agent\nOne week of asks from a claims-handling agent's users, classified with the fixed questions. Numbers are\nillustrative.\n\n| Ask | What the traces or a trial showed | Type | Route |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"It keeps searching and never answers\" | 9% of runs hit the step budget with repeated policy-search calls | Quality failure | Loop bucket, regression cases, fix in the search tool's empty result |\n| \"Stop asking me to approve small payouts\" | Approval gate is a per-tool setting; 98% of payouts under the threshold were approved unchanged last month | Configuration | Raise the threshold only after the risk owner signs off |\n| \"Connect it to our in-house fraud system\" | No such tool; no other customer uses this system | Customer-specific extension | Build a tool on the supported tool interface and add it to the extension register |\n| \"Let it settle claims end to end\" | Trial on 24 real claims: 14 settled correctly with no human | Capability limit | Narrow scope to simple claim types and keep human review for the rest |\n\n- **Only the trial makes the last row honest:** 14 of 24 is about 58%, far from \"end to end\", so the\n  answer is a scoped plan, not a roadmap promise.\n\n### Weekly triage pass: cluster, route and score\nSeven captured requests from five accounts. Requests are clustered by underlying problem and type, each\ncluster is routed, and only product gaps get a RICE score. ARR is shown next to the score but kept out of\nit. Impact, confidence and effort are the team's estimates.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\n# (account, arr_k, problem_key, kind)\nrequests = [\n    (\"Acme\", 400, \"cite-source-page\", \"product_gap\"),\n    (\"Globex\", 250, \"cite-source-page\", \"product_gap\"),\n    (\"Initech\", 120, \"cite-source-page\", \"product_gap\"),\n    (\"Acme\", 400, \"sap-form-export\", \"customer_specific\"),\n    (\"Globex\", 250, \"stale-fx-rates\", \"quality_failure\"),\n    (\"Umbrella\", 90, \"bulk-upload\", \"usage_gap\"),\n    (\"MegaCorp\", 2000, \"approval-workflow\", \"product_gap\"),\n]\nROUTE = {\n    \"quality_failure\": \"failure triage + regression case\",\n    \"usage_gap\": \"enablement; fix discoverability\",\n    \"customer_specific\": \"field build on extension point\",\n    \"product_gap\": \"product backlog with evidence\",\n}\n# Team estimates per product-gap cluster: impact (0.25-3), confidence (0-1), effort (weeks).\nESTIMATES = {\"cite-source-page\": (2, 0.8, 3), \"approval-workflow\": (2, 0.8, 3)}\n\nclusters = defaultdict(list)\nfor acct, arr, problem, kind in requests:\n    clusters[(problem, kind)].append((acct, arr))\n\nfor (problem, kind), rows in clusters.items():\n    accounts = {a for a, _ in rows}\n    arr = sum(dict(rows).values())          # ARR per distinct account\n    line = f\"{problem:17} {kind:17} accounts={len(accounts)} arr=${arr}k -> {ROUTE[kind]}\"\n    if kind == \"product_gap\":\n        impact, conf, effort = ESTIMATES[problem]\n        line += f\" | RICE={len(accounts) * impact * conf / effort:.2f}\"\n    print(line)\n```\n\nSeven tickets become five decisions:\n\n| Cluster | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| Page-level citations | One cluster spanning three accounts (RICE 3 × 2 × 0.8 ÷ 3 = 1.60, ARR $770k); goes to product with three concrete examples |\n| Approval workflow | A product gap because approval chains are common in the segment, but only one account has asked; with identical estimates it scores RICE 0.53 at ARR $2,000k |\n| Stale FX rates | Straight into the quality loop this week |\n| SAP export | Built by the field team on the integration API |\n| Bulk upload | Already exists, so a training note and a UI change review |\n\n- **Had reach been weighted by ARR, the order would flip:** 2,000 against 770.\n- **Neither ordering is automatically right;** the separate column puts that choice in front of the\n  product owner instead of letting arithmetic make it.\n\n### Classifying asks in a fixed order\nThe ordered questions from step 2 as a function, applied to asks from this lesson. The booleans are what\nthe FDE learns from traces, configuration checks and a small trial.\n\n```python\ndef classify(attempts, works_when_used_right, config_can_do, reliable_on_sample, unique):\n    \"\"\"Ask the questions in a fixed order: cheapest and most urgent first.\"\"\"\n    if attempts:                                   # traces show it already tries\n        return \"usage_gap\" if works_when_used_right else \"quality_failure\"\n    if config_can_do:\n        return \"configuration\"\n    if not reliable_on_sample:                     # tried on a couple of dozen real examples\n        return \"capability_limit\"\n    return \"customer_specific\" if unique else \"product_gap\"\n\nasks = {\n    \"more accurate on FX questions\": (True, False, False, True, False),\n    \"handle bulk uploads\":           (True, True, False, True, False),\n    \"answer from the new HR policy\": (False, True, True, True, False),\n    \"export to our SAP form\":        (False, False, False, True, True),\n    \"cite the source page\":          (False, False, False, True, False),\n    \"guarantee it is never wrong\":   (False, False, False, False, False),\n}\nfor ask, answers in asks.items():\n    print(f\"{ask:32} -> {classify(*answers)}\")\n# more accurate on FX questions    -> quality_failure\n# handle bulk uploads              -> usage_gap\n# answer from the new HR policy    -> configuration\n# export to our SAP form           -> customer_specific\n# cite the source page             -> product_gap\n# guarantee it is never wrong      -> capability_limit\n```","patterns":["**Structured intake with required fields** — when several field teams capture asks, use one form whose job, workaround, cost-of-no-change and example fields are mandatory, so tickets carry the problem rather than a proposed button. Monitor the share of requests with a concrete example attached.","**Shared tag taxonomy and a weekly clustering pass** — every field team tags by underlying problem from one list; triage merges duplicates weekly before scoring. Monitor how many tickets each cluster absorbs and how often new tags are created.","**Auto-route quality failures out of the backlog** — a request classified as a quality failure opens a failure-triage item with its example, and the agent run ID behind it, as a regression case. Monitor time from report to regression case and to a gated fix.","**ARR as its own column** — the backlog view shows RICE and commercial weight side by side, so the product owner sees when a large account would reorder the list. Monitor how often a decision overrode the RICE order, with the reason written down.","**Recurrence rule for field extensions** — keep a register of customer-specific builds by pattern, including custom agent tools; when a pattern repeats across several customers (three CRM tools that do the same lookup), raise a productization proposal. Monitor extensions per pattern and how many were later productized.","**Decision and closure log** — every request records a decision, a reason and the date the requester was told. Monitor time from request to decision and the share of requesters notified."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent asks hide their type: \"it keeps searching forever\" is a loop bug,\n  \"stop asking me to approve\" is a risk setting, \"connect it to our CRM\" is a new tool, and \"let it do\n  the whole claim alone\" is a reliability limit. Each goes to a different queue.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The first classifying step reads the agent's traces for the\n  requester's example; configuration means prompts, tool lists, approval gates and step budgets; new\n  tools land on your supported tool interface instead of a fork.\n- **What you control.** The intake fields, the fixed order of classifying questions, a small reliability\n  trial before calling anything a product gap, and keeping ARR out of the score.\n- **What you can skip.** Roadmap-planning frameworks beyond RICE; the scoring is a ranking aid, and the\n  routing is where agent teams win or lose time.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Weekly triage for a deployed agent.** Asks are checked against traces first, so loop and wrong-tool\n  complaints reach the improvement loop within days.\n- **New tool requests.** \"Connect it to our system\" becomes a tool on the supported interface, tracked in\n  the extension register until a pattern justifies productizing it.\n- **Autonomy requests.** Asks to drop approvals or raise step budgets go to a risk owner with\n  approval-gate data instead of being granted in the field.\n- **Cross-customer roadmap input.** Clusters with RICE and a separate ARR column show product which agent\n  capabilities several accounts need.","mini_project":"**Build a triage helper for agent asks (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** route a batch of agent feature requests correctly using traces and a small trial.\n- **Steps:** collect or write 15 realistic asks about a tool-calling agent; for each, record the\n  requester's example and look up (or simulate) the matching run; answer the four classifying questions\n  in order with a small function like the one above; run a 20-case trial for any \"can it do X\" ask; score\n  the product gaps with RICE and show ARR separately.\n- **Done when:** every ask has a type, a route and a written reply to the requester, and at least one ask\n  is reclassified from \"feature\" to quality failure or configuration with trace evidence."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Captured request: problem plus example\"] --> Q1{\"Does the system already attempt it?\"}\n    Q1 -->|\"yes, fails\"| QF[\"Quality failure: triage and regression case\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"yes, works\"| UG[\"Usage gap: enablement\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| Q2{\"Can configuration do it?\"}\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| CF[\"Field team configures prompts, tools, gates\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| Q3{\"Agent reliable on a real trial?\"}\n    Q3 -->|\"no\"| CL[\"Capability limit: reset, mitigate\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"yes\"| Q4{\"Unique to this customer?\"}\n    Q4 -->|\"yes\"| EX[\"Extension point build\"]\n    Q4 -->|\"no\"| PG[\"Cluster, RICE, product backlog\"]","caption":"The classifying questions run in a fixed order, so the cheapest and most urgent routes are tested before\nanything reaches the product backlog."},"interview":[{"q":"What makes feature-request triage different for an AI product?","a":"- **Many asks are not features:** they are quality failures, usage gaps or model capability limits phrased\n  as requests, and each has a different route.\n- **Why it matters:** a quality bug parked in a product queue keeps hurting users for a quarter, and\n  product time spent rebuilding an existing capability is wasted.\n- **So the first step is checking traces** for the requester's example before anything is scored."},{"q":"A user asks for \"support for scanned PDFs\". Walk me through triaging it.","a":"- **Capture the problem:** which documents, how often, what they do today, and one real file.\n- **Classify in order:** does ingestion already attempt scanned files and fail (quality failure), or work\n  when uploaded correctly (usage gap)? Can a configuration setting enable it? Does a trial on a couple of\n  dozen real scans work reliably?\n- **Then decide reach:** if other accounts have the same need, cluster and score it; if unique, build on an\n  extension point."},{"q":"A large customer says they will churn unless you build their feature. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Do not promise in the field;** capture the problem, classify it and bring evidence to the product owner.\n- **Show ARR next to the score, not inside it,** so leadership decides deliberately whether one account\n  should reorder the roadmap.\n- **Offer what you can own:** a configuration, an extension-point build, or a workaround, with a written\n  reason for anything declined."},{"q":"Two clusters: six accounts, impact 1, confidence 0.5, effort 2 weeks; versus two accounts, impact 3, confidence 0.8, effort 4 weeks. Which ranks higher?","a":"- **RICE:** 6 × 1 × 0.5 ÷ 2 = 1.5 against 2 × 3 × 0.8 ÷ 4 = 1.2, so the broad, lower-impact cluster ranks\n  higher.\n- **Read it as a ranking aid,** because the coarse scales are estimates; a small change in confidence can\n  swap the order.\n- **Then add the non-score inputs:** strategic fit, risk and commercial weight, kept visible."},{"q":"Users ask the agent to \"stop asking for approval\". How do you triage it?","a":"- **Classify with data first:** pull approval-gate decisions per tool; if almost every proposal below some\n  amount was approved unchanged, a higher threshold is a configuration change.\n- **But treat it as risk, not convenience:** a named risk owner signs off on the threshold, and the\n  change ships through the release gate with monitoring of reversals or complaints.\n- **Keep gates where rejections are frequent,** because those are the actions the agent still gets wrong."},{"q":"When is a field-built, customer-specific feature or agent tool acceptable?","a":"- **When the need is genuinely unique** and it can be built on a supported extension point, such as the\n  agent's tool interface, never as a fork, because forks tax every later upgrade.\n- **Track it in a register,** and apply the recurrence rule: once several customers need the same\n  extension, propose productizing it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Field build vs upgrade tax.** A customer-specific feature built in the field can save a deal now, but every fork taxes later upgrades; it is acceptable only on a supported extension point, with a recurrence rule for productizing it.","**ARR weighting vs fairness.** Weighting by ARR aligns with revenue but lets one large account dominate; keep it a separate, visible input so product leadership makes that trade-off deliberately.","**Capture effort vs evidence.** Detailed capture and clustering take FDE time each week, but turn anecdotes into evidence; for a single small pilot with one stakeholder, a lightweight list may be enough.","**Roadmap transparency vs implied commitments.** Sharing roadmap intent builds trust but creates implied commitments; share problem clusters and status, not delivery dates, unless product has committed.","**Saying yes to more autonomy vs risk.** Relaxing approval gates or step budgets on request removes friction users feel daily, but moves error cost onto the customer; treat it as a risk decision with an owner, backed by approval data."],"pitfalls":["**Logging the proposed solution instead of the problem.** The backlog fills with near-duplicate items, and the cluster that matters never looks big.","**Routing quality failures to the product backlog.** Users keep reporting the same wrong answers for weeks while the item waits for roadmap planning.","**Field promises the product never made.** Symptom: an escalation from the customer's executive asking where \"the committed feature\" is.","**Letting the largest customer's asks dominate.** The roadmap drifts toward one account's workflow, and other customers' adoption stalls.","**Treating capability limits as features.** Engineering chases \"never wrong\" for months instead of designing human review and scope limits.","**No closure to requesters.** The same asks reappear louder through other channels, and feedback from end users dries up.","**Granting \"more autonomy\" as a feature.** A field team removes an approval gate to please a user, and the first bad payout shows nobody owned the risk. Fix: route autonomy asks to a named risk owner with approval-rate evidence."],"when":"- **Use when** a deployed system has users who can ask for things, which is from the first pilot week\n  onward.\n- **Cadence:** weekly while volume is high, plus a cross-customer review with product monthly or per\n  planning cycle.\n- **Matters most for platforms serving several customers,** where clustering and the decline-with-reason\n  discipline protect the roadmap.\n- **Measure:** time from request to decision, the share of requests reclassified as quality or usage gaps,\n  and how many field extensions were later productized.\n- **Interviews:** the signal is separating problem from solution and routing AI-specific asks correctly.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the capture form and tagging, and turns quality-failure requests into regression cases instead of feature tickets.","architect":"Defines the supported extension points that make customer-specific asks buildable without forks, and sets the rule for when a pattern gets productized.","fde":"Interviews requesters to get to the underlying problem, classifies and clusters asks, and delivers clear decisions back to customers without making roadmap commitments."},"check":{"q":"A customer's VP says, \"We need the assistant to stop giving wrong answers about our pricing; put\nit on your roadmap for next quarter.\" How do you triage this?","a":"I would not treat it as a roadmap item. It is most likely a quality failure, so I collect concrete\nexamples and check the traces. Likely causes are retrieval of outdated pricing documents, missing\nsources, or a prompt that lets the model guess. Those go into failure triage now, with regression cases\nand a fix through the release gate, often within days. If part of the ask is \"never be wrong\", I explain\nthat as a model limit and propose mitigations: citing the pricing source, refusing when no document\nmatches, or human review for quotes. Then I tell the VP the plan and timeline."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should triage record the requester's underlying problem rather than their proposed solution?","options":["Proposed solutions are usually technically infeasible","Clustering across customers and choosing the best solution both depend on the problem, while solutions are worded differently","Product teams refuse to read solution proposals","It reduces the number of requests that must be logged"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Different customers propose different solutions to the same problem. Capturing the problem reveals the real size of the need and leaves room for a better design than any single proposal."},{"q":"A user asks for the assistant to \"be more accurate on currency conversions\", and traces show it citing last year's FX table. Where should this go?","options":["The product backlog as a new feature for the next planning cycle","Enablement, because the user should check the rates themselves","The continuous-improvement loop as a quality failure, with a regression case and a data or retrieval fix","Decline it as a model capability limit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The system already tries to answer this and fails because of stale data. That is a quality failure, fixed fastest through failure triage and the release gate, not a roadmap item."},{"q":"Why keep ARR as a separate input rather than multiplying it into the priority score?","options":["ARR data is usually confidential and cannot be stored","Revenue should never influence product decisions","Folding it in lets the largest account set the roadmap implicitly, while a separate input makes that trade-off a deliberate decision","RICE scores are only defined for non-financial inputs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Revenue matters, but hidden inside a score it silently outweighs reach and impact. Showing it separately lets product leaders decide openly when to favour a big account."}]}]});
